[[:db/cas 17592186054396 :question/problem "Calculate $(-3)^3 \\operatorname{div}  6$." "Calculate $(-3)^3 \\div 6$."] [:db/cas 17592186054398 :question/problem "What is the value of $\\left( 5 \\operatorname{div}  (-2)^2 \\right) \\times \\left( -\\dfrac{4}{5} \\right).$" "What is the value of $\\left( 5 \\div (-2)^2 \\right) \\times \\left( -\\dfrac{4}{5} \\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186054526 :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $\\textrm{i}$ to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=15.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n15\\operatorname{div}  4 = 3\\,\\textrm{R} \\color{blue}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $\\color{blue}3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^{15} = \\textrm{i}^{\\color{blue}{3}} = -\\textrm{i}.\n$$" "To find the value of $\\textrm{i}$ to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=15.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n15\\div 4 = 3\\,\\textrm{R} \\color{blue}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $\\color{blue}3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^{15} = \\textrm{i}^{\\color{blue}{3}} = -\\textrm{i}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186054527 :question/worked-solution "Using the addition law for exponents, we have\n\n$$\nab =\\textrm{i}^9\\cdot\\textrm{i}^7 = \\textrm{i}^{9+7} = \\textrm{i}^{16}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\textrm{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n,$ we divide $n$ by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=16.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n16\\operatorname{div}  4 = 4\\,\\textrm{R} \\color{blue}{0}.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $\\color{blue}0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^{16} = \\textrm{i}^{\\color{blue}{0}} =1.\n$$" "Using the addition law for exponents, we have\n\n$$\nab =\\textrm{i}^9\\cdot\\textrm{i}^7 = \\textrm{i}^{9+7} = \\textrm{i}^{16}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\textrm{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n,$ we divide $n$ by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=16.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n16\\div 4 = 4\\,\\textrm{R} \\color{blue}{0}.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $\\color{blue}0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^{16} = \\textrm{i}^{\\color{blue}{0}} =1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186055040 :question/problem "$6 \\operatorname{div}  3 + (-3)^{3} =$ {{field-1}}" "$6 \\div 3 + (-3)^{3} =$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186055042 :question/problem "$(4 \\operatorname{div}  (-2) + 5)^{4} =$ {{field-1}}" "$(4 \\div (-2) + 5)^{4} =$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186055091 :question/problem "$((-4)^{2} + 5 \\cdot 2) - ((-2)^{3} \\operatorname{div}  (-4)) =$ {{field-1}}" "$((-4)^{2} + 5 \\cdot 2) - ((-2)^{3} \\div (-4)) =$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186056032 :tutorial/content "A **variable** is any letter or symbol that can be replaced by a number. For example, the letters\n\n$$\nx, \\quad y, \\quad z, \\quad a, \\quad b, \\quad c, \\quad k, \\quad m,\\quad n,\\quad p, \\quad q,\\quad r, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nare commonly used as variables. But in general, we can use any letter we like.\n\nAn **algebraic expression** is a combination of variables with mathematical operations, like addition $(+)$, subtraction $(-)$, multiplication $(\\times)$, and division $(\\operatorname{div} ).$ So \n\n$$\nx+2,\\qquad 6-y,\\qquad 2\\times p + 1,\\qquad q\\operatorname{div}  r,\n$$\n\nare all examples of algebraic expressions." "A **variable** is any letter or symbol that can be replaced by a number. For example, the letters\n\n$$\nx, \\quad y, \\quad z, \\quad a, \\quad b, \\quad c, \\quad k, \\quad m,\\quad n,\\quad p, \\quad q,\\quad r, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nare commonly used as variables. But in general, we can use any letter we like.\n\nAn **algebraic expression** is a combination of variables with mathematical operations, like addition $(+)$, subtraction $(-)$, multiplication $(\\times)$, and division $(\\div).$ So \n\n$$\nx+2,\\qquad 6-y,\\qquad 2\\times p + 1,\\qquad q\\div r,\n$$\n\nare all examples of algebraic expressions."] [:db/cas 17592186056074 :tutorial/content "Before we continue, we need a brief discussion on the concept of **integer divisibility.**\n\nSuppose $a$ and $b$ are integers. We write $a  \\mid b$ to mean that $\\boldsymbol a$ **divides** $\\boldsymbol b.$ Informally, this means the quotient of $\\dfrac b a$ is an *integer*, and there is *no remainder*. \n\nFor example, consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n5 \\,|\\, 20\n$$\n\nThis statement reads, \"$5$ divides $20$\". Moreover, this is a *true* statement because $20\\operatorname{div}  5 = 4$ is an integer. \n\nNow consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n6\\,  |\\, 20\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false* because $20\\operatorname{div}  6 = 3 {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac13}},$ which is *not* an integer. The correct statement is\n\n$$\n6 \\! \\not | \\: 20.\n$$\n\nThis statement reads, \"$6$ does not divide $20$\".\n\nFor now, this is all we need to understand the notation $a\\mid b.$ More formal discussions on integer divisibility will be given in future lessons." "Before we continue, we need a brief discussion on the concept of **integer divisibility.**\n\nSuppose $a$ and $b$ are integers. We write $a  \\mid b$ to mean that $\\boldsymbol a$ **divides** $\\boldsymbol b.$ Informally, this means the quotient of $\\dfrac b a$ is an *integer*, and there is *no remainder*. \n\nFor example, consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n5 \\,|\\, 20\n$$\n\nThis statement reads, \"$5$ divides $20$\". Moreover, this is a *true* statement because $20\\div 5 = 4$ is an integer. \n\nNow consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n6\\,  |\\, 20\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false* because $20\\div 6 = 3 {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac13}},$ which is *not* an integer. The correct statement is\n\n$$\n6 \\! \\not | \\: 20.\n$$\n\nThis statement reads, \"$6$ does not divide $20$\".\n\nFor now, this is all we need to understand the notation $a\\mid b.$ More formal discussions on integer divisibility will be given in future lessons."] [:db/cas 17592186059439 :question/worked-solution "To subtract mixed numbers with like denominators, we subtract\n\n- whole numbers from whole numbers, and\n- fractions from fractions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's subtract our mixed numbers.\n\n- Subtracting the whole numbers, we get\n$8 - 4 = {\\color{red}{4}}$.\n- Subtracting the fractions, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{7}{9} - \\dfrac{4}{9} = \\dfrac{7 - 4}{9} = \\dfrac{3}{9}.\n$$\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{3}{9} = \\dfrac{3 \\operatorname{div}  3}{9 \\operatorname{div}  3} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{3}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the resulting whole number and fraction to create a mixed number:\n\n$$\n8 \\,\\dfrac{7}{9} - 4 \\,\\dfrac{4}{9} = {\\color{red}{4}}\\,{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{3}}}\n$$" "To subtract mixed numbers with like denominators, we subtract\n\n- whole numbers from whole numbers, and\n- fractions from fractions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's subtract our mixed numbers.\n\n- Subtracting the whole numbers, we get\n$8 - 4 = {\\color{red}{4}}$.\n- Subtracting the fractions, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{7}{9} - \\dfrac{4}{9} = \\dfrac{7 - 4}{9} = \\dfrac{3}{9}.\n$$\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{3}{9} = \\dfrac{3 \\div 3}{9 \\div 3} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{3}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the resulting whole number and fraction to create a mixed number:\n\n$$\n8 \\,\\dfrac{7}{9} - 4 \\,\\dfrac{4}{9} = {\\color{red}{4}}\\,{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{3}}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186059475 :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cot \\theta + \\csc \\theta} {\\cos \\theta + 1} = \\left(\\cot \\theta + \\csc \\theta\\right)\\operatorname{div}  \\left(\\cos \\theta + 1\\right)\n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identities\n\n$$\n\\cot \\theta = \\dfrac {\\cos \\theta} {\\sin \\theta}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nand we substitute them into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\cot θ + \\csc θ) \\operatorname{div}  (\\cos θ + 1) &= (\\frac{\\cos θ}{\\sin θ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin θ}) \\operatorname{div}  (\\cos θ + 1) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{\\cos θ + 1}{\\sin θ}\\right) \\operatorname{div}  (\\cos θ + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we turn the division into multiplication, and simplify the expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta }} ) \\operatorname{div}   ( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta }} ) \\cdot  ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta  ( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}}{\\sin   \\theta  \\cancel{( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sin   \\theta }} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\csc   \\theta \\end{array}}\n$$" "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cot \\theta + \\csc \\theta} {\\cos \\theta + 1} = \\left(\\cot \\theta + \\csc \\theta\\right)\\div \\left(\\cos \\theta + 1\\right)\n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identities\n\n$$\n\\cot \\theta = \\dfrac {\\cos \\theta} {\\sin \\theta}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nand we substitute them into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\cot θ + \\csc θ) \\div (\\cos θ + 1) &= (\\frac{\\cos θ}{\\sin θ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin θ}) \\div (\\cos θ + 1) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{\\cos θ + 1}{\\sin θ}\\right) \\div (\\cos θ + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we turn the division into multiplication, and simplify the expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta }} ) \\div  ( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta }} ) \\cdot  ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta  ( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}}{\\sin   \\theta  \\cancel{( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sin   \\theta }} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\csc   \\theta \\end{array}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186063941 :question/problem "Find the value of $-15 \\operatorname{div}  3.$" "Find the value of $-15 \\div 3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186063941 :question/worked-solution "Here, we are dividing a negative number $\\color{red}(-15)$ by a positive one $\\color{blue}(3).$ So the result will be negative:\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-15)} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}3} = {\\color{red}\\mathbf{-} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{5}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $15$ by $3$ without any signs: $15 \\operatorname{div}  3 = 5.$ Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-15)} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}3} = {\\color{red}-5}.\n$$" "Here, we are dividing a negative number $\\color{red}(-15)$ by a positive one $\\color{blue}(3).$ So the result will be negative:\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-15)} \\div {\\color{blue}3} = {\\color{red}\\mathbf{-} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{5}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $15$ by $3$ without any signs: $15 \\div 3 = 5.$ Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-15)} \\div {\\color{blue}3} = {\\color{red}-5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186063942 :question/problem "Calculate the value of $50 \\operatorname{div}  (-5).$" "Calculate the value of $50 \\div (-5).$"] [:db/cas 17592186063942 :question/worked-solution "We have to divide a positive number $\\color{blue}(50)$ by a negative one $\\color{red}(-5).$ So the result will be negative:\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{50}} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{red}{(-5)}} = {\\color{red}{\\mathbf{-} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{10}}} }}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $50$ by $5$ without any signs: $50 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 10.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{50}} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{red}{(-5)}} = {\\color{red}{-10}}.\n$$" "We have to divide a positive number $\\color{blue}(50)$ by a negative one $\\color{red}(-5).$ So the result will be negative:\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{50}} \\div {\\color{red}{(-5)}} = {\\color{red}{\\mathbf{-} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{10}}} }}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $50$ by $5$ without any signs: $50 \\div 5 = 10.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{50}} \\div {\\color{red}{(-5)}} = {\\color{red}{-10}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186063943 :question/problem "Calculate $-81\\operatorname{div}  (-9).$" "Calculate $-81\\div (-9).$"] [:db/cas 17592186063943 :question/worked-solution "We have to divide a negative number ${\\color{red}(-81)}$ by another negative number ${\\color{red}(-9)}.$ So the result will be positive:\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-81)} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{red}(-9)} = {\\color{blue} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{9}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $81$ by $9$ without any signs: $81 \\operatorname{div}  9 = 9.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-81)} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{red}(-9)} = {\\color{blue}9}.\n$$" "We have to divide a negative number ${\\color{red}(-81)}$ by another negative number ${\\color{red}(-9)}.$ So the result will be positive:\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-81)} \\div {\\color{red}(-9)} = {\\color{blue} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{9}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $81$ by $9$ without any signs: $81 \\div 9 = 9.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-81)} \\div {\\color{red}(-9)} = {\\color{blue}9}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186063949 :question/worked-solution "We can use either of the following two methods.\n\n**Method 1 - The Long Way** \n\nFirst, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6} = \\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)  \\operatorname{div}  6\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $6$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\dfrac{1}{6}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)÷6 &= \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\times \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\ &= -\\frac{3\\times 1}{2\\times 6} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n- \\dfrac{3}{12}  = - \\dfrac{3 \\operatorname{div}  3}{12 \\operatorname{div}  3}  = -\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6}= -\\dfrac14\n$$\n\n \n\n**Method 2 - The Quick Way**\n\nThe result will have the same numerator as the top fraction, and the denominator is just the product of the two denominators: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{6} &= \\frac{-3}{2\\times 6} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3}{12} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3÷3}{12÷3} \\\\ &= -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can use either of the following two methods.\n\n**Method 1 - The Long Way** \n\nFirst, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6} = \\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)  \\div 6\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $6$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\dfrac{1}{6}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)÷6 &= \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\times \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\ &= -\\frac{3\\times 1}{2\\times 6} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n- \\dfrac{3}{12}  = - \\dfrac{3 \\div 3}{12 \\div 3}  = -\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6}= -\\dfrac14\n$$\n\n \n\n**Method 2 - The Quick Way**\n\nThe result will have the same numerator as the top fraction, and the denominator is just the product of the two denominators: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{6} &= \\frac{-3}{2\\times 6} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3}{12} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3÷3}{12÷3} \\\\ &= -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064087 :question/problem "Calculate the value of $0 \\operatorname{div}  5$." "Calculate the value of $0 \\div 5$."] [:db/cas 17592186064087 :question/worked-solution "Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 0\n$$" "Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0 \\div 5 = 0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064088 :question/problem "Compute the value of $0 \\operatorname{div}  \\left(-\\dfrac 1 2\\right)$." "Compute the value of $0 \\div \\left(-\\dfrac 1 2\\right)$."] [:db/cas 17592186064088 :question/worked-solution "Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0 \\operatorname{div}  \\left(-\\dfrac 1 2\\right) = 0\n$$" "Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0 \\div \\left(-\\dfrac 1 2\\right) = 0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186064089 :question/problem "What is $-2 \\operatorname{div}  0$?" "What is $-2 \\div 0$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064089 :question/worked-solution "We cannot divide any number by zero. Therefore, $-2 \\operatorname{div}  0$ is undefined." "We cannot divide any number by zero. Therefore, $-2 \\div 0$ is undefined."] [:db/cas 17592186064489 :question/problem "Expressed as a mixed number, $(-25) \\operatorname{div}  6 =$" "Expressed as a mixed number, $(-25) \\div 6 =$"] [:db/cas 17592186064491 :question/problem "Expressed as a mixed number, $(-15) \\operatorname{div}  (-4) =$" "Expressed as a mixed number, $(-15) \\div (-4) =$"] [:db/cas 17592186064549 :question/problem "What is $(-1) \\operatorname{div}  (-1)$?" "What is $(-1) \\div (-1)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064551 :question/problem "What is $9 \\operatorname{div}  (-3)$?" "What is $9 \\div (-3)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064552 :question/problem "What is $0 \\operatorname{div}  5$?" "What is $0 \\div 5$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064553 :question/problem "What is $1 \\operatorname{div}  0$?" "What is $1 \\div 0$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064763 :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction, $(-9) \\operatorname{div}  (-4) =$" "Expressed as a fraction, $(-9) \\div (-4) =$"] [:db/cas 17592186064764 :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction, $(-7) \\operatorname{div}  3 =$" "Expressed as a fraction, $(-7) \\div 3 =$"] [:db/cas 17592186064768 :question/problem "What is $0 \\operatorname{div}  16$?" "What is $0 \\div 16$?"] [:db/cas 17592186064770 :question/problem "Compute the value of $0 \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{3}{5}$." "Compute the value of $0 \\div \\frac{3}{5}$."] [:db/cas 17592186064771 :question/problem "Calculate the value of $0 \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{3}{4}$." "Calculate the value of $0 \\div \\frac{3}{4}$."] [:db/cas 17592186065360 :tutorial/content "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ For example, \n\n$$\n2^0 = 1 \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (-55)^0=1.\n$$\n\nTo understand why, let's try to convince ourselves that $2^0 = 1.$ First, we write down a table containing all of the powers of $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4} & =16 \\\\ {2}^{3} & =8 \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {2}^{1} & =2 \\\\ {2}^{0} & =◻\\,?\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by $2$ each time. Following this pattern, we must have \n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{0}}  = {\\color{blue}{2}}\\operatorname{div}  2 = 1.\n$$\n\nIt doesn't matter whether the number is positive or negative, a fraction or a decimal, big or small. Raising any non-zero number to the power of zero always gives $1.$" "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ For example, \n\n$$\n2^0 = 1 \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (-55)^0=1.\n$$\n\nTo understand why, let's try to convince ourselves that $2^0 = 1.$ First, we write down a table containing all of the powers of $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4} & =16 \\\\ {2}^{3} & =8 \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {2}^{1} & =2 \\\\ {2}^{0} & =◻\\,?\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by $2$ each time. Following this pattern, we must have \n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{0}}  = {\\color{blue}{2}}\\div 2 = 1.\n$$\n\nIt doesn't matter whether the number is positive or negative, a fraction or a decimal, big or small. Raising any non-zero number to the power of zero always gives $1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186065363 :tutorial/content "Dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero. For example:\n\n- $0 \\operatorname{div}  2 = 0$\n- $0\\operatorname{div}  (-456) = 0$\n\nTo understand why dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero, think about it this way: if you have zero pizzas, and you want to divide zero pizzas among ten friends, then each friend will get exactly zero pizza." "Dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero. For example:\n\n- $0 \\div 2 = 0$\n- $0\\div (-456) = 0$\n\nTo understand why dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero, think about it this way: if you have zero pizzas, and you want to divide zero pizzas among ten friends, then each friend will get exactly zero pizza."] [:db/cas 17592186065364 :tutorial/content "Division by zero is **undefined.** No number can be divided by zero, not even zero itself.\n\nTo see why, notice that if we divide $6$ by $3$, we get\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{div}  3 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n6 = 3\\times 2\n$$\n\nHowever, this argument does not work if we divide by zero. Suppose we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThen, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nand this relationship cannot be true because any number multiplied by zero is zero! Therefore, ? is not a number." "Division by zero is **undefined.** No number can be divided by zero, not even zero itself.\n\nTo see why, notice that if we divide $6$ by $3$, we get\n\n$$\n6\\div 3 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n6 = 3\\times 2\n$$\n\nHowever, this argument does not work if we divide by zero. Suppose we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThen, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nand this relationship cannot be true because any number multiplied by zero is zero! Therefore, ? is not a number."] [:db/cas 17592186065416 :tutorial/content "Dividing with negative numbers follows similar rules as multiplying with negative numbers. \n\nFirst of all, a negative number divided by a positive number gives a negative number. We can picture this rule as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,÷\\,(+)\\,=\\,(-).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's use this rule to find the value of $(-6) \\operatorname{div}  2.$\n\nHere, since we are dividing a negative number $\\color{red}(-6)$ by a positive number ${\\color{blue}(2)},$ the result is going to be negative:\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-6)} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{red} \\mathbf{-} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{3}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $6$ by $2$ *without* any signs: $6 \\operatorname{div}  2 = 3.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-6)} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{red} -3 }.\n$$" "Dividing with negative numbers follows similar rules as multiplying with negative numbers. \n\nFirst of all, a negative number divided by a positive number gives a negative number. We can picture this rule as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,÷\\,(+)\\,=\\,(-).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's use this rule to find the value of $(-6) \\div 2.$\n\nHere, since we are dividing a negative number $\\color{red}(-6)$ by a positive number ${\\color{blue}(2)},$ the result is going to be negative:\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-6)} \\div {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{red} \\mathbf{-} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{3}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $6$ by $2$ *without* any signs: $6 \\div 2 = 3.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-6)} \\div {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{red} -3 }.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186065417 :tutorial/content "A positive number divided by a negative number is also a negative number. We can picture this rule as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(+)\\,÷\\,(-)\\,=\\,(-).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, to find $8\\operatorname{div}  (-2),$ we have to divide a positive number $\\color{blue}(8)$ by a negative one ${\\color{red}(-2)}.$ So the result will be negative:\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{red}(-2)} = {\\color{red}\\mathbf{-} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{10}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $8$ by $2$ without any signs: $8 \\operatorname{div}  2 = 4.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{red}(-2)} = {\\color{red}-4}.\n$$" "A positive number divided by a negative number is also a negative number. We can picture this rule as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(+)\\,÷\\,(-)\\,=\\,(-).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, to find $8\\div (-2),$ we have to divide a positive number $\\color{blue}(8)$ by a negative one ${\\color{red}(-2)}.$ So the result will be negative:\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} \\div {\\color{red}(-2)} = {\\color{red}\\mathbf{-} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{10}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $8$ by $2$ without any signs: $8 \\div 2 = 4.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} \\div {\\color{red}(-2)} = {\\color{red}-4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186065418 :tutorial/content "Finally, the third rule is that a negative number divided by a negative number is a positive number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,÷\\,(-)\\,=\\,(+)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $-12 \\operatorname{div}  (-3).$ We have to divide a negative number $\\color{red}(-12)$ by another negative number ${\\color{red}(-3)}.$ So the result will be positive:\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-12)} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{blue} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{4}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $12$ by $3$ without any signs: $12 \\operatorname{div}  3 = 4.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-12)} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{blue}4}.\n$$" "Finally, the third rule is that a negative number divided by a negative number is a positive number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,÷\\,(-)\\,=\\,(+)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $-12 \\div (-3).$ We have to divide a negative number $\\color{red}(-12)$ by another negative number ${\\color{red}(-3)}.$ So the result will be positive:\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-12)} \\div {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{blue} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{4}}} }\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $12$ by $3$ without any signs: $12 \\div 3 = 4.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-12)} \\div {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{blue}4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186065421 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to simplify the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe expression above is a fraction, but there are additional fractions in the numerator and denominator.\nSuch expressions are called **fractions of fractions**.\nWe use parentheses to make it easier to read.\n\nThe trick to working with fractions of fractions is to remember that a fraction represents a division. So, a fraction of fractions is a division of two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}\\right)} = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) \\boldsymbol{\\operatorname{div} } \\left(\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\dfrac{\\color{red}{4}}{\\color{blue}{3}}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)÷\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) &= \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\times \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\times 4}{3\\times 3} \\\\ &= \\frac{8}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)} = \\dfrac{8}{9}.\n$$" "Let's think about how to simplify the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe expression above is a fraction, but there are additional fractions in the numerator and denominator.\nSuch expressions are called **fractions of fractions**.\nWe use parentheses to make it easier to read.\n\nThe trick to working with fractions of fractions is to remember that a fraction represents a division. So, a fraction of fractions is a division of two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}\\right)} = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) \\boldsymbol{\\div} \\left(\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\dfrac{\\color{red}{4}}{\\color{blue}{3}}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)÷\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) &= \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\times \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\times 4}{3\\times 3} \\\\ &= \\frac{8}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)} = \\dfrac{8}{9}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068597 :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{y}{6x^2},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac 3 x \\operatorname{div}   \\dfrac y {6x^2} &= \\dfrac 3 x \\cdot  \\dfrac {6x^2} y \\\\ &= \\dfrac {18x^2} {xy} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {18x^\\cancel{2}} {\\cancel{x}y} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {18x} {y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of $x$ in the numerator and denominator." "To compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x} \\div \\dfrac{y}{6x^2},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac 3 x \\div  \\dfrac y {6x^2} &= \\dfrac 3 x \\cdot  \\dfrac {6x^2} y \\\\ &= \\dfrac {18x^2} {xy} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {18x^\\cancel{2}} {\\cancel{x}y} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {18x} {y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of $x$ in the numerator and denominator."] [:db/cas 17592186068598 :question/problem "Given that $\\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  =x$ and $\\dfrac {1+b}{5b^2} =y ,$ then what is $x \\operatorname{div}  y?$" "Given that $\\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  =x$ and $\\dfrac {1+b}{5b^2} =y ,$ then what is $x \\div y?$"] [:db/cas 17592186068598 :question/worked-solution "To compute $x \\operatorname{div}  y,$ i.e., \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{a}^{2}(1+b)}{3b} & ÷\\frac{1+b}{5{b}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  \\operatorname{div}   \\dfrac{1+b}{5b^2} &= \\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {5b^2} {1+b} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2a^2\\cancel{(1+b)}} {3\\cancel{b}}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {5b^{\\cancel{2}}} {\\cancel{1+b}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {10a^2b} {3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute $x \\div y,$ i.e., \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{a}^{2}(1+b)}{3b} & ÷\\frac{1+b}{5{b}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  \\div  \\dfrac{1+b}{5b^2} &= \\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {5b^2} {1+b} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2a^2\\cancel{(1+b)}} {3\\cancel{b}}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {5b^{\\cancel{2}}} {\\cancel{1+b}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {10a^2b} {3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068604 :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{5}{y},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac 3 x \\operatorname{div}   \\dfrac 5 y &= \\dfrac 3 x \\cdot  \\dfrac y 5 \\\\ &= \\dfrac {3y} {5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x} \\div \\dfrac{5}{y},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac 3 x \\div  \\dfrac 5 y &= \\dfrac 3 x \\cdot  \\dfrac y 5 \\\\ &= \\dfrac {3y} {5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186068605 :question/problem "Given that $\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  =x$ and $\\dfrac {2a} {14b^2}=y,$ then what is $x \\operatorname{div}  y?$" "Given that $\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  =x$ and $\\dfrac {2a} {14b^2}=y,$ then what is $x \\div y?$"] [:db/cas 17592186068605 :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\t\t\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  &\\operatorname{div}   \\dfrac {2a} {14b^2},\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  \\operatorname{div}   \\dfrac {2a} {14b^2} &= \\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  \\cdot \\dfrac {14b^2} {2a} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {2a(a^2 - 5)} {7b}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {2b \\cdot 7b} {2a} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {\\cancel{2a}(a^2 - 5)} {\\cancel{7b}}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {2b \\cdot \\cancel{7b}} {\\cancel{2a}} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {2b(a^2 - 5)} 1 \\\\ &= 2a^2b - 10b\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\n\t\t\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  &\\div  \\dfrac {2a} {14b^2},\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  \\div  \\dfrac {2a} {14b^2} &= \\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  \\cdot \\dfrac {14b^2} {2a} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {2a(a^2 - 5)} {7b}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {2b \\cdot 7b} {2a} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {\\cancel{2a}(a^2 - 5)} {\\cancel{7b}}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {2b \\cdot \\cancel{7b}} {\\cancel{2a}} \\\\ &= \\dfrac {2b(a^2 - 5)} 1 \\\\ &= 2a^2b - 10b\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069064 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{7s + 14} \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{1}{s^{2} - 4}\n$$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{7s + 14} \\div \\frac{1}{s^{2} - 4}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186069214 :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{1}{3z + 18} = a$ and $\\frac{1}{z^{2} + 6z} = b$, what is the value of $a \\operatorname{div}  b$?" "Given that $\\frac{1}{3z + 18} = a$ and $\\frac{1}{z^{2} + 6z} = b$, what is the value of $a \\div b$?"] [:db/cas 17592186069502 :question/problem "What is $\\frac{2x}{5} \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{3y}{2}$?" "What is $\\frac{2x}{5} \\div \\frac{3y}{2}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186069973 :tutorial/content "To divide one rational expression by another, we multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. Just like with fractions!\n\nFor example, let's divide $x$ by $\\dfrac{3}{y}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{3}{y}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. The divisor is $\\dfrac{3}{y},$ and to get the reciprocal, we just flip the fraction upside down: $\\dfrac{y}{3}.$ Multiplying by the reciprocal of the divisor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x÷\\frac{3}{y} &= x\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{xy}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples!" "To divide one rational expression by another, we multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. Just like with fractions!\n\nFor example, let's divide $x$ by $\\dfrac{3}{y}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx \\div \\dfrac{3}{y}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. The divisor is $\\dfrac{3}{y},$ and to get the reciprocal, we just flip the fraction upside down: $\\dfrac{y}{3}.$ Multiplying by the reciprocal of the divisor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x÷\\frac{3}{y} &= x\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{xy}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples!"] [:db/cas 17592186070221 :answer/value "3 \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{1}{4}" "3 \\div \\frac{1}{4}"] [:db/cas 17592186070222 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4} \\operatorname{div}  3" "\\frac{1}{4} \\div 3"] [:db/cas 17592186070225 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3} \\operatorname{div}  7" "\\frac{1}{3} \\div 7"] [:db/cas 17592186070228 :answer/value "7 \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{1}{3}" "7 \\div \\frac{1}{3}"] [:db/cas 17592186072405 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3} \\operatorname{div}  5 = \\frac{5}{15}" "\\frac{1}{3} \\div 5 = \\frac{5}{15}"] [:db/cas 17592186072406 :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3} \\operatorname{div}  5 = \\frac{1}{3}" "\\frac{5}{3} \\div 5 = \\frac{1}{3}"] [:db/cas 17592186072407 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5} \\operatorname{div}  3 = \\frac{1}{15}" "\\frac{1}{5} \\div 3 = \\frac{1}{15}"] [:db/cas 17592186072408 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5} \\operatorname{div}  3 = \\frac{3}{15}" "\\frac{1}{5} \\div 3 = \\frac{3}{15}"] [:db/cas 17592186072409 :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5} \\operatorname{div}  3 = \\frac{1}{5}" "\\frac{3}{5} \\div 3 = \\frac{1}{5}"] [:db/cas 17592186073415 :question/worked-solution "To find out the height of the pile of boxes, we have to multiply $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ by $5.$\n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n5 \\times \\dfrac34 = \\dfrac{5\\times 3}{4} = \\dfrac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert $\\dfrac {15}{4}$ to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n15 \\operatorname{div}  4 = 3\\,\\textrm{R}\\,3 = 3\\,\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nSo, Greg's stack of boxes was $3\\,\\dfrac{3}{4}\\,\\textrm{ft}$ high." "To find out the height of the pile of boxes, we have to multiply $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ by $5.$\n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n5 \\times \\dfrac34 = \\dfrac{5\\times 3}{4} = \\dfrac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert $\\dfrac {15}{4}$ to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n15 \\div 4 = 3\\,\\textrm{R}\\,3 = 3\\,\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nSo, Greg's stack of boxes was $3\\,\\dfrac{3}{4}\\,\\textrm{ft}$ high."] [:db/cas 17592186073416 :question/worked-solution "To find out how long Mr. Stewart watches the show each week, we need to multiply $\\dfrac{5}{6}$ by $5.$ \n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n5\\times\\dfrac56 = \\dfrac{5\\times 5}{6} = \\dfrac{25}6\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert $\\dfrac {25}{6}$ to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n25 \\operatorname{div}  6 = 4\\,\\textrm{R}\\,1 = 4\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, Mr. Stewart spends a total of  $4\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}$ hours watching the show, which lies between $4$ and $5$ hours." "To find out how long Mr. Stewart watches the show each week, we need to multiply $\\dfrac{5}{6}$ by $5.$ \n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n5\\times\\dfrac56 = \\dfrac{5\\times 5}{6} = \\dfrac{25}6\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert $\\dfrac {25}{6}$ to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n25 \\div 6 = 4\\,\\textrm{R}\\,1 = 4\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, Mr. Stewart spends a total of  $4\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}$ hours watching the show, which lies between $4$ and $5$ hours."] [:db/cas 17592186073421 :question/problem "![](images/f5/f5a23469d826e99bc4975a4f2901a18b8af46fb1afbe4315d34a1d82d075ddb2.png)\n\nUse the area model above to find the value of $9\\operatorname{div} \\dfrac 1 3$." "![](images/f5/f5a23469d826e99bc4975a4f2901a18b8af46fb1afbe4315d34a1d82d075ddb2.png)\n\nUse the area model above to find the value of $9\\div\\dfrac 1 3$."] [:db/cas 17592186073421 :question/worked-solution "The area of a rectangle equals its length multiplied by its width.\n\nFor the given rectangle:\n\n- its length is $27$\n- its width is $\\dfrac{1}{3}$\n- its area is ${9}$\n\nTherefore, this area model shows the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n27\\times\\dfrac 1 {3} = 9\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can turn this multiplication problem into the following division problem:\n\n$$\n27 = 9\\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac 13\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $27$." "The area of a rectangle equals its length multiplied by its width.\n\nFor the given rectangle:\n\n- its length is $27$\n- its width is $\\dfrac{1}{3}$\n- its area is ${9}$\n\nTherefore, this area model shows the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n27\\times\\dfrac 1 {3} = 9\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can turn this multiplication problem into the following division problem:\n\n$$\n27 = 9\\div \\dfrac 13\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $27$."] [:db/cas 17592186073422 :question/problem "Draw an area model that could be used to represent the division problem $\\dfrac 1 {8}\\operatorname{div}  5 = \\dfrac 1 {40}$." "Draw an area model that could be used to represent the division problem $\\dfrac 1 {8}\\div 5 = \\dfrac 1 {40}$."] [:db/cas 17592186073422 :question/worked-solution "Our division problem is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {8} \\operatorname{div}  5 = \\dfrac 1{40}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can turn this division problem into the following multiplication problems:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{8} = \\dfrac 1 {40} \\times 5 \\qquad\\textrm{or}\\qquad \\dfrac 1{8} = 5 \\times \\dfrac 1 {40}\n$$\n\nTo describe these multiplication problems using an area model, we need a rectangle with the following properties:\n\n- its area is $\\dfrac 1{8}$\n- its sides have lengths $\\dfrac1{40}$ and $5$\n\nAn area model that represents this multiplication problem could be the following:\n\n![](images/08/08643d8599a91b4976fefc2a0c89eefa794e29e25256851220aaea1d40622891.png)" "Our division problem is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {8} \\div 5 = \\dfrac 1{40}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can turn this division problem into the following multiplication problems:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{8} = \\dfrac 1 {40} \\times 5 \\qquad\\textrm{or}\\qquad \\dfrac 1{8} = 5 \\times \\dfrac 1 {40}\n$$\n\nTo describe these multiplication problems using an area model, we need a rectangle with the following properties:\n\n- its area is $\\dfrac 1{8}$\n- its sides have lengths $\\dfrac1{40}$ and $5$\n\nAn area model that represents this multiplication problem could be the following:\n\n![](images/08/08643d8599a91b4976fefc2a0c89eefa794e29e25256851220aaea1d40622891.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186073423 :question/worked-solution "The area of a rectangle equals its length multiplied by its width.\n\nFor the given rectangle:\n\n- its length is $3$\n- its width is $\\dfrac{1}{24}$\n- its area is $\\dfrac{1}{8}$\n\nTherefore, the area model represents the following multiplication problems:\n\n$$\n3 \\times {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{24}} = \\dfrac{1}{8} \\qquad\\textrm{or}\\qquad {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{24}} \\times 3 = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can turn this multiplication problem into a division problem where the quotient equals\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{24}}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\bbox[5pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\dfrac{1}{8} \\operatorname{div}  3} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{24}}\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8} \\operatorname{div}  3\n$$" "The area of a rectangle equals its length multiplied by its width.\n\nFor the given rectangle:\n\n- its length is $3$\n- its width is $\\dfrac{1}{24}$\n- its area is $\\dfrac{1}{8}$\n\nTherefore, the area model represents the following multiplication problems:\n\n$$\n3 \\times {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{24}} = \\dfrac{1}{8} \\qquad\\textrm{or}\\qquad {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{24}} \\times 3 = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can turn this multiplication problem into a division problem where the quotient equals\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{24}}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\bbox[5pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\dfrac{1}{8} \\div 3} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{24}}\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8} \\div 3\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073447 :question/worked-solution "All of the answer choices have the second number equal to $3.$ So our equivalent ratio is of the form $\\,\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}} : 3.$  \n\nTo find a ratio that's equivalent to $12 : 9,$ we divide both numbers by the same value. In particular, we need to get $3$ from ${\\color{red}{9}},$ so we divide ${\\color{red}{9}}$ by $3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\boxed{\\text{\\phantom{0}}} &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So to get $\\,\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}}$ from ${\\color{blue}{12}},$ we divide $12$ by $3$ as well:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}12} \\operatorname{div}  3 =4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12:9$ is equivalent to $4:3.$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "All of the answer choices have the second number equal to $3.$ So our equivalent ratio is of the form $\\,\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}} : 3.$  \n\nTo find a ratio that's equivalent to $12 : 9,$ we divide both numbers by the same value. In particular, we need to get $3$ from ${\\color{red}{9}},$ so we divide ${\\color{red}{9}}$ by $3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\boxed{\\text{\\phantom{0}}} &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So to get $\\,\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}}$ from ${\\color{blue}{12}},$ we divide $12$ by $3$ as well:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}12} \\div 3 =4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12:9$ is equivalent to $4:3.$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186073454 :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}6}:{\\color{red}18}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}6}}{{\\color{red}18}}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}6}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}18}$).\n\nNow we divide the numerator and the denominator by $6$ to find an equivalent ratio:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{6}}\\operatorname{div}  6}{{\\color{red}{18}}\\operatorname{div}  6} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}1}}{{\\color{red}3}}\n$$\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a denominator of $24,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of the previous fraction by $8{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{1}}\\times 8}{{\\color{red}{3}}\\times 8} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}}{{\\color{red}24}}\n$$\n\n- To create a numerator of $20,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $20{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{1}}\\times 20}{{\\color{red}{3}}\\times 20} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}20}}{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}60}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}18}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}24}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}20}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}60}$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $8$ and $60.$" "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}6}:{\\color{red}18}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}6}}{{\\color{red}18}}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}6}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}18}$).\n\nNow we divide the numerator and the denominator by $6$ to find an equivalent ratio:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{6}}\\div 6}{{\\color{red}{18}}\\div 6} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}1}}{{\\color{red}3}}\n$$\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a denominator of $24,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of the previous fraction by $8{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{1}}\\times 8}{{\\color{red}{3}}\\times 8} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}}{{\\color{red}24}}\n$$\n\n- To create a numerator of $20,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $20{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{1}}\\times 20}{{\\color{red}{3}}\\times 20} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}20}}{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}60}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}18}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}24}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}20}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}60}$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $8$ and $60.$"] [:db/cas 17592186073456 :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}12}:{\\color{red}4}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}12}}{{\\color{red}4}}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow we divide the numerator and the denominator by $4$ to find an equivalent ratio:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{12}}\\operatorname{div}  4}{{\\color{red}{4}}\\operatorname{div}  4} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nWe can generate ratios that are equivalent to $3:1$ by finding more equivalent fractions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\times 3}{1\\times 3} & =\\frac{9}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3\\times 5}{1\\times 5} & =\\frac{15}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reward (in dollars)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Hours of work</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9}$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}5}$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $9$ and $5.$" "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}12}:{\\color{red}4}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}12}}{{\\color{red}4}}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow we divide the numerator and the denominator by $4$ to find an equivalent ratio:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{12}}\\div 4}{{\\color{red}{4}}\\div 4} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nWe can generate ratios that are equivalent to $3:1$ by finding more equivalent fractions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\times 3}{1\\times 3} & =\\frac{9}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3\\times 5}{1\\times 5} & =\\frac{15}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reward (in dollars)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Hours of work</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9}$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}5}$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $9$ and $5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186073462 :question/problem "What model could represent the division problem below?\n\n$$\n3 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{6} = 18\n$$" "What model could represent the division problem below?\n\n$$\n3 \\div \\dfrac{1}{6} = 18\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073462 :question/worked-solution "Consider the following $\\color{red}3$ wholes:\n\n![](images/23/235e19c4e3231eb22e6d43ccadcd90c5694077ec4ce562e6c4f188cfff77545f.png)\n\nTo calculate ${\\color{red}3} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}6}$, we need to divide each of $\\color{red}3$ wholes into $\\color{blue}6$ equal parts, as shown below. This gives us the corresponding model for the given division problem.\n\n![](images/3b/3b3551ad59a9d5abe2bf03e350f373412959293c68a4f059123fafcbabf72936.png)\n\nWe get ${\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}6}= 18$ pieces in total." "Consider the following $\\color{red}3$ wholes:\n\n![](images/23/235e19c4e3231eb22e6d43ccadcd90c5694077ec4ce562e6c4f188cfff77545f.png)\n\nTo calculate ${\\color{red}3} \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}6}$, we need to divide each of $\\color{red}3$ wholes into $\\color{blue}6$ equal parts, as shown below. This gives us the corresponding model for the given division problem.\n\n![](images/3b/3b3551ad59a9d5abe2bf03e350f373412959293c68a4f059123fafcbabf72936.png)\n\nWe get ${\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}6}= 18$ pieces in total."] [:db/cas 17592186073463 :question/problem "What is the value of $\\dfrac{1}{4} \\operatorname{div}  3 ?$" "What is the value of $\\dfrac{1}{4} \\div 3 ?$"] [:db/cas 17592186073463 :question/worked-solution "To divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\operatorname{div}  3  = \\dfrac{1}{4 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{1}{12}\n$$" "To divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\div 3  = \\dfrac{1}{4 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{1}{12}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073465 :question/problem "What is the value of $\\dfrac{1}{16} \\operatorname{div}  5 ?$" "What is the value of $\\dfrac{1}{16} \\div 5 ?$"] [:db/cas 17592186073465 :question/worked-solution "To divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{16} \\operatorname{div}  5  = \\dfrac{1}{16 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{1}{80}\n$$" "To divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{16} \\div 5  = \\dfrac{1}{16 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{1}{80}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073466 :question/worked-solution "To determine the amount of ham on each pizza, we must divide the amount of ham by the number of pizzas:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\operatorname{div}  4\n$$\n\nTo divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\operatorname{div}  4  = \\dfrac{1}{2 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Joshua puts $\\dfrac{1}{8} \\, \\textrm{kg}$ of ham on each pizza." "To determine the amount of ham on each pizza, we must divide the amount of ham by the number of pizzas:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\div 4\n$$\n\nTo divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\div 4  = \\dfrac{1}{2 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Joshua puts $\\dfrac{1}{8} \\, \\textrm{kg}$ of ham on each pizza."] [:db/cas 17592186073467 :question/problem "Each of the three shapes in the model below represents one whole.\n\n![](images/22/222af9ec253a6624ece646225db926069cd7be3ac416fe846a9216360981f8cb.png)\n\n \n\nFrom left to right, what numbers are missing from the division problem below?\n$$\n\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}\n$$" "Each of the three shapes in the model below represents one whole.\n\n![](images/22/222af9ec253a6624ece646225db926069cd7be3ac416fe846a9216360981f8cb.png)\n\n \n\nFrom left to right, what numbers are missing from the division problem below?\n$$\n\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}} \\div \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073467 :question/worked-solution "In the model, there are $\\color{red}3$ shapes. Each shape consists of $4$ pieces that each represent $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ of a whole. There are $\\color{blue}12$ pieces in total.\n\nThe model tells us that when we divide $\\color{red}3$ wholes into quarters, we will be left with $\\color{blue}12$ pieces.\n\nTherefore, the division problem is:\n$$\n\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{red}3} \\hspace{0.167em}}} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{blue}12} \\hspace{0.167em}}}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing numbers are $\\color{red}3$ and $\\color{blue}12.$" "In the model, there are $\\color{red}3$ shapes. Each shape consists of $4$ pieces that each represent $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ of a whole. There are $\\color{blue}12$ pieces in total.\n\nThe model tells us that when we divide $\\color{red}3$ wholes into quarters, we will be left with $\\color{blue}12$ pieces.\n\nTherefore, the division problem is:\n$$\n\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{red}3} \\hspace{0.167em}}} \\div \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{blue}12} \\hspace{0.167em}}}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing numbers are $\\color{red}3$ and $\\color{blue}12.$"] 