86073470 :question/worked-solution "To divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator:\n\n$$\n5 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{15} = 5 \\times 15 = 75\n$$" "To divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator:\n\n$$\n5 \\div \\dfrac{1}{15} = 5 \\times 15 = 75\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073471 :question/worked-solution "To determine the number of pizzas that Johnny can make, we divide the amount of wheat flour by the amount of wheat flour each pizza needs.\n\n$$\n5 \\operatorname{div}   \\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nTo divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator:\n\n$$\n5 \\operatorname{div}   \\dfrac{1}{10} = 5 \\times 10 = 50\n$$\n\nTherefore, Johnny can make $50$ pizzas." "To determine the number of pizzas that Johnny can make, we divide the amount of wheat flour by the amount of wheat flour each pizza needs.\n\n$$\n5 \\div  \\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nTo divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator:\n\n$$\n5 \\div  \\dfrac{1}{10} = 5 \\times 10 = 50\n$$\n\nTherefore, Johnny can make $50$ pizzas."] [:db/cas 17592186073476 :question/problem "By rounding the dividend to the nearest whole number, estimate the value of $94.8 \\operatorname{div}  5$." "By rounding the dividend to the nearest whole number, estimate the value of $94.8 \\div 5$."] [:db/cas 17592186073476 :question/worked-solution "First, we round the dividend to the nearest whole number:\n$94.8 \\rightarrow 95$\n\nHence, we need to find the value of $95 \\operatorname{div}  5$. Computing the quotient, we get:\n\n| $1$$9$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$4$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$5$$0$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n95 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 19\n$$\n\nTherefore, $94.8 \\operatorname{div}  5$ is approximately equal to $19$." "First, we round the dividend to the nearest whole number:\n$94.8 \\rightarrow 95$\n\nHence, we need to find the value of $95 \\div 5$. Computing the quotient, we get:\n\n| $1$$9$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$4$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$5$$0$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n95 \\div 5 = 19\n$$\n\nTherefore, $94.8 \\div 5$ is approximately equal to $19$."] [:db/cas 17592186073477 :question/problem "Estimate $12.3 \\operatorname{div}  106.8$ by rounding the divisor to the nearest hundred." "Estimate $12.3 \\div 106.8$ by rounding the divisor to the nearest hundred."] [:db/cas 17592186073477 :question/worked-solution "First, we round the divisor to the nearest hundred:\n$106.8 \\rightarrow 100$\n\nNow, we need to compute\n\n$$\n12.3 \\operatorname{div}  100\n$$\n\nThere are two zeros in $100$, so we move the decimal point two places to the left:\n\n$$\n12.3 \\rightarrow 0.123\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12.3 \\operatorname{div}  106.8$ is approximately equal to $0.123 \\,$." "First, we round the divisor to the nearest hundred:\n$106.8 \\rightarrow 100$\n\nNow, we need to compute\n\n$$\n12.3 \\div 100\n$$\n\nThere are two zeros in $100$, so we move the decimal point two places to the left:\n\n$$\n12.3 \\rightarrow 0.123\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12.3 \\div 106.8$ is approximately equal to $0.123 \\,$."] [:db/cas 17592186073478 :question/problem "Estimate the value of $206.6 \\operatorname{div}  3.43$ by rounding each number to the nearest whole number." "Estimate the value of $206.6 \\div 3.43$ by rounding each number to the nearest whole number."] [:db/cas 17592186073478 :question/worked-solution "We round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}206.6 & ÷ & 3.43 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{207}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{3}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the value of\n\n$$\n207 \\operatorname{div}  3\n$$\n\nSo, we go through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $6$$9$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$8$$2$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$7$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $206.6 \\operatorname{div}  3.43$ is approximately equal to $69$." "We round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}206.6 & ÷ & 3.43 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{207}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{3}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the value of\n\n$$\n207 \\div 3\n$$\n\nSo, we go through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $6$$9$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$8$$2$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$7$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $206.6 \\div 3.43$ is approximately equal to $69$."] [:db/cas 17592186073479 :question/problem "Estimate the value of $116.8 \\operatorname{div}  5.15$ by rounding each number to the nearest whole number. Round your final answer to the nearest whole number." "Estimate the value of $116.8 \\div 5.15$ by rounding each number to the nearest whole number. Round your final answer to the nearest whole number."] [:db/cas 17592186073479 :question/worked-solution "We round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}116.8 & ÷ & 5.15 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{117}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{5}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the value of\n$117\\operatorname{div}  5$.\n\nSo, we go through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n|  |  | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$ |  |  |\n| $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n|  |  | $1$ | $7$ |\n|  | $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $1$ | $5$ |\n|  |  |  | $\\fbox{2}$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n117 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 23\\,\\text{R}2\n$$\n\nThe remainder $2$ is closer to $0$ than to the divisor $5$. So, to round to the nearest whole number, we must round down:\n\n$$\n117 \\operatorname{div}  5 \\rightarrow 23\n$$\n\nTherefore, $116.8 \\operatorname{div}  5.15$ is approximately equal to $23$." "We round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}116.8 & ÷ & 5.15 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{117}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{5}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the value of\n$117\\div 5$.\n\nSo, we go through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n|  |  | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$ |  |  |\n| $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n|  |  | $1$ | $7$ |\n|  | $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $1$ | $5$ |\n|  |  |  | $\\fbox{2}$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n117 \\div 5 = 23\\,\\text{R}2\n$$\n\nThe remainder $2$ is closer to $0$ than to the divisor $5$. So, to round to the nearest whole number, we must round down:\n\n$$\n117 \\div 5 \\rightarrow 23\n$$\n\nTherefore, $116.8 \\div 5.15$ is approximately equal to $23$."] [:db/cas 17592186073486 :question/worked-solution "We have $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/78/785deacac749a6ed43e5e6a2638677e8ff9e9b58087cb4e471ea85fd50609ce6.png)\n\nThe division operation splits the shaded region on the left into $2$ equal parts. So the shaded part on the left is $2$ times larger than the shaded part on the right.\n\nTherefore, the division problem shown is:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\operatorname{div}  2 = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$" "We have $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/78/785deacac749a6ed43e5e6a2638677e8ff9e9b58087cb4e471ea85fd50609ce6.png)\n\nThe division operation splits the shaded region on the left into $2$ equal parts. So the shaded part on the left is $2$ times larger than the shaded part on the right.\n\nTherefore, the division problem shown is:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\div 2 = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073487 :question/worked-solution "We have $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{1}{15}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/ed/ed5517a9242aed1c5b521ca07772dacbb7c96a73c69d4d9c6214a098742358e2.png)\n\nThe division operation splits the shaded region on the left into $5$ equal parts. So the shaded part on the left is $5$ times larger than the shaded part on the right. \n\nTherefore, the division problem shown is:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}{5}} = \\dfrac{1}{15}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{blue}5.$" "We have $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{1}{15}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/ed/ed5517a9242aed1c5b521ca07772dacbb7c96a73c69d4d9c6214a098742358e2.png)\n\nThe division operation splits the shaded region on the left into $5$ equal parts. So the shaded part on the left is $5$ times larger than the shaded part on the right. \n\nTherefore, the division problem shown is:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\div {\\color{blue}{5}} = \\dfrac{1}{15}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{blue}5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186073489 :question/problem "Use the model below to calculate $\\dfrac{1}{4} \\operatorname{div}  4.$\n\n![](images/8e/8eccf163bffabb43593188590a1f995544f3d1ac0aebcdd87dce638b230f8165.png)" "Use the model below to calculate $\\dfrac{1}{4} \\div 4.$\n\n![](images/8e/8eccf163bffabb43593188590a1f995544f3d1ac0aebcdd87dce638b230f8165.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186073489 :question/worked-solution "Since we need to divide by $\\color{blue}4$, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}4$ equal parts horizontally.\n\n![](images/a3/a34a3fb198ef2ea79f8baf8f0acbdbdc179c6259c614824315d3d96e5ea4e921.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac{1}{16}$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\operatorname{div}  4 = \\dfrac{1}{16}\\,.\n$$" "Since we need to divide by $\\color{blue}4$, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}4$ equal parts horizontally.\n\n![](images/a3/a34a3fb198ef2ea79f8baf8f0acbdbdc179c6259c614824315d3d96e5ea4e921.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac{1}{16}$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\div 4 = \\dfrac{1}{16}\\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073503 :question/worked-solution "To know how many kilograms they bought, we have to calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n3 + \\dfrac{11}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add\n\n$$\n3 + \\dfrac{11}{\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\n, we need to express the whole number $3$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of ${\\color{blue}{3}}$.\n\nFirst, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}3$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{3}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\times {\\color{blue}{3}}}{1 \\times{\\color{blue}{3}}} = \\dfrac{9}{3}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find the following sum:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{3} + \\dfrac{11}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same.\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{3} + \\frac{11}{3} = \\frac{9 + 11}{3} = \\frac{20}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert this to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n20 \\operatorname{div}  3 = 6 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 2 = 6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nThus, Tom and Margaret bought\n\n$$\n6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nkilograms of fruit." "To know how many kilograms they bought, we have to calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n3 + \\dfrac{11}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add\n\n$$\n3 + \\dfrac{11}{\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\n, we need to express the whole number $3$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of ${\\color{blue}{3}}$.\n\nFirst, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}3$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{3}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\times {\\color{blue}{3}}}{1 \\times{\\color{blue}{3}}} = \\dfrac{9}{3}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find the following sum:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{3} + \\dfrac{11}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same.\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{3} + \\frac{11}{3} = \\frac{9 + 11}{3} = \\frac{20}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert this to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n20 \\div 3 = 6 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 2 = 6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nThus, Tom and Margaret bought\n\n$$\n6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nkilograms of fruit."] [:db/cas 17592186073522 :question/worked-solution "To subtract $2$ from\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{31}{\\color{blue}{9}}\n$$\n\n, we need to express the whole number $2$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of ${\\color{blue}{9}}$.\n\nFirst, we write $2$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}9$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{9}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2 \\times {\\color{blue}{9}}}{1\\times{\\color{blue}{9}}} = \\dfrac{18}{9}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{31}{9} - \\dfrac{18}{9}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{31}{9} - \\frac{18}{9} = \\frac{31 - 18}{9} = \\frac{13}{9}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n13 \\operatorname{div}  9 = 1 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 4\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is\n\n$$\n1 \\, \\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$" "To subtract $2$ from\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{31}{\\color{blue}{9}}\n$$\n\n, we need to express the whole number $2$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of ${\\color{blue}{9}}$.\n\nFirst, we write $2$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}9$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{9}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2 \\times {\\color{blue}{9}}}{1\\times{\\color{blue}{9}}} = \\dfrac{18}{9}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{31}{9} - \\dfrac{18}{9}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{31}{9} - \\frac{18}{9} = \\frac{31 - 18}{9} = \\frac{13}{9}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n13 \\div 9 = 1 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 4\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is\n\n$$\n1 \\, \\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073523 :question/worked-solution "To find out how much juice is left, we have to calculate the difference\n\n$$\n4 - \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nTo subtract\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15}{\\color{blue}{8}}\n$$\n\nfrom $4$, we need to express the whole number $4$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of ${\\color{blue}{8}}$.\n\nFirst, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}8$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{8}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4 \\times {\\color{blue}{8}}}{1\\times{\\color{blue}{8}}} = \\dfrac{32}{8}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{32}{8} - \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{32}{8} - \\frac{15}{8} = \\frac{32 - 15}{8} = \\frac{17}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n17 \\operatorname{div}  8 = 2 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nliters of juice is left." "To find out how much juice is left, we have to calculate the difference\n\n$$\n4 - \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nTo subtract\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15}{\\color{blue}{8}}\n$$\n\nfrom $4$, we need to express the whole number $4$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of ${\\color{blue}{8}}$.\n\nFirst, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}8$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{8}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4 \\times {\\color{blue}{8}}}{1\\times{\\color{blue}{8}}} = \\dfrac{32}{8}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{32}{8} - \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{32}{8} - \\frac{15}{8} = \\frac{32 - 15}{8} = \\frac{17}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n17 \\div 8 = 2 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nliters of juice is left."] [:db/cas 17592186073524 :question/worked-solution "To find out how long each of the pieces is, we need to divide $85$ by $5$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}8} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$3$$\\color{lightgray}5$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\operatorname{div}  5 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,3$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $5 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 5$Subtract: ${\\color{red}8} - 5 = 3$Bring down: $5$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}7$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$\\color{red}3$$\\color{red}5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$5$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}35} \\operatorname{div}  5 = {\\color{blue}7}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}7$Multiply: $5 \\times {\\color{blue}7} = 35$Subtract: ${\\color{red}35} - 35 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $85$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n85 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 17\n$$\n\nThis means that each piece of rope is $17$ inches long." "To find out how long each of the pieces is, we need to divide $85$ by $5$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}8} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$3$$\\color{lightgray}5$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}8} \\div 5 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,3$Write over