iv></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe get $\\fbox{0}$, so the division is done.\n\nThe quotient is the sum of the numbers on top of the boxes:\n\n$$\n50+5+2=57\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1,482 \\div 26 = 57\\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073538 :question/worked-solution "To find out how much water Larry drank, we need to multiply $12$ by $\\dfrac{3}{4}.$\n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac34 \\times 12 = \\dfrac{3\\times 12}{4} = \\dfrac{36}4\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{36}{4} = 36 \\operatorname{div}  4=9\n$$\n\nSo, Larry drank $9\\,\\textrm{oz}$ of water." "To find out how much water Larry drank, we need to multiply $12$ by $\\dfrac{3}{4}.$\n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac34 \\times 12 = \\dfrac{3\\times 12}{4} = \\dfrac{36}4\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{36}{4} = 36 \\div 4=9\n$$\n\nSo, Larry drank $9\\,\\textrm{oz}$ of water."] [:db/cas 17592186073539 :question/worked-solution "To find the fraction of the original bag Jim received, we need to multiply $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ by $\\dfrac{6}{7}.$  \n\nTo do that, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{6}{7} =  \\dfrac {1 \\times 6} {3 \\times 7} = \\dfrac{6}{21}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{21} = \\dfrac{6 \\operatorname{div}  3}{21 \\operatorname{div}  3} = \\dfrac{2}{7}\n$$\n\nSo, Jim received $\\dfrac{2}{7}$ of the initial bag of marbles." "To find the fraction of the original bag Jim received, we need to multiply $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ by $\\dfrac{6}{7}.$  \n\nTo do that, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{6}{7} =  \\dfrac {1 \\times 6} {3 \\times 7} = \\dfrac{6}{21}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{21} = \\dfrac{6 \\div 3}{21 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{2}{7}\n$$\n\nSo, Jim received $\\dfrac{2}{7}$ of the initial bag of marbles."] [:db/cas 17592186073540 :question/worked-solution "To find out the amount of coffee Jacob drank, we need to calculate $\\dfrac{1}{4} \\times \\dfrac{8}{3}.$\n\nTo do that, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\times \\dfrac{8}{3} =  \\dfrac{1 \\times 8}{4 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{8}{12}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{12} = \\dfrac{8 \\operatorname{div}  4}{12\\operatorname{div}  4} =\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, Jacob drank $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ cups of coffee." "To find out the amount of coffee Jacob drank, we need to calculate $\\dfrac{1}{4} \\times \\dfrac{8}{3}.$\n\nTo do that, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\times \\dfrac{8}{3} =  \\dfrac{1 \\times 8}{4 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{8}{12}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{12} = \\dfrac{8 \\div 4}{12\\div 4} =\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, Jacob drank $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ cups of coffee."] [:db/cas 17592186073545 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7.2}{0.6}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$ so that both become whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7.2\\times 10}{0.6\\times 10} = \\dfrac{72}{6}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $72\\operatorname{div}  6$. We will use long division:\n\n| $6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}7} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}2$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}7} \\operatorname{div}  6 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}7} - 6 = 1$Bring down: $2$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}2$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}12} \\operatorname{div}  6 = {\\color{blue}2}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 12$Subtract: ${\\color{red}12} - 12 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, so the division is done.\n\nWe find that\n\n$$\n72 \\operatorname{div}  6 = 12\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n7.2 \\operatorname{div}  0.6 = 12 \\,\n$$" "First, we write the division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7.2}{0.6}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$ so that both become whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7.2\\times 10}{0.6\\times 10} = \\dfrac{72}{6}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $72\\div 6$. We will use long division:\n\n| $6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the tens:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}7} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}2$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}7} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}7} - 6 = 1$Bring down: $2$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}2$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}12} \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}2}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $6 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 12$Subtract: ${\\color{red}12} - 12 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, so the division is done.\n\nWe find that\n\n$$\n72 \\div 6 = 12\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n7.2 \\div 0.6 = 12 \\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073546 :question/problem "What is $86.5 \\operatorname{div}  0.5$?" "What is $86.5 \\div 0.5$?"] [:db/cas 17592186073546 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{86.5}{0.5}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$ so that both become whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{86.5\\times 10}{0.5\\times 10} = \\dfrac{865}{5}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $865\\operatorname{div}  5$. We will use long division:\n\n| $5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |\n| --- |\n\nCarrying out the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $1$$7$$3$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$3$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$5$$1$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$5$${0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, so the division is done.\n\nWe find that\n\n$$\n865 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 173\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n86.5 \\operatorname{div}  0.5 = 173 \\,\n$$" "First, we write the division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{86.5}{0.5}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$ so that both become whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{86.5\\times 10}{0.5\\times 10} = \\dfrac{865}{5}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $865\\div 5$. We will use long division:\n\n| $5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |\n| --- |\n\nCarrying out the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $1$$7$$3$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$3$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$5$$1$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$5$${0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, so the division is done.\n\nWe find that\n\n$$\n865 \\div 5 = 173\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n86.5 \\div 0.5 = 173 \\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073547 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9.45}{0.09}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply the numerator and denominator by a power of $10$ so that both become whole numbers. Here, there are $2$ decimal places, so we need to multiply by $100$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9.45\\times 100}{0.09\\times 100} = \\dfrac{945}{9}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $945\\operatorname{div}  9$. We will use long division:\n\n| $9$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |\n| --- |\n\nApplying the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $1$$0$$5$$9$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$0$$4$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$5$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, so the division is done.\n\nWe found that\n\n$$\n945 \\operatorname{div}  9 = 105\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n9.45 \\operatorname{div}  0.09 = 105 \\,\n$$" "First, we write the division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9.45}{0.09}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply the numerator and denominator by a power of $10$ so that both become whole numbers. Here, there are $2$ decimal places, so we need to multiply by $100$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9.45\\times 100}{0.09\\times 100} = \\dfrac{945}{9}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $945\\div 9$. We will use long division:\n\n| $9$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$ |\n| --- |\n\nApplying the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $1$$0$$5$$9$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$0$$4$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$5$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, so the division is done.\n\nWe found that\n\n$$\n945 \\div 9 = 105\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n9.45 \\div 0.09 = 105 \\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073548 :question/worked-solution "To determine the length of each piece, we need to divide $63$ by $4$.\n\nWe start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\, 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\,. \\,} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{blue}5} \\:$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$4$$2$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$3$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe went through all the digits and reached a remainder of $0$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n63 \\operatorname{div}  4= 15.75\n$$\n\nFinally, we round the quotient to the nearest tenth ($1$ decimal place). The hundredths place is $5$, which is greater than or equal to $5$, so we round up:\n\n$$\n15.75 \\rightarrow 15.8\n$$\n\nWe conclude that each piece would be about $15.8$ inches long." "To determine the length of each piece, we need to divide $63$ by $4$.\n\nWe start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\, 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\,. \\,} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{blue}5} \\:$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$4$$2$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$3$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe went through all the digits and reached a remainder of $0$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n63 \\div 4= 15.75\n$$\n\nFinally, we round the quotient to the nearest tenth ($1$ decimal place). The hundredths place is $5$, which is greater than or equal to $5$, so we round up:\n\n$$\n15.75 \\rightarrow 15.8\n$$\n\nWe conclude that each piece would be about $15.8$ inches long."] [:db/cas 17592186073549 :question/problem "Round the value of $137 \\operatorname{div}  8$ to $1$ decimal place." "Round the value of $137 \\div 8$ to $1$ decimal place."] [:db/cas 17592186073549 :question/worked-solution "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\,. \\,} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{black}2} \\: \\ldots$$8$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$5$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$8$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$6$$4$$\\vdots$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe could divide further, but since we want to round to $1$ decimal place, we stop after finding the quotient to $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n137 \\operatorname{div}  8 = 17.12\\ldots\n$$\n\nFinally, we round the quotient to the nearest tenth ($1$ decimal place). The hundredths place is $2$, which is less than $5$, so we round down:\n$17.12\\ldots \\rightarrow 17.1\\,$." "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\,. \\,} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{black}2} \\: \\ldots$$8$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$5$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$8$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$6$$4$$\\vdots$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe could divide further, but since we want to round to $1$ decimal place, we stop after finding the quotient to $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n137 \\div 8 = 17.12\\ldots\n$$\n\nFinally, we round the quotient to the nearest tenth ($1$ decimal place). The hundredths place is $2$, which is less than $5$, so we round down:\n$17.12\\ldots \\rightarrow 17.1\\,$."] [:db/cas 17592186073550 :question/problem "Round the value of $66 \\operatorname{div}  9$ to the nearest hundredth." "Round the value of $66 \\div 9$ to the nearest hundredth."] [:db/cas 17592186073550 :question/worked-solution "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\,. \\,} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{blue}3}\\: \\ldots$$9$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$3$$3$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$7$$3$$0$$-$$2$$7$$3$$0$$-\\!\\!$$2$$7$$3$$\\vdots$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe could divide further, but since we want to round to $2$ decimal places (the nearest hundredth), we stop after finding the quotient to $3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n66 \\operatorname{div}  9 = 7.333\\ldots\n$$\n\nFinally, we round the quotient to the nearest hundredth. The thousandths place is $3$, which is less than $5$, so we round down:\n$7.333\\ldots \\rightarrow 7.33\\,$." "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\,. \\,} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{blue}3}\\: \\ldots$$9$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$3$$3$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$7$$3$$0$$-$$2$$7$$3$$0$$-\\!\\!$$2$$7$$3$$\\vdots$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe could divide further, but since we want to round to $2$ decimal places (the nearest hundredth), we stop after finding the quotient to $3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n66 \\div 9 = 7.333\\ldots\n$$\n\nFinally, we round the quotient to the nearest hundredth. The thousandths place is $3$, which is less than $5$, so we round down:\n$7.333\\ldots \\rightarrow 7.33\\,$."] [:db/cas 17592186073551 :question/problem "What is $54.8 \\operatorname{div}  4$?" "What is $54.8 \\div 4$?"] [:db/cas 17592186073551 :question/worked-solution "We will use the standard algorithm.\n\nFirst, we write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 8}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\,\\, \\,\\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 8}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, we carry out the long division process as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,\\,. \\,} 7$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\,\\,. \\,} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$1$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$2$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n54.8 \\operatorname{div}  4 = 13.7 \\,\n$$" "We will use the standard algorithm.\n\nFirst, we write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 8}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\,\\, \\,\\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 8}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, we carry out the long division process as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,\\,. \\,} 7$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\,\\,. \\,} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$1$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$2$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n54.8 \\div 4 = 13.7 \\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073552 :question/worked-solution "To determine the amount of cocoa powder Rita used to make each serving, we need to divide $3.4$ by $4$. To do this, we can use the standard algorithm.\n\nFirst, we write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 4}{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\; \\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 4}{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, we carry out the long division process as usual:\n\n| $\\, 0 {\\,\\,. \\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}5$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{\\, 3 {\\,\\,. \\,} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}0}$$\\,-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$2$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3.4 \\operatorname{div}  4 = 0.85 \\,\n$$\n\nWe conclude that Rita used $0.85$ ounces of cocoa powder to make each serving of ice cream." "To determine the amount of cocoa powder Rita used to make each serving, we need to divide $3.4$ by $4$. To do this, we can use the standard algorithm.\n\nFirst, we write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 4}{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\; \\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 4}{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, we carry out the long division process as usual:\n\n| $\\, 0 {\\,\\,. \\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}5$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{\\, 3 {\\,\\,. \\,} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}0}$$\\,-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$2$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3.4 \\div 4 = 0.85 \\,\n$$\n\nWe conclude that Rita used $0.85$ ounces of cocoa powder to make each serving of ice cream."] [:db/cas 17592186073553 :question/problem "$0.0694 \\operatorname{div}  0.2=$" "$0.0694 \\div 0.2=$"] [:db/cas 17592186073553 :question/worked-solution "We can write this division problem as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.0694}{0.2}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.0694 \\times 10}{0.2\\times 10} = \\dfrac{0.694}{2}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the division problem is equivalent to $0.694 \\operatorname{div}  2$. We can compute this using long division as usual:\n\n| $0 {\\,. \\,} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7$$2$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{0 {\\,. \\,} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$9$$-\\!\\!\\!$$8$$1$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we find that\n\n$$\n0.694 \\operatorname{div}  2 = 0.347\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n0.0694 \\operatorname{div}  0.2 = 0.347 \\,\n$$" "We can write this division problem as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.0694}{0.2}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.0694 \\times 10}{0.2\\times 10} = \\dfrac{0.694}{2}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the division problem is equivalent to $0.694 \\div 2$. We can compute this using long division as usual:\n\n| $0 {\\,. \\,} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7$$2$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{0 {\\,. \\,} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$9$$-\\!\\!\\!$$8$$1$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we find that\n\n$$\n0.694 \\div 2 = 0.347\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n0.0694 \\div 0.2 = 0.347 \\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073554 :question/problem "What is $0.471 \\operatorname{div}  0.02$?" "What is $0.471 \\div 0.02$?"] [:db/cas 17592186073554 :question/worked-solution "We can write this division problem as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.471}{0.02}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $100$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.471 \\times 100}{0.02\\times 100} = \\dfrac{47.1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the division problem is equivalent to $47.1 \\operatorname{div}  2$. We can compute this using the standard algorithm:\n\n| $\\,2\\,\\,$$\\,3\\,$.$\\,5\\,\\,$$\\,5\\,\\,$$2$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}\\,} \\!\\!\\!.\\, \\!1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we find that\n\n$$\n47.1 \\operatorname{div}  2 = 23.55\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n0.471 \\operatorname{div}  0.02 = 23.55 \\,\n$$" "We can write this division problem as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.471}{0.02}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $100$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.471 \\times 100}{0.02\\times 100} = \\dfrac{47.1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the division problem is equivalent to $47.1 \\div 2$. We can compute this using the standard algorithm:\n\n| $\\,2\\,\\,$$\\,3\\,$.$\\,5\\,\\,$$\\,5\\,\\,$$2$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}\\,} \\!\\!\\!.\\, \\!1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we find that\n\n$$\n47.1 \\div 2 = 23.55\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n0.471 \\div 0.02 = 23.55 \\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073557 :question/worked-solution "The number of instructors relative to students for School A is the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{24}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $8{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8 \\operatorname{div}  8}{24 \\operatorname{div}  8} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThe number of instructors relative to students for School B is the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{27}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $8{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18 \\operatorname{div}  9}{27 \\operatorname{div}  9} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo compare\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nwe look at the numerators since the denominators are the same.\n\nSince $1 < 2$, the fraction for School A is *smaller.*\n\nThe complete statements are as follows:\n\n$\\quad$ The number of instructors relative to students for School A is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gray]{\\dfrac{1}{3}}\n$$\n\n$\\quad$ The number of instructors relative to students for School B is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, School A has a $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gray]{\\textrm{smaller}}$ fraction of instructors relative to students compared to School B." "The number of instructors relative to students for School A is the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{24}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $8{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8 \\div 8}{24 \\div 8} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThe number of instructors relative to students for School B is the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{27}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $8{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18 \\div 9}{27 \\div 9} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo compare\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nwe look at the numerators since the denominators are the same.\n\nSince $1 < 2$, the fraction for School A is *smaller.*\n\nThe complete statements are as follows:\n\n$\\quad$ The number of instructors relative to students for School A is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gray]{\\dfrac{1}{3}}\n$$\n\n$\\quad$ The number of instructors relative to students for School B is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\n$\\quad$ Therefore, School A has a $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gray]{\\textrm{smaller}}$ fraction of instructors relative to students compared to School B."] [:db/cas 17592186073560 :question/worked-solution "The number of laptops relative to students for Classroom A is the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{32}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $8{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8 \\operatorname{div}  8}{32 \\operatorname{div}  8} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThe number of laptops relative to students for Classroom B is the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{18}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6 \\operatorname{div}  6}{18 \\operatorname{div}  6} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTo compare\n