of minutes per pool, we divide the number of minutes, $15,$ by the total pool volume emptied, $\\dfrac{3}{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n15 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15÷\\frac{3}{2} & =15\\times \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{1}\\times \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\times 2}{1\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{3} \\\\  & =30÷3 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $10$ minutes per pool. To find the number of minutes that the pump takes to empty $5$ pools, we multiply the unit rate by $5$ pools and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\times 5 & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the pump will take $50$ minutes to empty $5$ pools." "To find the number of minutes that the pump takes to empty $5$ pools, we can first find the unit rate of minutes per pool, and then multiply the unit rate by $5$ pools.\n\nTo find the unit rate of minutes per pool, we divide the number of minutes, $15,$ by the total pool volume emptied, $\\dfrac{3}{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n15 \\div \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15÷\\frac{3}{2} & =15\\times \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{1}\\times \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\times 2}{1\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{3} \\\\  & =30÷3 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $10$ minutes per pool. To find the number of minutes that the pump takes to empty $5$ pools, we multiply the unit rate by $5$ pools and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\times 5 & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the pump will take $50$ minutes to empty $5$ pools."] [:db/cas 17592186073637 :question/worked-solution "To find the number of dollars that Ava earns if she sells $18$ liters of coffee, we can first find the unit rate of dollars per liter, and then multiply the unit rate by $18$ liters.\n\nTo find the unit rate of dollars per liter, we divide the number of dollars, $\\dfrac{3}{4},$ by the number of liters, $\\dfrac{3}{8},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nDividing one fraction by another is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{3}{8} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\times \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 8}{4\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{12} \\\\  & =24÷12 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $2$ dollars per liter. To find the number of dollars that Ava earns selling $18$ liters, we multiply the unit rate by $18$ liters and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\times 18 & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Ava earns $36$ dollars if she sells $18$ liters of coffee." "To find the number of dollars that Ava earns if she sells $18$ liters of coffee, we can first find the unit rate of dollars per liter, and then multiply the unit rate by $18$ liters.\n\nTo find the unit rate of dollars per liter, we divide the number of dollars, $\\dfrac{3}{4},$ by the number of liters, $\\dfrac{3}{8},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} \\div \\dfrac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nDividing one fraction by another is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{3}{8} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\times \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 8}{4\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{12} \\\\  & =24÷12 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $2$ dollars per liter. To find the number of dollars that Ava earns selling $18$ liters, we multiply the unit rate by $18$ liters and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\times 18 & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Ava earns $36$ dollars if she sells $18$ liters of coffee."] [:db/cas 17592186073638 :question/problem "Calculate the quotient of $681\\operatorname{div}  32$ using the relationship between multiplication and division." "Calculate the quotient of $681\\div 32$ using the relationship between multiplication and division."] [:db/cas 175921