 number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4,236 \\operatorname{div}  3 = 1,412\n$$" "We can also use the standard algorithm to divide a four-digit number by a one-digit number.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use the algorithm to divide $4,236$ by $3$. We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the thousands:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}2$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}4} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over thousands: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 3$Subtract: ${\\color{red}4} - 3 = 1$Bring down: $2$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the hundreds:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}4$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}3$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}12} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}4}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}4$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 12$Subtract: ${\\color{red}12} - 12 = 0$Bring down: $3$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $1$$4$$\\color{blue}1$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{red}3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Divide: ${\\