ata-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-\\!$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe result of the subtraction is ${\\color{red}50}$. This indicates that if we take $25$ a total of ${\\color{blue}10}$ times, then it will fit into $300$ with $\\color{red}50$ left over.\n\nNow, we repeat the process with a dividend of $\\color{red}50$ instead of $300$. To start the next round, we write $\\color{red}50$ in a new box to the right.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAgain, we are looking for a multiple of $25$ that is easy to compute and is no larger than ${\\color{red}50}$. We will choose the multiple $25 \\times {\\color{blue}2}$ because we can compute\n\n$$\n25 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 50\n$$\n\nquickly and the result is no larger than ${\\color{red}50}$.\n\nAs before, we write $\\color{blue}2$ above the box and subtract $25 \\times {\\color{blue}2}$ from ${\\color{red}50}$ inside the box.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-\\!$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\fbox{0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe get $\\fbox{0}$, so the division is done.\n\nTo find the quotient $300 \\div 25$, all we need to do is add the numbers on top of the boxes:\n\n$$\n10+2=12\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n300 \\div 25 = 12\\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074957 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll consider real-life situations where we have to multiply fractions by whole numbers.\n\nSuppose that Justin, a chef, has $8$ large tins of tomatoes. If each tin of tomatoes weighs $\\dfrac{5}{4}\\,\\textrm{lb},$ how many pounds of tomatoes does Justin have in total?\n\nTo find out the total weight of tomatoes, we need to multiply $\\dfrac{5}{4}\\,\\textrm{lb}$ by $8.$  \n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n8 \\times \\dfrac54= \\dfrac{8\\times 5}{4} = \\dfrac{40}4\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by interpreting the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{4} =  40 \\operatorname{div}  4 = 10\n$$\n\nSo, in total, Justin has $10\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of tomatoes." "In this lesson, we'll consider real-life situations where we have to multiply fractions by whole numbers.\n\nSuppose that Justin, a chef, has $8$ large tins of tomatoes. If each tin of tomatoes weighs $\\dfrac{5}{4}\\,\\textrm{lb},$ how many pounds of tomatoes does Justin have in total?\n\nTo find out the total weight of tomatoes, we need to multiply $\\dfrac{5}{4}\\,\\textrm{lb}$ by $8.$  \n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n8 \\times \\dfrac54= \\dfrac{8\\times 5}{4} = \\dfrac{40}4\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by interpreting the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{4} =  40 \\div 4 = 10\n$$\n\nSo, in total, Justin has $10\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of tomatoes."] [:db/cas 17592186074962 :tutorial/content "There are many ways to divide decimals. One such way is to write the division as a fraction and then multiply the numerator and denominator by a power of $10$ so that both become whole numbers. Then, we carry out the division as usual.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use this method to find the value of $4.5\\operatorname{div}  0.5$.\n\n**Step 1.** First, we write the division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4.5}{0.5}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Then, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4.5\\times 10}{0.5\\times 10} = \\dfrac{45}{5}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Finally, we solve the resulting division problem. In this case, we have\n\n$$\n45 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 9\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4.5 \\operatorname{div}  0.5 = 9 \\,\n$$" "There are many ways to divide decimals. One such way is to write the division as a fraction and then multiply the numerator and denominator by a power of $10$ so that both become whole numbers. Then, we carry out the division as usual.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use this method to find the value of $4.5\\div 0.5$.\n\n**Step 1.** First, we write the division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4.5}{0.5}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Then, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4.5\\times 10}{0.5\\times 10} = \\dfrac{45}{5}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Finally, we solve the resulting division problem. In this case, we have\n\n$$\n45 \\div 5 = 9\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4.5 \\div 0.5 = 9 \\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074963 :tutorial/content "When using long division to divide whole numbers, it's often appropriate to round the final answer.\n\nFor instance, let's find the value of $58 \\operatorname{div}  7$, rounded to the nearest tenth. We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\,. \\,} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{black}8} \\: \\ldots$$7$$\\phantom{\\mid}\\!\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$6$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$4$$\\vdots$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe could divide further, but since we want to round to $1$ decimal place (the nearest tenth), we stop after finding the quotient to $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n58 \\operatorname{div}  7 = 8.28 \\ldots\n$$\n\nFinally, we round the quotient to the nearest tenth. The hundredths place is $8$, which is greater than $5$, so we round up:\n$8.28 \\ldots \\rightarrow 8.3$" "When using long division to divide whole numbers, it's often appropriate to round the final answer.\n\nFor instance, let's find the value of $58 \\div 7$, rounded to the nearest tenth. We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\,. \\,} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{black}8} \\: \\ldots$$7$$\\phantom{\\mid}\\!\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$6$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$4$$\\vdots$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe could divide further, but since we want to round to $1$ decimal place (the nearest tenth), we stop after finding the quotient to $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n58 \\div 7 = 8.28 \\ldots\n$$\n\nFinally, we round the quotient to the nearest tenth. The hundredths place is $8$, which is greater than $5$, so we round up:\n$8.28 \\ldots \\rightarrow 8.3$"] [:db/cas 17592186074964 :tutorial/content "We can use the standard algorithm to divide decimals by whole numbers.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute $7.2 \\operatorname{div}  3$.\n\nFirst, we write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $3$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2}{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\,\\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$3$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.} 2{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, we carry out the long division process as usual:\n\n| $\\,2 {\\,\\,. \\,} 4$$3$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\,\\,. \\,} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7.2 \\operatorname{div}  3 = 2.4 \\,\n$$\n\n**Note:** The \"standard algorithm\" is the same as \"long division.\" The two phrases can be used interchangeably." "We can use the standard algorithm to divide decimals by whole numbers.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute $7.2 \\div 3$.\n\nFirst, we write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $3$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2}{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\,\\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$3$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.} 2{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, we carry out the long division process as usual:\n\n| $\\,2 {\\,\\,. \\,} 4$$3$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\,\\,. \\,} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7.2 \\div 3 = 2.4 \\,\n$$\n\n**Note:** The \"standard algorithm\" is the same as \"long division.\" The two phrases can be used interchangeably."] [:db/cas 17592186074965 :tutorial/content "We can also use the standard algorithm to divide decimals by other decimals. The only difference is that we first need to find an equivalent division problem where the divisor is a whole number.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute $1.11\\operatorname{div}  0.3 \\,$. First, we write this division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1.11}{0.3}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$ so that we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1.11 \\times 10}{0.3\\times 10} = \\dfrac{11.1}{3}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the division problem is equivalent to $11.1 \\operatorname{div}  3$. We can compute this using long division as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,\\,. \\,} 7$$3$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\,\\,. \\,} 1}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$2$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$1$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we find that\n\n$$\n11.1 \\operatorname{div}  3 = 3.7\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n1.11 \\operatorname{div}  0.3 = 3.7 \\,\n$$" "We can also use the standard algorithm to divide decimals by other decimals. The only difference is that we first need to find an equivalent division problem where the divisor is a whole number.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute $1.11\\div 0.3 \\,$. First, we write this division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1.11}{0.3}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$ so that we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1.11 \\times 10}{0.3\\times 10} = \\dfrac{11.1}{3}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the division problem is equivalent to $11.1 \\div 3$. We can compute this using long division as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,\\,. \\,} 7$$3$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\,\\,. \\,} 1}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$2$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$1$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we find that\n\n$$\n11.1 \\div 3 = 3.7\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n1.11 \\div 0.3 = 3.7 \\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074970 :tutorial/content "A **unit rate** is a comparison of two quantities where one of the terms has a quantity of $1.$ This means the ratio, in fraction form, has a denominator of $1.$\n\nFor instance, suppose Michael earned $\\$ 200$ by selling $4$ identical tires.\n\nThe ratio of dollars to tires is ${\\color{blue}200}:{\\color{red}4}.$ Writing the ratio as a fraction, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200}}{{\\color{red}4}} \\, .\n$$\n\nThe unit rate is how many dollars Michael got for $1$ tire.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200} \\operatorname{div}  4}{{\\color{red}4} \\operatorname{div}  4} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}50}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nThis is the unit rate. In this case, we say that Michael got $\\color{blue}50$ dollars *per* tire." "A **unit rate** is a comparison of two quantities where one of the terms has a quantity of $1.$ This means the ratio, in fraction form, has a denominator of $1.$\n\nFor instance, suppose Michael earned $\\$ 200$ by selling $4$ identical tires.\n\nThe ratio of dollars to tires is ${\\color{blue}200}:{\\color{red}4}.$ Writing the ratio as a fraction, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200}}{{\\color{red}4}} \\, .\n$$\n\nThe unit rate is how many dollars Michael got for $1$ tire.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200} \\div 4}{{\\color{red}4} \\div 4} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}50}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nThis is the unit rate. In this case, we say that Michael got $\\color{blue}50$ dollars *per* tire."] [:db/cas 17592186074971 :tutorial/content "In some tape diagrams, each box can represent more than one unit.\n\nFor instance, the ratio of pins to paper clips in a case is given by the tape diagram below. If there are $15$ pins in the case, how many paper clips are there in total?\n\n![](images/82/82f94d7377ea3923ffdd825de324b128cd8b4f102070bf6890dee2cbc619657e.png)\n\nWe are told that there are $15$ pins in the case, but the tape diagram for pins shows $3$ boxes. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents $15 \\operatorname{div}  3 = 5$ units. \n\nNow, we fill the number $5$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](images/7d/7d8d1e22ee067588593b7b08442dd2a44142f9f684d84809056ee0ffb4b2dfc0.png)\n\nSince there are $4$ boxes corresponding to paper clips, and each box represents $5$ units, the total number of paper clips is\n\n$$\n4 \\times 5 = 20 \\, .\n$$\n\nSo, there are $20$ paper clips in Mrs. Robinson's case." "In some tape diagrams, each box can represent more than one unit.\n\nFor instance, the ratio of pins to paper clips in a case is given by the tape diagram below. If there are $15$ pins in the case, how many paper clips are there in total?\n\n![](images/82/82f94d7377ea3923ffdd825de324b128cd8b4f102070bf6890dee2cbc619657e.png)\n\nWe are told that there are $15$ pins in the case, but the tape diagram for pins shows $3$ boxes. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents $15 \\div 3 = 5$ units. \n\nNow, we fill the number $5$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](images/7d/7d8d1e22ee067588593b7b08442dd2a44142f9f684d84809056ee0ffb4b2dfc0.png)\n\nSince there are $4$ boxes corresponding to paper clips, and each box represents $5$ units, the total number of paper clips is\n\n$$\n4 \\times 5 = 20 \\, .\n$$\n\nSo, there are $20$ paper clips in Mrs. Robinson's case."] [:db/cas 17592186075004 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to divide larger numbers by two-digit numbers *with remainders* using the relationship between multiplication and division.\n\nTo begin with, let's figure out how to compute $47\\operatorname{div}  13$.\n\nWe will start by computing products of $13$ and other whole numbers, starting from $1$, until we get to a number that is greater than $47$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n13 \\cdot 1 &= 13 \\\\\n13 \\cdot 2 &= 13 + 13 = 26 \\\\\n13 \\cdot 3 &= 26 + 13 = 39✓ \\\\\n13 \\cdot 4 &= 39 + 13 = 52\\cdot(> {47}, so too big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $13 \\times {\\color{blue}3}$ is *smaller than* $47$, but $13 \\times 4$ is *greater than* $47$. This means that ${47}\\operatorname{div}  {13}$ has a whole number part (or quotient) of ${\\color{blue}3}$.\n\nTo find the remainder, we subtract the product from the dividend:\n\n$$\n{47} - {39} = {\\color{red}8}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{47}\\operatorname{div}  {13} = {\\color{blue}3}\\,\\textrm{R}\\,{\\color{red}8}\n$$" "In this lesson, we will learn how to divide larger numbers by two-digit numbers *with remainders* using the relationship between multiplication and division.\n\nTo begin with, let's figure out how to compute $47\\div 13$.\n\nWe will start by computing products of $13$ and other whole numbers, starting from $1$, until we get to a number that is greater than $47$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n13 \\cdot 1 &= 13 \\\\\n13 \\cdot 2 &= 13 + 13 = 26 \\\\\n13 \\cdot 3 &= 26 + 13 = 39✓ \\\\\n13 \\cdot 4 &= 39 + 13 = 52\\cdot(> {47}, so too big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $13 \\times {\\color{blue}3}$ is *smaller than* $47$, but $13 \\times 4$ is *greater than* $47$. This means that ${47}\\div {13}$ has a whole number part (or quotient) of ${\\color{blue}3}$.\n\nTo find the remainder, we subtract the product from the dividend:\n\n$$\n{47} - {39} = {\\color{red}8}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{47}\\div {13} = {\\color{blue}3}\\,\\textrm{R}\\,{\\color{red}8}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186075018 :tutorial/content "We can use area models to understand what it means to divide a fraction by a whole number.\n\nFor example, let's consider the area model below:\n\n![](images/66/664911a4bae7edacdf2b68f8fddb7dd54af3da02ab995c003e9001cd8b27aaff.png)\n\nThe area of a rectangle equals its length multiplied by its width.\n\nFor the given rectangle:\n\n- its length is $3$\n- its width is $\\dfrac{1}{12}$\n- its area is $\\dfrac{1}{4}$\n\nTherefore, this area model shows the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n3\\times \\dfrac{1}{12} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can turn this multiplication problem into the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{12} = \\dfrac14\\operatorname{div}  3\n$$\n\nWe can also use area models to interpret the division of a whole number by a fraction. Let's see an example." "We can use area models to understand what it means to divide a fraction by a whole number.\n\nFor example, let's consider the area model below:\n\n![](images/66/664911a4bae7edacdf2b68f8fddb7dd54af3da02ab995c003e9001cd8b27aaff.png)\n\nThe area of a rectangle equals its length multiplied by its width.\n\nFor the given rectangle:\n\n- its length is $3$\n- its width is $\\dfrac{1}{12}$\n- its area is $\\dfrac{1}{4}$\n\nTherefore, this area model shows the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n3\\times \\dfrac{1}{12} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can turn this multiplication problem into the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{12} = \\dfrac14\\div 3\n$$\n\nWe can also use area models to interpret the division of a whole number by a fraction. Let's see an example."] [:db/cas 17592186075044 :tutorial/content "The model below represents $2$ wholes. Let's use it to calculate the value of $2 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{5}.$\n\n![](images/bc/bceafee7d9ca5e92e69b5d33d9504f4290526b80059ad62ed26229d02712a278.png)\n\n \n\nTo calculate ${\\color{red}2} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}$, we need to divide each of $\\color{red}2$ wholes into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cf/cf608ce8f5d8e2a47a4bebe21f198d193b10dd8f6e95436986bc954c89c60d19.png)\n\n \n\nWe get ${\\color{red}2} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 10$ pieces in total. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{5} = 10\\,.\n$$" "The model below represents $2$ wholes. Let's use it to calculate the value of $2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5}.$\n\n![](images/bc/bceafee7d9ca5e92e69b5d33d9504f4290526b80059ad62ed26229d02712a278.png)\n\n \n\nTo calculate ${\\color{red}2} \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}$, we need to divide each of $\\color{red}2$ wholes into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cf/cf608ce8f5d8e2a47a4bebe21f198d193b10dd8f6e95436986bc954c89c60d19.png)\n\n \n\nWe get ${\\color{red}2} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 10$ pieces in total. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5} = 10\\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186075045 :tutorial/content "To divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by the whole number.\n\nFor example, to calculate $\\dfrac{1}{5} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}2},$ we multiply the fraction's denominator by ${\\color{blue}2}\\!:$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}2}  = \\dfrac{1}{5 \\times {\\color{blue}2}} = \\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nNotice that we get the same answer using a fraction model, and the process is similar.\n\n![](images/3f/3f1f0dddd115c0d820de4d79624bd87492e215a6c2de43071fda73a4a0684d77.png)" "To divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by the whole number.\n\nFor example, to calculate $\\dfrac{1}{5} \\div {\\color{blue}2},$ we multiply the fraction's denominator by ${\\color{blue}2}\\!:$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5} \\div {\\color{blue}2}  = \\dfrac{1}{5 \\times {\\color{blue}2}} = \\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nNotice that we get the same answer using a fraction model, and the process is similar.\n\n![](images/3f/3f1f0dddd115c0d820de4d79624bd87492e215a6c2de43071fda73a4a0684d77.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186075046 :tutorial/content "To divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we can multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator.\n\nSo, to divide $2 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5},$ we multiply the $2$ by ${\\color{blue}5}\\!:$\n\n$$\n2 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5} = 2 \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 10\n$$\n\nAnd that's the answer! Notice that this method does the same job as the fraction model method we used before:\n\n![](images/5f/5f2eb6ab39ea67466d7a42a8809b8a039a84f9a20a13cca3659d81f669a772a5.png)" "To divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we can multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator.\n\nSo, to divide $2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5},$ we multiply the $2$ by ${\\color{blue}5}\\!:$\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5} = 2 \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 10\n$$\n\nAnd that's the answer! Notice that this method does the same job as the fraction model method we used before:\n\n![](images/5f/5f2eb6ab39ea67466d7a42a8809b8a039a84f9a20a13cca3659d81f669a772a5.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186075048 :tutorial/content "We can estimate decimal division by rounding each decimal to the nearest whole number and dividing the resulting numbers.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to estimate the quotient of the following division problem.\n$19.7 \\operatorname{div}  5.3$\n\nOne way to proceed is first to round both quantities to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}19.7 & ÷ & 5.3 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{20}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{5}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, we can form an estimate of $19.7 \\operatorname{div}  5.3$ by evaluating $20 \\operatorname{div}  5$.\n\nComputing the quotient of this simplified problem gives\n\n$$\n20 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $19.7 \\operatorname{div}  5.3$ is approximately equal to $4$. We can write this symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n19.7 \\operatorname{div}  5.3 \\approx 4\n$$\n\nThe symbol\n\n$$\n\\approx\n$$\n\nmeans \"is approximately equal to.\"" "We can estimate decimal division by rounding each decimal to the nearest whole number and dividing the resulting numbers.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to estimate the quotient of the following division problem.\n$19.7 \\div 5.3$\n\nOne way to proceed is first to round both quantities to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}19.7 & ÷ & 5.3 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{20}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{5}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, we can form an estimate of $19.7 \\div 5.3$ by evaluating $20 \\div 5$.\n\nComputing the quotient of this simplified problem gives\n\n$$\n20 \\div 5 = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $19.7 \\div 5.3$ is approximately equal to $4$. We can write this symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n19.7 \\div 5.3 \\approx 4\n$$\n\nThe symbol\n\n$$\n\\approx\n$$\n\nmeans \"is approximately equal to.\""] [:db/cas 17592186075052 :tutorial/content "Remember that a *unit* fraction is a fraction containing $\\color{blue}1$ in the numerator. So, for example, the fractions \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}2, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{12}\n$$\nare both unit fractions, whereas \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}{2}}9, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}{3}}{5}\n$$\nare *not* unit fractions.\n\nIn this lesson, we will use models to divide unit fractions by whole numbers. \n\nLet's use a fraction model to find the value of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\operatorname{div}  2.\n$$\nWe start with a square model of $\\dfrac12,$ split *vertically*.\n\n![](images/cc/ccfe0f457342c456200c98cfc46e63bdc1153698282085164eeafae5c2092c45.png)\n\nTo divide $\\dfrac12$ by ${\\color{blue}2},$ we split the above whole into $\\color{blue}2$ equal parts *horizontally*. Then, we remove any shaded parts that are not in the bottom row.\n\n![](images/79/7940073ba05d3136ee1d465530a60bc3624b7aa06fd7484d30797758a28976d7.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac14$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\operatorname{div}  2 = \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$" "Remember that a *unit* fraction is a fraction containing $\\color{blue}1$ in the numerator. So, for example, the fractions \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}2, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{12}\n$$\nare both unit fractions, whereas \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}{2}}9, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}{3}}{5}\n$$\nare *not* unit fractions.\n\nIn this lesson, we will use models to divide unit fractions by whole numbers. \n\nLet's use a fraction model to find the value of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\div 2.\n$$\nWe start with a square model of $\\dfrac12,$ split *vertically*.\n\n![](images/cc/ccfe0f457342c456200c98cfc46e63bdc1153698282085164eeafae5c2092c45.png)\n\nTo divide $\\dfrac12$ by ${\\color{blue}2},$ we split the above whole into $\\color{blue}2$ equal parts *horizontally*. Then, we remove any shaded parts that are not in the bottom row.\n\n![](images/79/7940073ba05d3136ee1d465530a60bc3624b7aa06fd7484d30797758a28976d7.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac14$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\div 2 = \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186075058 :tutorial/content "How do we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{\\color{blue}4} + 5\n$$\n\nas a mixed number?\n\nNotice that we cannot write\n\n$$\n5\\,\\dfrac 7 4\n$$\n\nbecause\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 7 4\n$$\n\nis an improper fraction. Instead, let's find this sum using the same method as before.\n\nFirst, we write $5$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}4$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{4}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5 \\times {\\color{blue}{4}}}{1 \\times{\\color{blue}{4}}} = \\dfrac{20}{4}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find the following sum:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{4} + \\dfrac{20}{4}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{4} + \\frac{20}{4} = \\frac{7 + 20}{4} = \\frac{27}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert this to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n27 \\operatorname{div}  4 = 6 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 3 = 6 \\, \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$" "How do we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{\\color{blue}4} + 5\n$$\n\nas a mixed number?\n\nNotice that we cannot write\n\n$$\n5\\,\\dfrac 7 4\n$$\n\nbecause\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 7 4\n$$\n\nis an improper fraction. Instead, let's find this sum using the same method as before.\n\nFirst, we write $5$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}4$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{4}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5 \\times {\\color{blue}{4}}}{1 \\times{\\color{blue}{4}}} = \\dfrac{20}{4}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find the following sum:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{4} + \\dfrac{20}{4}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{4} + \\frac{20}{4} = \\frac{7 + 20}{4} = \\frac{27}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert this to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n27 \\div 4 = 6 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 3 = 6 \\, \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078288 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the terms of the corresponding sequence\n\n$$\n4, \\quad 8, \\quad 12, \\quad , \\ldots, \\quad 56, \\quad 60.\n$$\n\n are multiples of $4,$ and since $60\\operatorname{div}  4 = 15,$ there are $15$ terms in total. \n\nTherefore, the $n$th term of the sequence has the form\n\n$$\na_n =4n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ ranges from $n=1$ to $n=15\\mathbin{.}$ Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =4(1)=4 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =4(2)=8 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =4(3)=12 \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮ \\\\ {a}_{14} & =4(14)=56 \\\\ {a}_{15} & =4(15)=60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS=4+8+12+\\cdots+56+60=\\sum_{n=1}^{15} 4n.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the terms of the corresponding sequence\n\n$$\n4, \\quad 8, \\quad 12, \\quad , \\ldots, \\quad 56, \\quad 60.\n$$\n\n are multiples of $4,$ and since $60\\div 4 = 15,$ there are $15$ terms in total. \n\nTherefore, the $n$th term of the sequence has the form\n\n$$\na_n =4n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ ranges from $n=1$ to $n=15\\mathbin{.}$ Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =4(1)=4 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =4(2)=8 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =4(3)=12 \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮ \\\\ {a}_{14} & =4(14)=56 \\\\ {a}_{15} & =4(15)=60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS=4+8+12+\\cdots+56+60=\\sum_{n=1}^{15} 4n.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078318 :question/problem "Find the value of the expression $\\sqrt{ (3^4+7^2-2) \\operatorname{div}  2}.$" "Find the value of the expression $\\sqrt{ (3^4+7^2-2) \\div 2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078319 :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\sqrt{((5^2-4) \\operatorname{div}  3)-2}.$" "Simplify the expression $\\sqrt{((5^2-4) \\div 3)-2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078910 :question/problem "Find the value of $(\\sqrt{81} + 7) \\operatorname{div}  (2 - 6)$." "Find the value of $(\\sqrt{81} + 7) \\div (2 - 6)$."] [:db/cas 17592186079184 :question/problem "What is the value of $\\sqrt{(24 \\operatorname{div}  2) + 12} - 3$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "What is the value of $\\sqrt{(24 \\div 2) + 12} - 3$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186079665 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n-3\\sqrt{\\left(2^4-1\\right) \\operatorname{div}  3}\\,.\n$$\n\nThe first thing to realize is that there is an invisible multiplication symbol here. The above expression is short for\n\n$$\n-3\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}\\,\\sqrt{\\left(2^4-1\\right) \\operatorname{div}  3}\\,.\n$$\n\nWhen multiplying a radical by a number, we usually omit the additional multiplication symbol.\n\nNow, to simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS.  In terms of the order of operations:\n\n- Expressions underneath radicals are treated as if they are contained within parentheses (the **P** in **P**EMDAS), so we always deal with this first.\n\n- Radicals themselves are treated as exponents (the **E** in P**E**MDAS).\n\nTherefore, following PEMDAS, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\sqrt{({2}^{4}-1)÷3} &= -3\\sqrt{(16-1)÷3} \\\\ &= -3\\sqrt{15÷3} \\\\ &= -3\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's our answer! \n\nTo get a numerical approximation, we need to use a calculator. In this case, we get\n\n$$\n-3 \\sqrt{5} = -3\\times \\sqrt{5} \\approx -6.708\\,204\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places." "Suppose we want to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n-3\\sqrt{\\left(2^4-1\\right) \\div 3}\\,.\n$$\n\nThe first thing to realize is that there is an invisible multiplication symbol here. The above expression is short for\n\n$$\n-3\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}\\,\\sqrt{\\left(2^4-1\\right) \\div 3}\\,.\n$$\n\nWhen multiplying a radical by a number, we usually omit the additional multiplication symbol.\n\nNow, to simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS.  In terms of the order of operations:\n\n- Expressions underneath radicals are treated as if they are contained within parentheses (the **P** in **P**EMDAS), so we always deal with this first.\n\n- Radicals themselves are treated as exponents (the **E** in P**E**MDAS).\n\nTherefore, following PEMDAS, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\sqrt{({2}^{4}-1)÷3} &= -3\\sqrt{(16-1)÷3} \\\\ &= -3\\sqrt{15÷3} \\\\ &= -3\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's our answer! \n\nTo get a numerical approximation, we need to use a calculator. In this case, we get\n\n$$\n-3 \\sqrt{5} = -3\\times \\sqrt{5} \\approx -6.708\\,204\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186079667 :tutorial/content "Suppose we need to evaluate an expression like\n\n$$\n1+\\sqrt{4},\n$$\n\nwhere we have a square root sign. What would be the order of operations? It turns out that the root signs ($\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$ or $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}$) can be thought of as parentheses. \nIn other words, we find the value under the square root first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\sqrt{4} &= 1+2 \\\\ &= 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSimilarly, let's evaluate $\\big(1-\\sqrt[3]{-27}\\big) \\operatorname{div}  2$.\n\nTo evaluate expressions with radicals, we use PEMDAS as before, but with the following additional rules:\n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nFollowing the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-\\sqrt[3]{-27})÷2 &= (1-\\sqrt[3]{(-3)^{3}})÷2 \\\\ &= (1-(-3))÷2 \\\\ &= 4÷2 \\\\ &= 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose we need to evaluate an expression like\n\n$$\n1+\\sqrt{4},\n$$\n\nwhere we have a square root sign. What would be the order of operations? It turns out that the root signs ($\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$ or $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}$) can be thought of as parentheses. \nIn other words, we find the value under the square root first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\sqrt{4} &= 1+2 \\\\ &= 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSimilarly, let's evaluate $\\big(1-\\sqrt[3]{-27}\\big) \\div 2$.\n\nTo evaluate expressions with radicals, we use PEMDAS as before, but with the following additional rules:\n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nFollowing the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-\\sqrt[3]{-27})÷2 &= (1-\\sqrt[3]{(-3)^{3}})÷2 \\\\ &= (1-(-3))÷2 \\\\ &= 4÷2 \\\\ &= 2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186079710 :tutorial/content "We know how to evaluate expressions like $2^3, 2^2, 2^1$, and even $2^0.$ But what if the power is negative? How would we evaluate $2^{-1},$ for example?\n\nTo help us with that, let's go back to the results table for powers of $2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4} & =16 \\\\ {2}^{3} & =8 \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {2}^{1} & =2 \\\\ {2}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {2}^{-1} & =◻\\,?\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by $2$ every time. If we follow the same pattern, we find that \n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{-1}} = {\\color{blue}{1}}\\operatorname{div}  2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is just the reciprocal of $2.$ So, raising a number to the power of $-1$ is the *same* as finding its reciprocal!" "We know how to evaluate expressions like $2^3, 2^2, 2^1$, and even $2^0.$ But what if the power is negative? How would we evaluate $2^{-1},$ for example?\n\nTo help us with that, let's go back to the results table for powers of $2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4} & =16 \\\\ {2}^{3} & =8 \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {2}^{1} & =2 \\\\ {2}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {2}^{-1} & =◻\\,?\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by $2$ every time. If we follow the same pattern, we find that \n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{-1}} = {\\color{blue}{1}}\\div 2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is just the reciprocal of $2.$ So, raising a number to the power of $-1$ is the *same* as finding its reciprocal!"] [:db/cas 17592186082908 :question/worked-solution "The limit comparison test states that if $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = L\n$$\n\nwhere $L$ is positive and finite, then the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\neither *both* converge or *both* diverge.\n\nThe given series looks as though it will behave like the convergent series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{2n^2} = \\dfrac 1 2\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nSo, we let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{2n^2+n}, \\qquad b_n = \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{a_{n}}{b_{n}} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{1}{2n^{2} + n} \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{1}{n^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{n^{2}}{2n^{2} + n}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(1)/((2 + \\frac{1}{n})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2 + 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nis positive and finite, we can use the limit comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\nconverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nconverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{2n^2+n}\n$$\n\nconverges." "The limit comparison test states that if $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = L\n$$\n\nwhere $L$ is positive and finite, then the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\neither *both* converge or *both* diverge.\n\nThe given series looks as though it will behave like the convergent series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{2n^2} = \\dfrac 1 2\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nSo, we let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{2n^2+n}, \\qquad b_n = \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{a_{n}}{b_{n}} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{1}{2n^{2} + n} \\div \\frac{1}{n^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{n^{2}}{2n^{2} + n}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(1)/((2 + \\frac{1}{n})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2 + 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nis positive and finite, we can use the limit comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\nconverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nconverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{2n^2+n}\n$$\n\nconverges."] [:db/cas 17592186082909 :question/worked-solution "The limit comparison test states that if $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = L\n$$\n\nwhere $L$ is positive and finite, then the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\neither *both* converge or *both* diverge.\n\nThe given series looks as though it will behave like the divergent series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2n^3}{4n^4} = \\dfrac 2 4\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{n^3}{n^4} = \\dfrac 1 2\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nSo, we let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{2n^3 - n^2}{4n^4+n}, \\qquad b_n = \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{a_{n}}{b_{n}} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{2n^{3} - n^{2}}{4n^{4} + n} \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{1}{n}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{2n^{3} - n^{2}}{4n^{4} + n} \\cdot n) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{2n^{4} - n^{3}}{4n^{4} + n}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(((2 - \\frac{1}{n}))/((4 + \\frac{1}{n^{3}}))) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 - 0}{4 + 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive, and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nis positive and finite, we can use the limit comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\ndiverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\ndiverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2n^3-n^2}{4n^4+n}\n$$\n\ndiverges." "The limit comparison test states that if $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = L\n$$\n\nwhere $L$ is positive and finite, then the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\neither *both* converge or *both* diverge.\n\nThe given series looks as though it will behave like the divergent series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2n^3}{4n^4} = \\dfrac 2 4\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{n^3}{n^4} = \\dfrac 1 2\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nSo, we let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{2n^3 - n^2}{4n^4+n}, \\qquad b_n = \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{a_{n}}{b_{n}} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{2n^{3} - n^{2}}{4n^{4} + n} \\div \\frac{1}{n}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{2n^{3} - n^{2}}{4n^{4} + n} \\cdot n) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{2n^{4} - n^{3}}{4n^{4} + n}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(((2 - \\frac{1}{n}))/((4 + \\frac{1}{n^{3}}))) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 - 0}{4 + 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive, and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nis positive and finite, we can use the limit comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\ndiverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\ndiverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2n^3-n^2}{4n^4+n}\n$$\n\ndiverges."] [:db/cas 17592186082910 :question/worked-solution "The limit comparison test states that if $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = L\n$$\n\nwhere $L$ is positive and finite, then the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\neither *both* converge or *both* diverge.\n\nThe given series looks as though it will behave like the convergent geometric series\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2^n}{3^n} = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3} \\right)^n\n$$\n\nWe know the above series converges because it is a geometric series with common ratio\n\n$$\n\\mid r \\mid = \\dfrac{2}{3} < 1\n$$\n\nSo, we let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{3(2^n)+1}{2(3^n)+1}, \\qquad b_n = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3} \\right)^n\n$$\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {a}_{n}}{ {b}_{n}}} & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 ( {2}^{n} ) + 1}{2 ( {3}^{n} ) + 1}} \\operatorname{div}   {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {2}^{n}}{ {3}^{n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 ( {2}^{n} ) + 1}{2 ( {3}^{n} ) + 1}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {3}^{n}}{ {2}^{n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {2}^{n} ( 3 + {2}^{- n} )}{ {3}^{n} ( 2 + {3}^{- n} )}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {3}^{n}}{ {2}^{n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {2}^{n}} ( 3 + {2}^{- n} )}{\\cancel{ {3}^{n}} ( 2 + {3}^{- n} )}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {3}^{n}}}{\\cancel{ {2}^{n}}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 + {2}^{- n}}{2 + {3}^{- n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 + 2 ( 0 )}{2 + 3 ( 0 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nis positive and finite, we can use the limit comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\nconverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nconverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{3(2^n)+1}{2(3^n)+1}\n$$\n\nconverges." "The limit comparison test states that if $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = L\n$$\n\nwhere $L$ is positive and finite, then the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\neither *both* converge or *both* diverge.\n\nThe given series looks as though it will behave like the convergent geometric series\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2^n}{3^n} = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3} \\right)^n\n$$\n\nWe know the above series converges because it is a geometric series with common ratio\n\n$$\n\\mid r \\mid = \\dfrac{2}{3} < 1\n$$\n\nSo, we let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{3(2^n)+1}{2(3^n)+1}, \\qquad b_n = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3} \\right)^n\n$$\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {a}_{n}}{ {b}_{n}}} & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 ( {2}^{n} ) + 1}{2 ( {3}^{n} ) + 1}} \\div  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {2}^{n}}{ {3}^{n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 ( {2}^{n} ) + 1}{2 ( {3}^{n} ) + 1}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {3}^{n}}{ {2}^{n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {2}^{n} ( 3 + {2}^{- n} )}{ {3}^{n} ( 2 + {3}^{- n} )}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {3}^{n}}{ {2}^{n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {2}^{n}} ( 3 + {2}^{- n} )}{\\cancel{ {3}^{n}} ( 2 + {3}^{- n} )}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {3}^{n}}}{\\cancel{ {2}^{n}}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{n \\to  \\mathrm{\\infty }} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 + {2}^{- n}}{2 + {3}^{- n}}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 + 2 ( 0 )}{2 + 3 ( 0 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nis positive and finite, we can use the limit comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\nconverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nconverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{3(2^n)+1}{2(3^n)+1}\n$$\n\nconverges."] [:db/cas 17592186082911 :question/worked-solution "Let $a_n$ and $b_n$ be two positive sequences. The one-sided limit comparison tests state the following:\n\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = 0,\n$$ and it is known that $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$ converges, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ also converges.\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = \\infty,\n$$ and it is known that $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$ diverges, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ also diverges.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\nis a divergent $p$-series.\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{a_{n}}{b_{n}} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{n^{2}}}{3n + 4} \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{n^{2}}}{3n + 4} \\cdot \\sqrt{n}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{n^{2/3}}{3n + 4} \\cdot n^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{n^{7/6}}{3n + 4}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{n^{1/6}}{3 + 4/n}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∞}{3 + 0} \\\\\n&= ∞\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to\\infty} \\dfrac{a_n}{b_n}=\\infty\n$$\n\nwe can use the one-sided comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\ndiverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\ndiverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{\\sqrt[3]{n^2}}{3n+4}\n$$\n\ndiverges." "Let $a_n$ and $b_n$ be two positive sequences. The one-sided limit comparison tests state the following:\n\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = 0,\n$$ and it is known that $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$ converges, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ also converges.\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = \\infty,\n$$ and it is known that $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$ diverges, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ also diverges.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\nis a divergent $p$-series.\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{a_{n}}{b_{n}} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{n^{2}}}{3n + 4} \\div \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{n^{2}}}{3n + 4} \\cdot \\sqrt{n}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{n^{2/3}}{3n + 4} \\cdot n^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{n^{7/6}}{3n + 4}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{n^{1/6}}{3 + 4/n}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∞}{3 + 0} \\\\\n&= ∞\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to\\infty} \\dfrac{a_n}{b_n}=\\infty\n$$\n\nwe can use the one-sided comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\ndiverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\ndiverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{\\sqrt[3]{n^2}}{3n+4}\n$$\n\ndiverges."] [:db/cas 17592186082950 :question/worked-solution "The profit is equal to the revenue minus the cost:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= R(x) - C(x) \\\\\n&= (130x - 0.4x^{2}) - (6700 + 26x) \\\\\n&=-0.4x^{2} + 104x - 6700\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we want a profit of $\\$20\\,000$, and $P(x)$ is measured in thousands of dollars, we set $P(x)$ equal to\n\n$$\n20\\,000\\operatorname{div}  1\\,000 = 20\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= 20 \\\\\n- 0.4x^{2} + 104x - 6700 &= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can tidy up the equation so that it becomes a quadratic equation in standard form with a leading coefficient of $1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-0.4x^{2} + 104x - 6700 &= 20 \\\\\n0.4x^{2} - 104x + 6720 &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{0.4x^{2} - 104x + 6720}{0.4} &= \\frac{0}{0.4} \\\\\nx^{2} - 260x + 16800 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to solve for $x$, we apply the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (- (-260) ± \\sqrt{(-260)^{2} - 4(1)(16800)})/(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{260 ± \\sqrt{400}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{260 ± 20}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=120$ and $x=140$. Therefore, the company will make a profit of $\\$20\\,000$ if it produces $120$ or $140$ units." "The profit is equal to the revenue minus the cost:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= R(x) - C(x) \\\\\n&= (130x - 0.4x^{2}) - (6700 + 26x) \\\\\n&=-0.4x^{2} + 104x - 6700\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we want a profit of $\\$20\\,000$, and $P(x)$ is measured in thousands of dollars, we set $P(x)$ equal to\n\n$$\n20\\,000\\div 1\\,000 = 20\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= 20 \\\\\n- 0.4x^{2} + 104x - 6700 &= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can tidy up the equation so that it becomes a quadratic equation in standard form with a leading coefficient of $1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-0.4x^{2} + 104x - 6700 &= 20 \\\\\n0.4x^{2} - 104x + 6720 &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{0.4x^{2} - 104x + 6720}{0.4} &= \\frac{0}{0.4} \\\\\nx^{2} - 260x + 16800 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to solve for $x$, we apply the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (- (-260) ± \\sqrt{(-260)^{2} - 4(1)(16800)})/(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{260 ± \\sqrt{400}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{260 ± 20}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=120$ and $x=140$. Therefore, the company will make a profit of $\\$20\\,000$ if it produces $120$ or $140$ units."] [:db/cas 17592186083097 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{n}{3^n}\n$$\n\nIf we replace $n$ with $n+1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_{n+1}= \\frac{n+1}{3^{n+1}}= \\frac{n+1}{3\\cdot 3^n}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have expressions for the consecutive terms $a_n$ and $a_{n+1}$, we can work out the necessary limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} L &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left|\\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n} \\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{n+1}{3\\cdot 3^n} \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{n}{3^n}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{n+1}{3\\cdot 3^n} \\cdot \\frac{3^n}{n}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{n+1}{3n} \\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{n+1}{n}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{n}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(1 + 0\\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\frac{1}{3}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we dropped the absolute value bars because the sequence is positive for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac{1}{3}<1\n$$\n\nwe conclude that the series is convergent by the ratio test." "Let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{n}{3^n}\n$$\n\nIf we replace $n$ with $n+1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_{n+1}= \\frac{n+1}{3^{n+1}}= \\frac{n+1}{3\\cdot 3^n}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have expressions for the consecutive terms $a_n$ and $a_{n+1}$, we can work out the necessary limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} L &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left|\\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n} \\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{n+1}{3\\cdot 3^n} \\div \\frac{n}{3^n}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{n+1}{3\\cdot 3^n} \\cdot \\frac{3^n}{n}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{n+1}{3n} \\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{n+1}{n}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{n}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(1 + 0\\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\frac{1}{3}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we dropped the absolute value bars because the sequence is positive for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac{1}{3}<1\n$$\n\nwe conclude that the series is convergent by the ratio test."] [:db/cas 17592186083098 :question/worked-solution "This is an example of an **alternating series**. The $(-1)^n$ causes the signs of the successive terms to alternate between positive and negative.\n\nLet\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{(-1)^n\\,n!}{3^n}\n$$\n\nIf we replace $n$ with $n+1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_{n+1}= \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}(n+1)!}{3^{n+1}}= \\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\\,(n+1)!}{3\\cdot 3^n}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have expressions for the consecutive terms $a_n$ and $a_{n+1}$, we can work out the necessary limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} L &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left|\\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n} \\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left| \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}\\,(n+1)!}{3\\cdot 3^n} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{(-1)^n\\,n!}{3^n}\\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left| \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}\\,(n+1)!}{3\\cdot 3^n} \\cdot \\dfrac{3^n}{(-1)^n\\,n!}\\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left|-\\dfrac{n+1}{3}\\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac 1 3\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(n+1\\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\infty. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nL=\\infty > 1\n$$\n\nwe conclude that the series is divergent by the ratio test." "This is an example of an **alternating series**. The $(-1)^n$ causes the signs of the successive terms to alternate between positive and negative.\n\nLet\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{(-1)^n\\,n!}{3^n}\n$$\n\nIf we replace $n$ with $n+1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_{n+1}= \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}(n+1)!}{3^{n+1}}= \\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\\,(n+1)!}{3\\cdot 3^n}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have expressions for the consecutive terms $a_n$ and $a_{n+1}$, we can work out the necessary limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} L &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left|\\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n} \\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left| \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}\\,(n+1)!}{3\\cdot 3^n} \\div \\dfrac{(-1)^n\\,n!}{3^n}\\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left| \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}\\,(n+1)!}{3\\cdot 3^n} \\cdot \\dfrac{3^n}{(-1)^n\\,n!}\\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left|-\\dfrac{n+1}{3}\\right| \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac 1 3\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(n+1\\right) \\\\[5pt] &=\\infty. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nL=\\infty > 1\n$$\n\nwe conclude that the series is divergent by the ratio test."] [:db/cas 17592186083099 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{2n+1}\n$$\n\nIf we replace $n$ with $n+1$, we get\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = \\frac{1}{2(n+1)+1}= \\frac{1}{2n+3}\n$$\n\nNow that we have expressions for the consecutive terms $a_n$ and $a_{n+1}$, we can work out the necessary limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty} \\mid \\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty} \\mid \\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty} \\mid \\frac{1}{2n + 3} \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{1}{2n + 1} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{1}{2n + 3} \\cdot \\frac{2n + 1}{1}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{2n + 1}{2n + 3}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{2 + \\frac{1}{n}}{2 + \\frac{3}{n}}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L=1$, the ratio test gives *no conclusion* about whether the series is convergent or divergent.\n\n**Note:** Although the ratio test gave no conclusion, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{2n+1}\n$$\n\nis actually divergent." "Let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{2n+1}\n$$\n\nIf we replace $n$ with $n+1$, we get\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = \\frac{1}{2(n+1)+1}= \\frac{1}{2n+3}\n$$\n\nNow that we have expressions for the consecutive terms $a_n$ and $a_{n+1}$, we can work out the necessary limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty} \\mid \\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty} \\mid \\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty} \\mid \\frac{1}{2n + 3} \\div \\frac{1}{2n + 1} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{1}{2n + 3} \\cdot \\frac{2n + 1}{1}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{2n + 1}{2n + 3}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{2 + \\frac{1}{n}}{2 + \\frac{3}{n}}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L=1$, the ratio test gives *no conclusion* about whether the series is convergent or divergent.\n\n**Note:** Although the ratio test gave no conclusion, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{2n+1}\n$$\n\nis actually divergent."] [:db/cas 17592186084503 :tutorial/content "Let's use the limit comparison test to determine whether the following series converges or diverges:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n + 2}\n$$\n\nThis looks a bit like the diverging $p$-series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nHowever, we cannot use the comparison test because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{n + 2} < \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nLet's instead try the limit comparison test. Let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{n + 2}, \\qquad b_n = \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{a_{n}}{b_{n}} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{1}{n + 2} \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{1}{n} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{n}{n + 2} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\left(\\frac{n}{n}\\right))/((\\frac{n}{n} + \\frac{2}{n})) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(1)/((1 + \\frac{2}{n})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1 + 0} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = 1\n$$\n\nis positive and finite, we can use the limit comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\ndiverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\ndiverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n + 2}\n$$\n\ndiverges." "Let's use the limit comparison test to determine whether the following series converges or diverges:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n + 2}\n$$\n\nThis looks a bit like the diverging $p$-series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nHowever, we cannot use the comparison test because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{n + 2} < \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nLet's instead try the limit comparison test. Let\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{n + 2}, \\qquad b_n = \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nComputing the limit of the ratio, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{a_{n}}{b_{n}} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{1}{n + 2} \\div \\frac{1}{n} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{n}{n + 2} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\left(\\frac{n}{n}\\right))/((\\frac{n}{n} + \\frac{2}{n})) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(1)/((1 + \\frac{2}{n})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1 + 0} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{a_n}{b_n} = 1\n$$\n\nis positive and finite, we can use the limit comparison test.\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\ndiverges, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\ndiverges as well. Therefore, the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n + 2}\n$$\n\ndiverges."] [:db/cas 17592186087429 :question/worked-solution "The prime factors of a number are the primes that divide the number with no remainder.\n\nThe first few prime numbers are given below.\n\n$$\n2, \\quad 3, \\quad 5, \\quad 7, \\quad 11, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nLet's see which of the given numbers are prime factors of $12{:}$\n\n- $3$ is prime. Also, $3$ is a factor of $12$ since $12 \\operatorname{div}  3 = 4. \\quad \\color{green} \\checkmark$\n- $4$ is **not** prime.\n\n- $5$ is prime. However, $5$ is **not** a factor of $12$ since dividing $12$ by\n$5$ gives a remainder of $2.\\quad\\color{red} \\times$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$3$ only.\"" "The prime factors of a number are the primes that divide the number with no remainder.\n\nThe first few prime numbers are given below.\n\n$$\n2, \\quad 3, \\quad 5, \\quad 7, \\quad 11, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nLet's see which of the given numbers are prime factors of $12{:}$\n\n- $3$ is prime. Also, $3$ is a factor of $12$ since $12 \\div 3 = 4. \\quad \\color{green} \\checkmark$\n- $4$ is **not** prime.\n\n- $5$ is prime. However, $5$ is **not** a factor of $12$ since dividing $12$ by\n$5$ gives a remainder of $2.\\quad\\color{red} \\times$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$3$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186088988 :tutorial/content "The first few prime numbers are\n\n$$\n2,\\quad 3, \\quad 5, \\quad 7, \\quad 11, \\quad 13, \\quad 17, \\quad 19, \\quad 23, \\quad 29, \\quad \\ldots\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember that $1$ is *not* a prime number.\n\nA number's **prime factors** are the prime numbers that divide the number with no remainder. \n\nLet's list the prime factors of $45{:}$\n\n- $2$ is *not* a prime factor of $45$ since dividing $45$ by $2$ gives a remainder of $1. \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\n- $3$ *is* a prime factor of $45$ since $45 \\operatorname{div}  3 = 15.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $5$ *is* a prime factor of $45$ since $45 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 9.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $7$ is *not* a prime factor of $45$ since dividing $45$ by $7$ gives a remainder of $3. \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nThere are no other prime numbers that divide $45.$ Therefore, the prime factors of $45$ are $3$ and $5.$\n\nWe often wish to find a number's prime factors. We'll discuss this more in a future lesson." "The first few prime numbers are\n\n$$\n2,\\quad 3, \\quad 5, \\quad 7, \\quad 11, \\quad 13, \\quad 17, \\quad 19, \\quad 23, \\quad 29, \\quad \\ldots\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember that $1$ is *not* a prime number.\n\nA number's **prime factors** are the prime numbers that divide the number with no remainder. \n\nLet's list the prime factors of $45{:}$\n\n- $2$ is *not* a prime factor of $45$ since dividing $45$ by $2$ gives a remainder of $1. \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\n- $3$ *is* a prime factor of $45$ since $45 \\div 3 = 15.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $5$ *is* a prime factor of $45$ since $45 \\div 5 = 9.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $7$ is *not* a prime factor of $45$ since dividing $45$ by $7$ gives a remainder of $3. \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nThere are no other prime numbers that divide $45.$ Therefore, the prime factors of $45$ are $3$ and $5.$\n\nWe often wish to find a number's prime factors. We'll discuss this more in a future lesson."] [:db/cas 17592186091946 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(5x^2-10x^3) \\operatorname{div}  (5x)$\n\nII. $(1-x^3) \\operatorname{div}  (x^3)$\n\nIII. $(2x^5) \\cdot (-x^3-7)$" "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(5x^2-10x^3) \\div (5x)$\n\nII. $(1-x^3) \\div (x^3)$\n\nIII. $(2x^5) \\cdot (-x^3-7)$"] [:db/cas 17592186092554 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n1. $(-7x) + (2x^{2} + x)$\n2. $(2x - x^{3}) \\operatorname{div}  (x)$\n3. $(2x^{7}) \\cdot (1 + 2x^{5})$" "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n1. $(-7x) + (2x^{2} + x)$\n2. $(2x - x^{3}) \\div (x)$\n3. $(2x^{7}) \\cdot (1 + 2x^{5})$"] [:db/cas 17592186100600 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(-5)^{7}}{(-5)^3} = (-5)^{7} \\operatorname{div}  (-5)^3\n$$\n\nNow, to divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n(-5)^{7} \\operatorname{div}  (-5)^3 = (-5)^{7-3} = (-5)^{4}\n$$" "First, we write the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(-5)^{7}}{(-5)^3} = (-5)^{7} \\div (-5)^3\n$$\n\nNow, to divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n(-5)^{7} \\div (-5)^3 = (-5)^{7-3} = (-5)^{4}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186100601 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^4 \\operatorname{div}  \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^2\n$$ as a base raised to a single exponent." "Express $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^4 \\div \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^2\n$$ as a base raised to a single exponent."] [:db/cas 17592186100601 :question/worked-solution "To divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^4 \\operatorname{div}  \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4-2}= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}\n$$" "To divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^4 \\div \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4-2}= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186100602 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.2^5}{0.2^2} = 0.2^5 \\operatorname{div}  0.2^2\n$$\n\nNow, to divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0.2}^{5}÷{0.2}^{2}={0.2}^{5-2}={0.2}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we write the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.2^5}{0.2^2} = 0.2^5 \\div 0.2^2\n$$\n\nNow, to divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0.2}^{5}÷{0.2}^{2}={0.2}^{5-2}={0.2}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186101510 :question/problem "What is $3^{8} \\operatorname{div}  3^{4}$?" "What is $3^{8} \\div 3^{4}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186101513 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{7} \\operatorname{div}  \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{5}\n$$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{7} \\div \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{5}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186101515 :question/problem "What is $(-0.5)^{8} \\operatorname{div}  (-0.5)^{2}$?" "What is $(-0.5)^{8} \\div (-0.5)^{2}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186101517 :question/problem "What is $3^{-5} \\operatorname{div}  3^{-2}$?" "What is $3^{-5} \\div 3^{-2}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186102023 :tutorial/content "To divide powers of the same base, we simply *subtract* the exponents. This is known as the **quotient rule for exponents**. For instance, in the expression \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}3}^2,\n$$\n\nboth powers have the same base $({\\color{blue}3})$. Therefore, we can simplify this expression by subtracting the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{5-2} = {\\color{blue}3}^3.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to the following process:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{5}÷{3}^{2} &= {3}^{3+2}÷{3}^{2} \\\\ &= {3}^{3}\\times \\underbrace{{3}^{2}÷{3}^{2}}_{=1} \\\\ &= {3}^{3}\\times 1 \\\\ &= {3}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To divide powers of the same base, we simply *subtract* the exponents. This is known as the **quotient rule for exponents**. For instance, in the expression \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\div {\\color{blue}3}^2,\n$$\n\nboth powers have the same base $({\\color{blue}3})$. Therefore, we can simplify this expression by subtracting the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\div {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{5-2} = {\\color{blue}3}^3.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to the following process:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{5}÷{3}^{2} &= {3}^{3+2}÷{3}^{2} \\\\ &= {3}^{3}\\times \\underbrace{{3}^{2}÷{3}^{2}}_{=1} \\\\ &= {3}^{3}\\times 1 \\\\ &= {3}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186102024 :tutorial/content "We can use the quotient rule for exponents with negative exponents too. For example, to simplify $3^{-5}\\operatorname{div}  3^{-3},$ we subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n3^{-5} \\operatorname{div}  3^{-3} = 3^{-5-(-3)} = 3^{-2}\n$$\n\nNow, to evaluate $3^{-2},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $(3)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n3^{-2}  = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 =\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{9}\n$$" "We can use the quotient rule for exponents with negative exponents too. For example, to simplify $3^{-5}\\div 3^{-3},$ we subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n3^{-5} \\div 3^{-3} = 3^{-5-(-3)} = 3^{-2}\n$$\n\nNow, to evaluate $3^{-2},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $(3)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n3^{-2}  = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 =\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{9}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186104963 :answer/value "4 \\operatorname{div}  (11 \\cdot 3)" "4 \\div (11 \\cdot 3)"] [:db/cas 17592186104964 :answer/value "4^{3} \\operatorname{div}  11" "4^{3} \\div 11"] [:db/cas 17592186104965 :answer/value "4 \\operatorname{div}  (11 + 3)" "4 \\div (11 + 3)"] [:db/cas 17592186104966 :answer/value "4^{3} \\operatorname{div}  11^{3}" "4^{3} \\div 11^{3}"] [:db/cas 17592186104967 :answer/value "4 \\operatorname{div}  11^{3}" "4 \\div 11^{3}"] [:db/cas 17592186114997 :question/worked-solution "Here, we are given that the area of the sector is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=6\\pi\\,\\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nand the central angle is\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi\n$$\n\nradians.\n\nRearranging the formula for the area of a sector, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{r^{2}θ}{2} \\\\\nr^{2}θ &= 2A \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{2A}{θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} &= \\frac{2 \\cdot A}{θ} \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot 6π)/((\\frac{4}{3}π)) \\\\\n&= 12π \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{4π}{3} \\\\\n&= 12π \\cdot \\frac{3}{4π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36π}{4π} \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{9} = 3\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$" "Here, we are given that the area of the sector is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=6\\pi\\,\\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nand the central angle is\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi\n$$\n\nradians.\n\nRearranging the formula for the area of a sector, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{r^{2}θ}{2} \\\\\nr^{2}θ &= 2A \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{2A}{θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} &= \\frac{2 \\cdot A}{θ} \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot 6π)/((\\frac{4}{3}π)) \\\\\n&= 12π \\div \\frac{4π}{3} \\\\\n&= 12π \\cdot \\frac{3}{4π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36π}{4π} \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{9} = 3\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186119493 :question/worked-solution "Recall that we always round to $3$ decimal places unless stated otherwise.\n\nOur expression can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{e+7}{\\pi-1} = {\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{(}e+7\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{)}} \\operatorname{div}  {\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{(}\\pi-1\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{)}}\n$$\n\nWe're now ready to enter this expression into a calculator. To do so, we note the following:\n\n- We enter the symbols from left to right as they appear.\n- Your calculator will have special $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,(\\,}$ and $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,)\\,}$ buttons for left and right parentheses.\n- Scientific and graphing calculators usually have special $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,e\\,}$ and $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\pi\\,}$ buttons. You may need to press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\textrm{2nd}}$ or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\textrm{SHIFT}}$ to access these.\n\nNotice the parentheses in the expression above.\n\nEvaluating this using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n(e+7) \\operatorname{div}  (\\pi-1) \\approx 4.538\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Recall that we always round to $3$ decimal places unless stated otherwise.\n\nOur expression can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{e+7}{\\pi-1} = {\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{(}e+7\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{)}} \\div {\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{(}\\pi-1\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{)}}\n$$\n\nWe're now ready to enter this expression into a calculator. To do so, we note the following:\n\n- We enter the symbols from left to right as they appear.\n- Your calculator will have special $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,(\\,}$ and $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,)\\,}$ buttons for left and right parentheses.\n- Scientific and graphing calculators usually have special $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,e\\,}$ and $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\pi\\,}$ buttons. You may need to press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\textrm{2nd}}$ or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\textrm{SHIFT}}$ to access these.\n\nNotice the parentheses in the expression above.\n\nEvaluating this using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n(e+7) \\div (\\pi-1) \\approx 4.538\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186119507 :question/worked-solution "To determine if the relationship is linear or exponential, we need to determine how $y$ changes as $x$ increases at each step:\n\n- For a linear relationship, there should be a common *difference* between successive values of $y$.\n- For an exponential relationship, there should be a common *ratio* between successive values of $y$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Let's check for a common ratio by dividing successive values of $y$. We see that\n$1 \\operatorname{div}  \\left(\\dfrac15\\right) = 5$,\nand similarly,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{1} = \\frac{25}{5} = 5.\n$$\nSince the *ratio* of any two successive terms is constant, the relationship is *exponential*. In this case, the common ratio is $5$.\n- Statement III is false. When $x$ increases by $1$, the respective value of $y$ increases by a factor of $5$, not $10$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SteelBlue}\\overset{+1}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SteelBlue}\\overset{+1}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SteelBlue}\\overset{+1}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SandyBrown}\\underset{\\times 5}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SandyBrown}\\underset{\\times 5}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SandyBrown}\\underset{\\times 5}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "To determine if the relationship is linear or exponential, we need to determine how $y$ changes as $x$ increases at each step:\n\n- For a linear relationship, there should be a common *difference* between successive values of $y$.\n- For an exponential relationship, there should be a common *ratio* between successive values of $y$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Let's check for a common ratio by dividing successive values of $y$. We see that\n$1 \\div \\left(\\dfrac15\\right) = 5$,\nand similarly,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{1} = \\frac{25}{5} = 5.\n$$\nSince the *ratio* of any two successive terms is constant, the relationship is *exponential*. In this case, the common ratio is $5$.\n- Statement III is false. When $x$ increases by $1$, the respective value of $y$ increases by a factor of $5$, not $10$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SteelBlue}\\overset{+1}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SteelBlue}\\overset{+1}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SteelBlue}\\overset{+1}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SandyBrown}\\underset{\\times 5}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SandyBrown}\\underset{\\times 5}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SandyBrown}\\underset{\\times 5}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186121116 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to evaluate the following expression using a scientific calculator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{e-1}\n$$\n\nUsing parentheses when inputting the denominator $e-1$ into a calculator is *essential*. This is because $\\pi \\operatorname{div}  e - 1$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}e - 1\n$$\n\n \nwhich is *not* the same as the expression we want to evaluate! \n\nSo, let's begin by rewriting our expression using parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{e-1} = \\pi \\operatorname{div}  \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{(}e - 1\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{)}\n$$\n\nWe're now ready to enter this expression into a calculator. To do so, we note the following:\n\n- We enter the symbols from left to right as they appear.\n\n- Your calculator will have special $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,(\\,}$ and $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,)\\,}$ buttons for left and right parentheses.\n\n- Scientific and graphing calculators usually have special $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,e\\,}$ and $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\pi\\,}$ buttons. You may need to press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\textrm{2nd}}$ or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\textrm{SHIFT}}$ to access these.\n\nEvaluating this using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\pi \\operatorname{div}  (e - 1) \\approx 1.828\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nWhen evaluating expressions on a scientific or graphing calculator, we always round to $3$ decimal places unless stated otherwise." "Suppose we want to evaluate the following expression using a scientific calculator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{e-1}\n$$\n\nUsing parentheses when inputting the denominator $e-1$ into a calculator is *essential*. This is because $\\pi \\div e - 1$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}e - 1\n$$\n\n \nwhich is *not* the same as the expression we want to evaluate! \n\nSo, let's begin by rewriting our expression using parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{e-1} = \\pi \\div \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{(}e - 1\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{)}\n$$\n\nWe're now ready to enter this expression into a calculator. To do so, we note the following:\n\n- We enter the symbols from left to right as they appear.\n\n- Your calculator will have special $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,(\\,}$ and $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,)\\,}$ buttons for left and right parentheses.\n\n- Scientific and graphing calculators usually have special $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,e\\,}$ and $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\pi\\,}$ buttons. You may need to press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\textrm{2nd}}$ or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\textrm{SHIFT}}$ to access these.\n\nEvaluating this using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\pi \\div (e - 1) \\approx 1.828\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nWhen evaluating expressions on a scientific or graphing calculator, we always round to $3$ decimal places unless stated otherwise."] [:db/cas 17592186121118 :tutorial/content "Most calculators have a special $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}\\,}$ button for the square root. However, many calculators do not have a special symbol for other radicals.\n\nIf your calculator *does* have a button for other radicals, it will look similar to $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\sqrt[n]{x}\\,}.$ \n\nAn alternative way to compute the numerical value of a radical is to rewrite the expression as a fractional exponent and then use the exponent button. Common symbols used for the exponent button are $\\bbox[5px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\land\\,},$ $\\bbox[5px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,x^y\\,},$ or similar.\n\nLet's use the exponent button to evaluate the following expression to $5$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[7]{38}\n$$\n\nFirstly, our radical can be written as a fractional exponent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[7]{38} = 38^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1/7}}\n$$\n\nSo, we'd use a sequence of buttons similar to the following to evaluate this expression:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,3\\,}  \\qquad\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,8\\,}  \\qquad\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\land\\,} \\qquad\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,1\\,}  \\qquad\n\\bbox[5px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\operatorname{div} \\,}  \\qquad\n\\bbox[7px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,7\\,}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n38^{1/7} \\approx 1.681\\,45\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\n**Watch out!** In the sequence of buttons above, we assume that pressing the exponent button moves the cursor upwards into a superscript. If your calculator does not do this, then it is likely necessary to enclose the exponent in parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,3\\,}  \\qquad\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,8\\,}  \\qquad\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\land\\,} \\qquad\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,(\\,}  \\qquad\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,1\\,}  \\qquad\n\\bbox[5px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\operatorname{div} \\,}  \\qquad\n\\bbox[7px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,7\\,} \\qquad\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,)\\,}  \\qquad\n$$" "Most calculators have a special $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}\\,}$ button for the square root. However, many calculators do not have a special symbol for other radicals.\n\nIf your calculator *does* have a button for other radicals, it will look similar to $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\sqrt[n]{x}\\,}.$ \n\nAn alternative way to compute the numerical value of a radical is to rewrite the expression as a fractional exponent and then use the exponent button. Common symbols used for the exponent button are $\\bbox[5px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\land\\,},$ $\\bbox[5px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,x^y\\,},$ or similar.\n\nLet's use the exponent button to evaluate the following expression to $5$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[7]{38}\n$$\n\nFirstly, our radical can be written as a fractional exponent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[7]{38} = 38^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1/7}}\n$$\n\nSo, we'd use a sequence of buttons similar to the following to evaluate this expression:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,3\\,}  \\qquad\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,8\\,}  \\qquad\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\land\\,} \\qquad\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,1\\,}  \\qquad\n\\bbox[5px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\div\\,}  \\qquad\n\\bbox[7px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,7\\,}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n38^{1/7} \\approx 1.681\\,45\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\n**Watch out!** In the sequence of buttons above, we assume that pressing the exponent button moves the cursor upwards into a superscript. If your calculator does not do this, then it is likely necessary to enclose the exponent in parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,3\\,}  \\qquad\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,8\\,}  \\qquad\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\land\\,} \\qquad\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,(\\,}  \\qquad\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,1\\,}  \\qquad\n\\bbox[5px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\div\\,}  \\qquad\n\\bbox[7px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,7\\,} \\qquad\n\\bbox[6px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,)\\,}  \\qquad\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186128870 :question/problem "Calculate $10\\operatorname{div}  5^0 + (3^2-5).$" "Calculate $10\\div 5^0 + (3^2-5).$"] [:db/cas 17592186128872 :question/problem "Find the value of $2 + \\left(-4\\right)^{-2} \\operatorname{div}  \\left( 1.5 + \\dfrac{2}{15} \\right)^0.$" "Find the value of $2 + \\left(-4\\right)^{-2} \\div \\left( 1.5 + \\dfrac{2}{15} \\right)^0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186129400 :question/problem "What is $8^{2} \\operatorname{div}  16 + 11^{0}$?" "What is $8^{2} \\div 16 + 11^{0}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186129401 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle 18 \\operatorname{div}  \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{-2} + (\\frac{1}{101} - 0.01)^{0}\n$$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle 18 \\div \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{-2} + (\\frac{1}{101} - 0.01)^{0}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186129832 :question/problem "What is $5^{-2} \\operatorname{div}  \\frac{1}{5}$?" "What is $5^{-2} \\div \\frac{1}{5}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186129833 :question/problem "What is $2^{-2} \\operatorname{div}  (\\frac{1}{4} - 1)$?" "What is $2^{-2} \\div (\\frac{1}{4} - 1)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186130444 :tutorial/content "Rational expressions are *closed* under the arithmetic operations of addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division.\n\nSo, we have that:\n\n- The *sum* of two rational expressions is a rational expression:\n$\\textrm{rational} + \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- The *difference* between two rational expressions is a rational expression:\n$\\textrm{rational} - \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- The *product* of two rational expressions is a rational expression:\n$\\textrm{rational} \\:\\cdot\\: \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- The *quotient* of two rational expressions is a rational expression:\n$\\textrm{rational} \\operatorname{div}  \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\nprovided that the denominator is nonzero.\n\nIn this sense, rational expressions and rational numbers have similar properties." "Rational expressions are *closed* under the arithmetic operations of addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division.\n\nSo, we have that:\n\n- The *sum* of two rational expressions is a rational expression:\n$\\textrm{rational} + \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- The *difference* between two rational expressions is a rational expression:\n$\\textrm{rational} - \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- The *product* of two rational expressions is a rational expression:\n$\\textrm{rational} \\:\\cdot\\: \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- The *quotient* of two rational expressions is a rational expression:\n$\\textrm{rational} \\div \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\nprovided that the denominator is nonzero.\n\nIn this sense, rational expressions and rational numbers have similar properties."] [:db/cas 17592186130481 :tutorial/content "As we know already, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we follow the order of operations (PEMDAS).\n\nThe same rules apply to numerical expressions with zero and negative exponents. To demonstrate, let's compute the value of\n\n$$\n(-3)^0 +2^2 \\operatorname{div}  2.\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that $\\left(-3\\right)^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-3)}^{0}+{2}^{2}÷2 &= 1+{2}^{2}÷2 \\\\ &= 1+4÷2 \\\\ &= 1+2 \\\\ &= 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "As we know already, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we follow the order of operations (PEMDAS).\n\nThe same rules apply to numerical expressions with zero and negative exponents. To demonstrate, let's compute the value of\n\n$$\n(-3)^0 +2^2 \\div 2.\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that $\\left(-3\\right)^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-3)}^{0}+{2}^{2}÷2 &= 1+{2}^{2}÷2 \\\\ &= 1+4÷2 \\\\ &= 1+2 \\\\ &= 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186133496 :question/worked-solution "To find the reciprocal of $-21,$ we divide $1$ by $-21.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n1 \\operatorname{div}  (-21) = -\\dfrac{1}{21}.\n$$" "To find the reciprocal of $-21,$ we divide $1$ by $-21.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n1 \\div (-21) = -\\dfrac{1}{21}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186135172 :tutorial/content "The **reciprocal** of a number is equal to ${\\color{red}{1}}$ divided by the number. For example:\n\n- the reciprocal of ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ is equal to ${\\color{red}{1}} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}{2}} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\n- the reciprocal of ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ is equal to  ${\\color{red}{1}} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}{3}} = \\dfrac{1}{3}$\n\n- the reciprocal of $-{\\color{blue}{7}}$ is equal to  ${\\color{red}{1}} \\operatorname{div}  ({\\color{blue}{-7}}) = -\\dfrac{1}{7}$\n\nThe reciprocal of ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ is  equal to  ${\\color{red}{1}} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}{1}} = 1.$ So the number $1$ is equal to its reciprocal. The same is true for $-1.$\n\nThe reciprocal of a number always has the same sign as the original number.\n\n- If the number is positive, its reciprocal will be positive, while\n\n- if the number is negative, its reciprocal is also negative." "The **reciprocal** of a number is equal to ${\\color{red}{1}}$ divided by the number. For example:\n\n- the reciprocal of ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ is equal to ${\\color{red}{1}} \\div {\\color{blue}{2}} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\n- the reciprocal of ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ is equal to  ${\\color{red}{1}} \\div {\\color{blue}{3}} = \\dfrac{1}{3}$\n\n- the reciprocal of $-{\\color{blue}{7}}$ is equal to  ${\\color{red}{1}} \\div ({\\color{blue}{-7}}) = -\\dfrac{1}{7}$\n\nThe reciprocal of ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ is  equal to  ${\\color{red}{1}} \\div {\\color{blue}{1}} = 1.$ So the number $1$ is equal to its reciprocal. The same is true for $-1.$\n\nThe reciprocal of a number always has the same sign as the original number.\n\n- If the number is positive, its reciprocal will be positive, while\n\n- if the number is negative, its reciprocal is also negative."] [:db/cas 17592186138086 :question/worked-solution "To add two mixed numbers, we add\n\n- whole numbers to whole numbers, and\n- fractions to fractions.\n\nWith that in mind let's add our mixed numbers.\n\n- Adding the whole numbers, we get\n$2 + 4 = {\\color{red}{6}}$.\n- Adding the fractions, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac 5 {10} + \\dfrac 3 {10} = \\dfrac{5+3}{10} = \\dfrac 8 {10}.\n$$\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{8}{10} = \\dfrac{8\\operatorname{div}  2}{10\\operatorname{div}  2} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{4}{5}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 5 {10} + 4\\,\\dfrac 3 {10} = {\\color{red}{6}} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 4 5}} = 6\\,\\dfrac 4 5\n$$" "To add two mixed numbers, we add\n\n- whole numbers to whole numbers, and\n- fractions to fractions.\n\nWith that in mind let's add our mixed numbers.\n\n- Adding the whole numbers, we get\n$2 + 4 = {\\color{red}{6}}$.\n- Adding the fractions, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac 5 {10} + \\dfrac 3 {10} = \\dfrac{5+3}{10} = \\dfrac 8 {10}.\n$$\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{8}{10} = \\dfrac{8\\div 2}{10\\div 2} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{4}{5}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 5 {10} + 4\\,\\dfrac 3 {10} = {\\color{red}{6}} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 4 5}} = 6\\,\\dfrac 4 5\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138091 :question/worked-solution "Whenever we write a decimal as a mixed number, our mixed number will have\n\n- a *whole number part* that corresponds to the whole number to the left of the decimal point, and\n- a *fractional part* that corresponds to the decimal part to the right of the decimal point.\n\nWith that in mind, let's write ${\\color{blue}51}.\\!{\\color{red}4}$ as a mixed number.\n\n- The whole number part is ${\\color{blue}51}$.\n- The decimal part has $1$ decimal place, so we can write it as a fraction in the following way:\n$0.4= \\dfrac{4}{10}$\nNow we reduce the fraction to its simplest form by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{4}{10} = \\dfrac{4 \\operatorname{div}  2}{10 \\operatorname{div}  2} = {\\color{red}\\dfrac{2}{5}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the given decimal as the following mixed number:\n\n$$\n51.4 = 51 \\, \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$" "Whenever we write a decimal as a mixed number, our mixed number will have\n\n- a *whole number part* that corresponds to the whole number to the left of the decimal point, and\n- a *fractional part* that corresponds to the decimal part to the right of the decimal point.\n\nWith that in mind, let's write ${\\color{blue}51}.\\!{\\color{red}4}$ as a mixed number.\n\n- The whole number part is ${\\color{blue}51}$.\n- The decimal part has $1$ decimal place, so we can write it as a fraction in the following way:\n$0.4= \\dfrac{4}{10}$\nNow we reduce the fraction to its simplest form by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{4}{10} = \\dfrac{4 \\div 2}{10 \\div 2} = {\\color{red}\\dfrac{2}{5}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the given decimal as the following mixed number:\n\n$$\n51.4 = 51 \\, \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138100 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide $71$ by ${\\color{blue}{8}}{:}$\n\n$$\n71 \\operatorname{div}  8 = 8\\, \\textrm{R}\\,{\\color{red}{7}}\n$$\n\nSo, our division gives $8$ wholes, and we're left with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}7}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{71}{8} = 8\\,\\dfrac{\\color{red}7}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$" "First, we divide $71$ by ${\\color{blue}{8}}{:}$\n\n$$\n71 \\div 8 = 8\\, \\textrm{R}\\,{\\color{red}{7}}\n$$\n\nSo, our division gives $8$ wholes, and we're left with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}7}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{71}{8} = 8\\,\\dfrac{\\color{red}7}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138101 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide $51$ by ${\\color{blue}{6}}{:}$\n\n$$\n51 \\operatorname{div}  6 = 8 \\, \\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nSo, our division gives $8$ wholes, and we're left with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nWe can reduce the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nto its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{6} &= \\frac{3 \\operatorname{div}  3}{6 \\operatorname{div}  3} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our mixed number is equivalent to\n\n$$\n8 \\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" "First, we divide $51$ by ${\\color{blue}{6}}{:}$\n\n$$\n51 \\div 6 = 8 \\, \\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nSo, our division gives $8$ wholes, and we're left with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nWe can reduce the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nto its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{6} &= \\frac{3 \\div 3}{6 \\div 3} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our mixed number is equivalent to\n\n$$\n8 \\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138118 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $0.05$ has $2$ decimal places. So, we can write it as a fraction with $100$ in the denominator:\n\n$$\n0.05 = \\dfrac{5}{100}\n$$\n\nWe then reduce this fraction to its simplest form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{100} &= \\frac{5 \\operatorname{div}  5}{100 \\operatorname{div}  5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{100 \\operatorname{div}  5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10 \\cdot 10 \\operatorname{div}  5} \\\\\n&= (1)/(10 \\cdot (10 \\operatorname{div}  5)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.05$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{20}\n$$" "Notice that $0.05$ has $2$ decimal places. So, we can write it as a fraction with $100$ in the denominator:\n\n$$\n0.05 = \\dfrac{5}{100}\n$$\n\nWe then reduce this fraction to its simplest form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{100} &= \\frac{5 \\div 5}{100 \\div 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{100 \\div 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10 \\cdot 10 \\div 5} \\\\\n&= (1)/(10 \\cdot (10 \\div 5)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.05$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{20}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138120 :question/worked-solution "To add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{8} + \\frac{1}{8} = \\frac{3 + 1}{8} = \\frac{4}{8}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and the denominator by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{8} = \\dfrac{4\\operatorname{div}  4}{8\\operatorname{div}  4} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 8 + \\dfrac 1 8 = \\dfrac 1 2\n$$" "To add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{8} + \\frac{1}{8} = \\frac{3 + 1}{8} = \\frac{4}{8}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and the denominator by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{8} = \\dfrac{4\\div 4}{8\\div 4} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 8 + \\dfrac 1 8 = \\dfrac 1 2\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138121 :question/worked-solution "To add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{2 + 2}{3} = \\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nWe then convert\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 4 3\n$$\n\nto a mixed number:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{div}  3 = 1\\,\\textrm{R}1 = 1\\,\\dfrac 1 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 3 + \\dfrac 2 3 = 1\\,\\dfrac 1 3\n$$" "To add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{2 + 2}{3} = \\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nWe then convert\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 4 3\n$$\n\nto a mixed number:\n\n$$\n4\\div 3 = 1\\,\\textrm{R}1 = 1\\,\\dfrac 1 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 3 + \\dfrac 2 3 = 1\\,\\dfrac 1 3\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138122 :question/worked-solution "To add two mixed numbers, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions.\n\n**Step 1**: Adding the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n5+2 = {\\color{red}{7}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now we add the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac 1 6\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with unlike denominators, we must express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nHere, we can make a common denominator of $6$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1\\times 3}{2\\times 3} = \\dfrac{3}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we add the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can simplify the above fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6} = \\dfrac{4\\operatorname{div}  2}{6\\operatorname{div}  2} = \\color{blue} \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Combining our results gives:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{7}} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 2 3}} = 7\\,\\dfrac 2 3\n$$" "To add two mixed numbers, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions.\n\n**Step 1**: Adding the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n5+2 = {\\color{red}{7}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now we add the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac 1 6\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with unlike denominators, we must express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nHere, we can make a common denominator of $6$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1\\times 3}{2\\times 3} = \\dfrac{3}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we add the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can simplify the above fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6} = \\dfrac{4\\div 2}{6\\div 2} = \\color{blue} \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Combining our results gives:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{7}} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 2 3}} = 7\\,\\dfrac 2 3\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138124 :question/worked-solution "To add two mixed numbers, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions.\n\n**Step 1**: Adding the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n1+3 = {\\color{red}{4}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now we add the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac 3 4\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with unlike denominators, we must express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this question, we can make a common denominator of $4$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $4$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1\\times 2}{2\\times 2} = \\dfrac{2}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we add the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{4} + \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is an improper fraction. We can convert the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nto a mixed number in the usual way:\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{div}  4 = 1\\,\\textrm{R}1 = {\\color{blue}{1\\,\\dfrac 1{4}}}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We combine the results to get our answer.\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{4}} + {\\color{blue}{1\\,\\dfrac 1{4}}} = 5\\,\\dfrac 1{4}\n$$\n\nNotice that we needed to add the whole numbers one final time." "To add two mixed numbers, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions.\n\n**Step 1**: Adding the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n1+3 = {\\color{red}{4}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now we add the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac 3 4\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with unlike denominators, we must express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this question, we can make a common denominator of $4$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $4$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1\\times 2}{2\\times 2} = \\dfrac{2}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we add the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{4} + \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is an improper fraction. We can convert the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nto a mixed number in the usual way:\n\n$$\n5\\div 4 = 1\\,\\textrm{R}1 = {\\color{blue}{1\\,\\dfrac 1{4}}}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We combine the results to get our answer.\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{4}} + {\\color{blue}{1\\,\\dfrac 1{4}}} = 5\\,\\dfrac 1{4}\n$$\n\nNotice that we needed to add the whole numbers one final time."] [:db/cas 17592186138125 :question/worked-solution "To subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we must express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nSince $\\color{blue}6$ is a multiple of ${\\color{red}{3}}$, we can make a common denominator of ${\\color{blue}{6}}$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of ${\\color{blue}{6}}$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{\\color{red}3} = \\dfrac{2\\times 2}{{\\color{red}{3}}\\times 2} = \\dfrac{4}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{6} - \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{3}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3\\operatorname{div}  3}{6\\operatorname{div}  3} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" "To subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we must express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nSince $\\color{blue}6$ is a multiple of ${\\color{red}{3}}$, we can make a common denominator of ${\\color{blue}{6}}$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of ${\\color{blue}{6}}$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{\\color{red}3} = \\dfrac{2\\times 2}{{\\color{red}{3}}\\times 2} = \\dfrac{4}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{6} - \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{3}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3\\div 3}{6\\div 3} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138139 :question/worked-solution "To subtract two mixed numbers, we subtract whole numbers from whole numbers and fractions from fractions.\n\n**Step 1**: Subtracting the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n6-3 = {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now we subtract the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} - \\dfrac 3 {10}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this example, we can make a common denominator of $10$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $10$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1\\times 5}{2\\times 5} = \\dfrac{5}{10}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{10} - \\frac{3}{10} = \\frac{2}{10}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{10} = \\dfrac{2\\operatorname{div}  2}{10\\operatorname{div}  2} = \\color{blue} \\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We combine the results to get our final answer:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 1 5}} = {\\color{red}{3}}\\,{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 1 5}}\n$$" "To subtract two mixed numbers, we subtract whole numbers from whole numbers and fractions from fractions.\n\n**Step 1**: Subtracting the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n6-3 = {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now we subtract the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} - \\dfrac 3 {10}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this example, we can make a common denominator of $10$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $10$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1\\times 5}{2\\times 5} = \\dfrac{5}{10}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{10} - \\frac{3}{10} = \\frac{2}{10}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{10} = \\dfrac{2\\div 2}{10\\div 2} = \\color{blue} \\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We combine the results to get our final answer:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 1 5}} = {\\color{red}{3}}\\,{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 1 5}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138155 :question/problem "Calculate $256 \\operatorname{div}  10$." "Calculate $256 \\div 10$."] [:db/cas 17592186138155 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down our number. We can attach as many leading zeros to the whole number part as we want:\n\n![](images/cd/cd2429edae47b41137f1049fbd5169a83ebf3463d346d9bd736f74e40defcb15.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $10$. Since $10$ contains $1$ zero, we move the decimal point $1$ place to the left:\n\n![](images/9e/9e067f5edc626c4990f2b1e9b800247e2c4d535d7df0f024218cc9b34a19d1ca.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/c3/c3efba66d65199557ea643ef0ef00a881d806a98901d6dc25f073b3d90c78820.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n\n$$\n25.60 = 25.6\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n256 \\operatorname{div}  10 = 25.6\n$$" "First, we write down our number. We can attach as many leading zeros to the whole number part as we want:\n\n![](images/cd/cd2429edae47b41137f1049fbd5169a83ebf3463d346d9bd736f74e40defcb15.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $10$. Since $10$ contains $1$ zero, we move the decimal point $1$ place to the left:\n\n![](images/9e/9e067f5edc626c4990f2b1e9b800247e2c4d535d7df0f024218cc9b34a19d1ca.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/c3/c3efba66d65199557ea643ef0ef00a881d806a98901d6dc25f073b3d90c78820.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n\n$$\n25.60 = 25.6\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n256 \\div 10 = 25.6\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138158 :question/problem "Calculate $8 \\operatorname{div}  10$." "Calculate $8 \\div 10$."] [:db/cas 17592186138158 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down our number. We can attach as many leading zeros to the whole number part as we want:\n\n![](images/c9/c9feb4af087a54a16eed8179cc88fc62c3a128eaa2f3d4595e49bbf2056a5958.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $10$. Since $10$ contains $1$ zero, we move the decimal point $1$ place to the left:\n\n![](images/67/6799c901ac93a3d38157e0569589f9b4c9a9087733ea88a8aff69f9249c12a21.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/93/9336332175adc32566e1b8a965c46e2219bc2b8b4da5629d1422702757b7584b.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n$0.80 = 0.8$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n8 \\operatorname{div}  10 = 0.8\n$$" "First, we write down our number. We can attach as many leading zeros to the whole number part as we want:\n\n![](images/c9/c9feb4af087a54a16eed8179cc88fc62c3a128eaa2f3d4595e49bbf2056a5958.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $10$. Since $10$ contains $1$ zero, we move the decimal point $1$ place to the left:\n\n![](images/67/6799c901ac93a3d38157e0569589f9b4c9a9087733ea88a8aff69f9249c12a21.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/93/9336332175adc32566e1b8a965c46e2219bc2b8b4da5629d1422702757b7584b.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n$0.80 = 0.8$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n8 \\div 10 = 0.8\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138159 :question/problem "Calculate $66.3 \\operatorname{div}  100$." "Calculate $66.3 \\div 100$."] [:db/cas 17592186138159 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down our number. We can attach as many leading zeros to the whole number part as we want:\n\n![](images/f0/f0c75135166d339375263710870125a922a165c11a487120ad3e7145e5f56773.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $100$. Since $100$ contains $2$ zeros, we move the decimal point $2$ places to the left:\n\n![](images/06/0647f8e29be7464b291e9a93dd167a62bf2e17112dc934d29911fa3bac964c72.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/fa/fa94a68303aa9d99acbea2baac216268487469bc784e3984dda4595bd73224fc.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n$0.663$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n66.3 \\operatorname{div}  100 = 0.663\n$$" "First, we write down our number. We can attach as many leading zeros to the whole number part as we want:\n\n![](images/f0/f0c75135166d339375263710870125a922a165c11a487120ad3e7145e5f56773.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $100$. Since $100$ contains $2$ zeros, we move the decimal point $2$ places to the left:\n\n![](images/06/0647f8e29be7464b291e9a93dd167a62bf2e17112dc934d29911fa3bac964c72.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/fa/fa94a68303aa9d99acbea2baac216268487469bc784e3984dda4595bd73224fc.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n$0.663$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n66.3 \\div 100 = 0.663\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138160 :question/problem "Find the value of $769 \\operatorname{div}  1,000$." "Find the value of $769 \\div 1,000$."] [:db/cas 17592186138160 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down our number. We can attach as many leading zeros to the whole number part as we want:\n\n![](images/ce/ce1cfcb2ea606dcf5f7d9dd8719c6e3a42ca7a7f02255dc397b403e1046bfbf2.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $1,000$. Since $1,000$ contains $3$ zeros, we move the decimal point $3$ places to the left:\n\n![](images/0c/0c5f8f880698eb9da65a268bb6a301f192d8e6c147502f2628338a0a805c2397.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/35/354b25663ba585a237e8592a311f1c4f3ff22e9a5af8723627320eeda5f0188a.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n\n$$\n0.7690 = 0.769\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n769 \\operatorname{div}  1,000 = 0.769\n$$" "First, we write down our number. We can attach as many leading zeros to the whole number part as we want:\n\n![](images/ce/ce1cfcb2ea606dcf5f7d9dd8719c6e3a42ca7a7f02255dc397b403e1046bfbf2.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $1,000$. Since $1,000$ contains $3$ zeros, we move the decimal point $3$ places to the left:\n\n![](images/0c/0c5f8f880698eb9da65a268bb6a301f192d8e6c147502f2628338a0a805c2397.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/35/354b25663ba585a237e8592a311f1c4f3ff22e9a5af8723627320eeda5f0188a.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n\n$$\n0.7690 = 0.769\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n769 \\div 1,000 = 0.769\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138248 :question/problem "Beth calculates $12.5 \\operatorname{div}  10$ and places her answer in the table below. From **left to right**, what are the missing digits?\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}}$ | **.** | $2$ | $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}}$ |" "Beth calculates $12.5 \\div 10$ and places her answer in the table below. From **left to right**, what are the missing digits?\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}}$ | **.** | $2$ | $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}}$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186138248 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the place value chart for $12.5$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThen, we move all digits by one place to the *right.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{5}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n12.5\\operatorname{div}  10 = 1.25\\,\n$$\n\nThe missing digits are therefore $1$ and $5$." "First, we write down the place value chart for $12.5$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThen, we move all digits by one place to the *right.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{5}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n12.5\\div 10 = 1.25\\,\n$$\n\nThe missing digits are therefore $1$ and $5$."] [:db/cas 17592186138249 :question/problem "Find the place value chart of $225 \\operatorname{div}  10$." "Find the place value chart of $225 \\div 10$."] [:db/cas 17592186138250 :question/problem "What is the missing digit in the place value chart below for $0.3\\operatorname{div}  100$?\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | **.** | $0$ | $\\fbox{\\phantom{0}}$ | $3$ |" "What is the missing digit in the place value chart below for $0.3\\div 100$?\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | **.** | $0$ | $\\fbox{\\phantom{0}}$ | $3$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186138250 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the place value chart for $0.3$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThen, we determine the number of places to move. We're dividing by $100$, which has $2$ zeros, so we move all values to the *right* by $2$ places:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we put zeros in all empty cells that are to the right of the decimal point, and also in the empty ones place:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n0.3\\operatorname{div}  100 = 0.003 \\,\n$$\n\nThe missing digit is $0$." "First, we write down the place value chart for $0.3$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThen, we determine the number of places to move. We're dividing by $100$, which has $2$ zeros, so we move all values to the *right* by $2$ places:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we put zeros in all empty cells that are to the right of the decimal point, and also in the empty ones place:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n0.3\\div 100 = 0.003 \\,\n$$\n\nThe missing digit is $0$."] [:db/cas 17592186138251 :question/problem "From **left to right**, what are the missing digits in the place value chart below for $246\\operatorname{div}  1,000$?\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\fbox{\\phantom{0}}$ | **.** | $2$ | $\\fbox{\\phantom{0}}$ | $6$ |" "From **left to right**, what are the missing digits in the place value chart below for $246\\div 1,000$?\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\fbox{\\phantom{0}}$ | **.** | $2$ | $\\fbox{\\phantom{0}}$ | $6$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186138251 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the place value chart for $246$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThen, we determine the number of places to move. We're dividing by $1,000$, which has $3$ zeros, so we move all values to the *right* by $3$ places:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we put zeros in all empty cells that are to the right of the decimal point, and also in the empty ones place:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n246\\operatorname{div}  1,000= 0.246 \\,\n$$\n\nThe missing digits are $0$ and $4$." "First, we write down the place value chart for $246$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThen, we determine the number of places to move. We're dividing by $1,000$, which has $3$ zeros, so we move all values to the *right* by $3$ places:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we put zeros in all empty cells that are to the right of the decimal point, and also in the empty ones place:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n246\\div 1,000= 0.246 \\,\n$$\n\nThe missing digits are $0$ and $4$."] [:db/cas 17592186138252 :question/problem "Calculate the value of $46 \\operatorname{div}  5$." "Calculate the value of $46 \\div 5$."] [:db/cas 17592186138252 :question/worked-solution "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\,\\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9$$5$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$5$$\\color{red}1$ |\n| --- |\n\nNotice that the remainder $\\color{red}1$ is less than the divisor $5$. But we can proceed further by adding a $\\color{blue}0$ after the decimal point in the dividend. We also add a decimal point in the quotient, as shown below:\n\n| $\\,\\,\\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}}$$5$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}} {\\color{blue}0}}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$5$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{lightgray}0$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow we complete the division, as usual, stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0$:\n\n| $\\,\\,\\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\,. \\,} 2$$5$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\,. \\,} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$5$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n46 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 9.2\\,\n$$" "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\,\\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9$$5$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$5$$\\color{red}1$ |\n| --- |\n\nNotice that the remainder $\\color{red}1$ is less than the divisor $5$. But we can proceed further by adding a $\\color{blue}0$ after the decimal point in the dividend. We also add a decimal point in the quotient, as shown below:\n\n| $\\,\\,\\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}}$$5$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}} {\\color{blue}0}}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$5$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{lightgray}0$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow we complete the division, as usual, stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0$:\n\n| $\\,\\,\\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\,. \\,} 2$$5$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\,. \\,} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$5$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n46 \\div 5 = 9.2\\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138253 :question/problem "Find $51 \\operatorname{div}  4$." "Find $51 \\div 4$."] [:db/cas 17592186138253 :question/worked-solution "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\,1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$1$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{red}3$ |\n| --- |\n\nNotice that the remainder $\\color{red}3$ is less than the divisor $4$, but we can proceed further by adding a $\\color{blue}0$ after the decimal point in the dividend. We also add a decimal point in the quotient, as shown below:\n\n| $\\,\\,1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}}$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}} {\\color{blue}0}}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$1$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}3$$\\color{lightgray}0$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow we complete the division, as usual, adding more zeros as needed but stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0$:\n\n| $\\,\\,1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\,. \\,} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$1$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$3$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n51 \\operatorname{div}  4 = 12.75\\,\n$$" "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\,1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$1$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{red}3$ |\n| --- |\n\nNotice that the remainder $\\color{red}3$ is less than the divisor $4$, but we can proceed further by adding a $\\color{blue}0$ after the decimal point in the dividend. We also add a decimal point in the quotient, as shown below:\n\n| $\\,\\,1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}}$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}} {\\color{blue}0}}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$1$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}3$$\\color{lightgray}0$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow we complete the division, as usual, adding more zeros as needed but stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0$:\n\n| $\\,\\,1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\,. \\,} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$1$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$3$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n51 \\div 4 = 12.75\\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138254 :question/problem "What is $79 \\operatorname{div}  8$?" "What is $79 \\div 8$?"] [:db/cas 17592186138254 :question/worked-solution "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\phantom{0}{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9$$8$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$2$${\\color{red}7}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNotice that the remainder $\\color{red}7$ is less than the divisor $8$, but we can proceed further by adding a $\\color{blue}0$ after the decimal point in the dividend. We also add a decimal point in the quotient, as shown below:\n\n| $\\,\\,\\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}}$$8$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}} {\\color{blue}0}}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}7$$\\color{lightgray}0$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow we complete the division, as usual, adding more zeros as needed but stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0$:\n\n| $\\,\\,{\\phantom{0}} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\,. \\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5$$8$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$2$$7$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$4$$6$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$4$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n79 \\operatorname{div}  8 = 9.875\\,\n$$" "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\phantom{0}{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9$$8$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$2$${\\color{red}7}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNotice that the remainder $\\color{red}7$ is less than the divisor $8$, but we can proceed further by adding a $\\color{blue}0$ after the decimal point in the dividend. We also add a decimal point in the quotient, as shown below:\n\n| $\\,\\,\\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}}$$8$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}} {\\color{blue}0}}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}7$$\\color{lightgray}0$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow we complete the division, as usual, adding more zeros as needed but stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0$:\n\n| $\\,\\,{\\phantom{0}} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\,. \\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5$$8$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$2$$7$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$4$$6$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$4$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n79 \\div 8 = 9.875\\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138255 :question/worked-solution "To determine the amount Cameron spent each day, we need to divide $607$ by $5$.\n\nWe start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\,}r} & 121 \\\\ 5 & \\enclose{longdiv}{607} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{00}} \\\\ & 10\\phantom{0} \\\\ & \\underline{-10\\phantom{0}} \\\\ & 07 \\\\ & \\underline{-5} \\\\ & \\color{red}{2}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNotice that the remainder $\\color{red}2$ is less than the divisor $5$. But we can proceed further by adding a $\\color{blue}0$ after the decimal point in the dividend. We also add a decimal point in the quotient, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\,}r} & 121\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{.}} \\\\ 5 & \\enclose{longdiv}{607\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{.}}\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{00.0}}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & 10\\phantom{0.0}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & \\underline{-10\\phantom{0.0}}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & 07\\phantom{.0}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{.0}}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{lightgray}{0}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow we complete the division, as usual, stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\,}r} & 121.4 \\\\ 5 & \\enclose{longdiv}{607.0} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{00.0}} \\\\ & 10\\phantom{0.0} \\\\ & \\underline{-10\\phantom{0.0}} \\\\ & 07\\phantom{.0} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{.0}} \\\\ & 20 \\\\ & \\underline{-20} \\\\ & \\boxed{0}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n607 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 121.4 \\,\n$$\n\nWe conclude that Cameron spent $\\$121.40$ each day." "To determine the amount Cameron spent each day, we need to divide $607$ by $5$.\n\nWe start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\,}r} & 121 \\\\ 5 & \\enclose{longdiv}{607} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{00}} \\\\ & 10\\phantom{0} \\\\ & \\underline{-10\\phantom{0}} \\\\ & 07 \\\\ & \\underline{-5} \\\\ & \\color{red}{2}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNotice that the remainder $\\color{red}2$ is less than the divisor $5$. But we can proceed further by adding a $\\color{blue}0$ after the decimal point in the dividend. We also add a decimal point in the quotient, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\,}r} & 121\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{.}} \\\\ 5 & \\enclose{longdiv}{607\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{.}}\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{00.0}}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & 10\\phantom{0.0}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & \\underline{-10\\phantom{0.0}}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & 07\\phantom{.0}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{.0}}\\quad\\color{lightgray}{\\downarrow} \\\\ & {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{lightgray}{0}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow we complete the division, as usual, stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\,}r} & 121.4 \\\\ 5 & \\enclose{longdiv}{607.0} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{00.0}} \\\\ & 10\\phantom{0.0} \\\\ & \\underline{-10\\phantom{0.0}} \\\\ & 07\\phantom{.0} \\\\ & \\underline{-5\\phantom{.0}} \\\\ & 20 \\\\ & \\underline{-20} \\\\ & \\boxed{0}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n607 \\div 5 = 121.4 \\,\n$$\n\nWe conclude that Cameron spent $\\$121.40$ each day."] [:db/cas 17592186138263 :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to estimate the value of $997 \\operatorname{div}  19$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest ten:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}997 & ÷ & 19 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1,000}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{20}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo we need to find the value of $1,000 \\operatorname{div}  20$, which can be written as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,000}{20}\n$$\n\nBy dividing the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,000 \\operatorname{div}  10}{\\, \\, \\, \\, \\,\\, \\, \\, 20 \\operatorname{div}  10} = \\dfrac{100}{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as\n\n$$\n100 \\operatorname{div}  2 = 50\n$$\n\nTherefore, $997 \\operatorname{div}  19$ is about $50$.\n\nSo, it will take about $50$ minutes to fill the pool." "Here, we need to estimate the value of $997 \\div 19$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest ten:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}997 & ÷ & 19 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{1,000}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{20}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo we need to find the value of $1,000 \\div 20$, which can be written as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,000}{20}\n$$\n\nBy dividing the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,000 \\div 10}{\\, \\, \\, \\, \\,\\, \\, \\, 20 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{100}{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as\n\n$$\n100 \\div 2 = 50\n$$\n\nTherefore, $997 \\div 19$ is about $50$.\n\nSo, it will take about $50$ minutes to fill the pool."] [:db/cas 17592186138264 :question/problem "Estimate the value of $52,284 \\operatorname{div}  841$ by rounding the dividend to the nearest thousand and rounding the divisor to the nearest hundred." "Estimate the value of $52,284 \\div 841$ by rounding the dividend to the nearest thousand and rounding the divisor to the nearest hundred."] [:db/cas 17592186138264 :question/worked-solution "We round the dividend to the nearest thousand:\n$52,284 \\rightarrow 52,000$\n\nAlso, we round the divisor to the nearest hundred:\n$841 \\rightarrow 800$\n\nSo we need to find the value of $52,000 \\operatorname{div}  80