inator by $10$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{730 \\operatorname{div}  10}{20 \\operatorname{div}  10} = \\dfrac{73}{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as $73 \\operatorname{div}  2$.\n\nComputing the quotient, we obtain:\n\n| $\\,\\,3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\,. \\,} 5$$2$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,. \\,} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $727 \\operatorname{div}  22$ is approximately $36.5 \\,$." "First, we round both numbers to the nearest ten:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}727 & ÷ & 22 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{730}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{20}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo we need to find the value of $730 \\div 20$, which we write as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{730}{20}\n$$\n\nDividing the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{730 \\div 10}{20 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{73}{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as $73 \\div 2$.\n\nComputing the quotient, we obtain:\n\n| $\\,\\,3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\,. \\,} 5$$2$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,. \\,} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $727 \\div 22$ is approximately $36.5 \\,$."] [:db/cas 17592186138266 :question/problem "By rounding both numbers to the nearest hundred, estimate the value of $142,773 \\operatorname{div}  398$." "By rounding both numbers to the nearest hundred, estimate the value of $142,773 \\div 398$."] [:db/cas 17592186138266 :question/worked-solution "We round both numbers to the nearest hundred:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}142,773 & ÷ & 398 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{142,800}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{400}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo we need to find the value of $142,800 \\operatorname{div}  400$, which we can write as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{142,800}{400}\n$$\n\nDividing the numerator and denominator by $100$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{142,800 \\operatorname{div}  100}{400 \\operatorname{div}  100} = \\dfrac{1,428}{4}\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as $1,428 \\operatorname{div}  4$.\n\nComputing the quotient, we obtain:\n\n| $\\,\\, {\\:\\phantom{\\mid} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$2$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$2$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $142,773 \\operatorname{div}  398$ is approximately $357$." "We round both numbers to the nearest hundred:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}142,773 & ÷ & 398 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{142,800}} & ÷ & {\\color{blue}{400}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo we need to find the value of $142,800 \\div 400$, which we can write as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{142,800}{400}\n$$\n\nDividing the numerator and denominator by $100$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{142,800 \\div 100}{400 \\div 100} = \\dfrac{1,428}{4}\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as $1,428 \\div 4$.\n\nComputing the quotient, we obtain:\n\n| $\\,\\, {\\:\\phantom{\\mid} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$2$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$2$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $142,773 \\div 398$ is approximately $357$."] [:db/cas 17592186138267 :question/problem "Find the quotient and the remainder of $504 \\operatorname{div}  25$." "Find the quotient and the remainder of $504 \\div 25$."] [:db/cas 17592186138267 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $25$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the hundreds and tens. Notice that $25$ goes ${\\color{blue}2}$ times into ${\\color{red}50}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}2$$25$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}5} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$0$$\\color{lightgray}\\,\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}4$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $25 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 50$Subtract: ${\\color{red}50} - 50 = 0$Bring down: $4$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $25$ goes ${\\color{blue}0}$ times into ${\\color{red}4}$. So, ${\\color{red}4}$ is the remainder and we get:\n\n| $2$$\\color{blue}0$$25$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$0$$0$$\\color{red}4$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $504$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n504 \\operatorname{div}  25 = 20\\,\\text{R}\\,4 \\,\n$$\n\nSo the quotient is $20$ and the remainder is $4$." "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $25$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the hundreds and tens. Notice that $25$ goes ${\\color{blue}2}$ times into ${\\color{red}50}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}2$$25$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}5} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$0$$\\color{lightgray}\\,\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}4$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $25 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 50$Subtract: ${\\color{red}50} - 50 = 0$Bring down: $4$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $25$ goes ${\\color{blue}0}$ times into ${\\color{red}4}$. So, ${\\color{red}4}$ is the remainder and we get:\n\n| $2$$\\color{blue}0$$25$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$0$$0$$\\color{red}4$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $504$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n504 \\div 25 = 20\\,\\text{R}\\,4 \\,\n$$\n\nSo the quotient is $20$ and the remainder is $4$."] [:db/cas 17592186138268 :question/worked-solution "To find out how many bottles the store owner will use, we need to divide $864$ by $24$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $24$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the hundreds and tens. Notice that $24$ goes ${\\color{blue}3}$ times into ${\\color{red}86}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}3$$24$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}8} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}6} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\,\\downarrow$$1$$4$$\\color{lightgray}4$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}3$Multiply: $24 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 72$Subtract: ${\\color{red}86} - 72 = 14$Bring down: $4$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $24$ goes ${\\color{blue}6}$ times into ${\\color{red}144}$. So, we get:\n\n| $3$$\\color{blue}6$$24$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$2$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}4$$\\color{red}4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$4$$\\phantom{0}$$0$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}6$Multiply: $24 \\times {\\color{blue}6} = 144$Subtract: ${\\color{red}144} - 144 = 0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $864$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n864 \\operatorname{div}  24 = 36\n$$\n\nSo the store owner will need a total of $36$ bottles to bottle all the olive oil." "To find out how many bottles the store owner will use, we need to divide $864$ by $24$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $24$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the hundreds and tens. Notice that $24$ goes ${\\color{blue}3}$ times into ${\\color{red}86}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}3$$24$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}8} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}6} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\,\\downarrow$$1$$4$$\\color{lightgray}4$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}3$Multiply: $24 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 72$Subtract: ${\\color{red}86} - 72 = 14$Bring down: $4$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $24$ goes ${\\color{blue}6}$ times into ${\\color{red}144}$. So, we get:\n\n| $3$$\\color{blue}6$$24$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$2$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}4$$\\color{red}4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$4$$\\phantom{0}$$0$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}6$Multiply: $24 \\times {\\color{blue}6} = 144$Subtract: ${\\color{red}144} - 144 = 0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $864$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n864 \\div 24 = 36\n$$\n\nSo the store owner will need a total of $36$ bottles to bottle all the olive oil."] [:db/cas 17592186138269 :question/problem "What is the **remainder** of $987 \\operatorname{div}  17$?" "What is the **remainder** of $987 \\div 17$?"] [:db/cas 17592186138270 :question/problem "$518 \\operatorname{div}  74=$" "$518 \\div 74=$"] [:db/cas 17592186138270 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $74$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nSince $74$ does not go into ${\\color{red}51}$, we consider the hundreds, tens and ones altogether. Notice that $74$ goes ${\\color{blue}7}$ times into ${\\color{red}518}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}7$$74$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}5} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}1} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}8}}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$1$$8$$\\phantom{0}$$0$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}7$Multiply: $74 \\times {\\color{blue}7} = 518$Subtract: ${\\color{red}518} - 518 = 0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $518$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n518 \\operatorname{div}  74 = 7\n$$" "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $74$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nSince $74$ does not go into ${\\color{red}51}$, we consider the hundreds, tens and ones altogether. Notice that $74$ goes ${\\color{blue}7}$ times into ${\\color{red}518}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}7$$74$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}5} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}1} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}8}}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$1$$8$$\\phantom{0}$$0$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}7$Multiply: $74 \\times {\\color{blue}7} = 518$Subtract: ${\\color{red}518} - 518 = 0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $518$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n518 \\div 74 = 7\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186138271 :question/worked-solution "To find out the length of each piece of cable, we need to divide $1,560$ by $15$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $15$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the thousands and hundreds. Notice that $15$ goes ${\\color{blue}1}$ time into ${\\color{red}15}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$15$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}1} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}5} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$5$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $15 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 15$Subtract: ${\\color{red}15} - 15 = 0$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nSince $15$ goes ${\\color{blue}0}$ times into ${\\color{red}6}$, we need to bring one more digit down. So, we get:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}0$$15$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$5$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{red}6$$\\color{lightgray}0$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}0$Bring down: $0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $15$ goes ${\\color{blue}4}$ times into ${\\color{red}60}$. So, we get:\n\n| $1$$0$$\\color{blue}4$$15$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$5$$0$$\\color{red}6$$\\color{red}0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$0$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}4$Multiply: $15 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 60$Subtract: ${\\color{red}60} - 60 = 0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $1,560$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1,560 \\operatorname{div}  15 = 104\n$$\n\nThis means that each piece of cable is $104$ inches long." "To find out the length of each piece of cable, we need to divide $1,560$ by $15$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $15$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the thousands and hundreds. Notice that $15$ goes ${\\color{blue}1}$ time into ${\\color{red}15}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$15$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}1} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}5} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$5$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $15 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 15$Subtract: ${\\color{red}15} - 15 = 0$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nSince $15$ goes ${\\color{blue}0}$ times into ${\\color{red}6}$, we need to bring one more digit down. So, we get:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}0$$15$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$5$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{red}6$$\\color{lightgray}0$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}0$Bring down: $0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $15$ goes ${\\color{blue}4}$ times into ${\\color{red}60}$. So, we get:\n\n| $1$$0$$\\color{blue}4$$15$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$5$$0$$\\color{red}6$$\\color{red}0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$0$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}4$Multiply: $15 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 60$Subtract: ${\\color{red}60} - 60 = 0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $1,560$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1,560 \\div 15 = 104\n$$\n\nThis means that each piece of cable is $104$ inches long."] [:db/cas 17592186138272 :question/problem "What is the **quotient** of $7,368 \\operatorname{div}  73$?" "What is the **quotient** of $7,368 \\div 73$?"] [:db/cas 17592186138272 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $73$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the thousands and hundreds. Notice that $73$ goes ${\\color{blue}1}$ time into ${\\color{red}73}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$73$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}7} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}3} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $73 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 73$Subtract: ${\\color{red}73} - 73 = 0$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nSince $73$ goes ${\\color{blue}0}$ times into ${\\color{red}6}$, we need to bring one more digit down. So, we get:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}0$$73$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{red}6$$\\color{lightgray}8$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}0$Bring down: $8$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $73$ goes ${\\color{blue}0}$ times into ${\\color{red}68}$. So, ${\\color{red}68}$ is the remainder and we get:\n\n| $1$$0$$\\color{blue}0$$73$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$3$$0$$\\color{red}6$$\\color{red}8$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $7,368$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7,368 \\operatorname{div}  73 = 100\\,\\text{R}\\,68\n$$\n\nSo, the quotient is $100$." "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $73$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the thousands and hundreds. Notice that $73$ goes ${\\color{blue}1}$ time into ${\\color{red}73}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$73$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}7} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}3} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $73 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 73$Subtract: ${\\color{red}73} - 73 = 0$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nSince $73$ goes ${\\color{blue}0}$ times into ${\\color{red}6}$, we need to bring one more digit down. So, we get:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}0$$73$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{red}6$$\\color{lightgray}8$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}0$Bring down: $8$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $73$ goes ${\\color{blue}0}$ times into ${\\color{red}68}$. So, ${\\color{red}68}$ is the remainder and we get:\n\n| $1$$0$$\\color{blue}0$$73$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$3$$0$$\\color{red}6$$\\color{red}8$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $7,368$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7,368 \\div 73 = 100\\,\\text{R}\\,68\n$$\n\nSo, the quotient is $100$."] [:db/cas 17592186138273 :question/problem "Find the **remainder** of $3,528 \\operatorname{div}  34$." "Find the **remainder** of $3,528 \\div 34$."] [:db/cas 17592186138273 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $34$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the thousands and hundreds. Notice that $34$ goes ${\\color{blue}1}$ time into ${\\color{red}35}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$34$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}3} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}5} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}2$ | Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $34 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 34$Subtract: ${\\color{red}35} - 34 = 1$Bring down: $2$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nSince $34$ goes ${\\color{blue}0}$ times into ${\\color{red}12}$, we need to bring one more digit down. So, we get:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}0$$34$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{lightgray}8$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}0$Bring down: $8$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $34$ goes ${\\color{blue}3}$ times into ${\\color{red}128}$. So, we get:\n\n| $1$$0$$\\color{blue}3$$34$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$4$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$2$$2$$6$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}3$Multiply: $34 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 102$Subtract: ${\\color{red}128} - 102 = 26$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $3,528$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3,528 \\operatorname{div}  34 = 103\\,\\text{R}\\,26 \\,\n$$\n\nSo, the remainder is ${\\color{black}26}$." "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $34$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the thousands and hundreds. Notice that $34$ goes ${\\color{blue}1}$ time into ${\\color{red}35}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$34$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}3} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}5} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}2$ | Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $34 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 34$Subtract: ${\\color{red}35} - 34 = 1$Bring down: $2$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nSince $34$ goes ${\\color{blue}0}$ times into ${\\color{red}12}$, we need to bring one more digit down. So, we get:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}0$$34$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{lightgray}8$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}0$Bring down: $8$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $34$ goes ${\\color{blue}3}$ times into ${\\color{red}128}$. So, we get:\n\n| $1$$0$$\\color{blue}3$$34$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$4$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$2$$2$$6$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}3$Multiply: $34 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 102$Subtract: ${\\color{red}128} - 102 = 26$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $3,528$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3,528 \\div 34 = 103\\,\\text{R}\\,26 \\,\n$$\n\nSo, the remainder is ${\\color{black}26}$."] [:db/cas 17592186138274 :question/problem "Find the **quotient** of $3,755 \\operato