tor by $10$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7.47 \\times 10}{1.2 \\times 10} = \\dfrac{74.7}{12}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $74.7 \\operatorname{div}  12$.\n\nNext, we write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\,}r} &  \\\\ 12 & \\enclose{longdiv}{74{\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{.}}}7}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\,}r} & \\phantom{6}{\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{.}}}\\phantom{225} \\\\ 12 & \\enclose{longdiv}{74{\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{.}}}7}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process, as usual, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\,}r} & 6.225 \\\\ 12 & \\enclose{longdiv}{74.700} \\\\ & \\underline{-72\\phantom{.000}} \\\\ & 27\\phantom{00} \\\\ & \\underline{-24\\phantom{00}} \\\\ & 30\\phantom{0} \\\\ & \\underline{-24\\phantom{0}} \\\\ & 60 \\\\ & \\underline{-60} \\\\ & 0\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n7.47 \\operatorname{div}  1.2 = 6.225\n$$\n\nTherefore, Dave was paid $\\$6.225$ per hour." "To determine Dave's hourly rate, we have to calculate\n\n$$\n7.47 \\div 1.2\n$$\n\nWe can write this division problem as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7.47}{1.2}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7.47 \\times 10}{1.2 \\times 10} = \\dfrac{74.7}{12}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $74.7 \\div 12$.\n\nNext, we write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\,}r} &  \\\\ 12 & \\enclose{longdiv}{74{\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{.}}}7}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\,}r} & \\phantom{6}{\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{.}}}\\phantom{225} \\\\ 12 & \\enclose{longdiv}{74{\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{.}}}7}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process, as usual, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\,}r} & 6.225 \\\\ 12 & \\enclose{longdiv}{74.700} \\\\ & \\underline{-72\\phantom{.000}} \\\\ & 27\\phantom{00} \\\\ & \\underline{-24\\phantom{00}} \\\\ & 30\\phantom{0} \\\\ & \\underline{-24\\phantom{0}} \\\\ & 60 \\\\ & \\underline{-60} \\\\ & 0\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n7.47 \\div 1.2 = 6.225\n$$\n\nTherefore, Dave was paid $\\$6.225$ per hour."] [:db/cas 17592186138678 :question/problem "What is $3 \\operatorname{div}  1, 000$?" "What is $3 \\div 1, 000$?"] [:db/cas 17592186138679 :question/problem "What is $0.32 \\operatorname{div}  1, 000$?" "What is $0.32 \\div 1, 000$?"] [:db/cas 17592186138921 :question/problem "From **left to right**, what are the missing digits in the place value chart below for $2.1 \\operatorname{div}  100$?\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $0$ | **.** | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |" "From **left to right**, what are the missing digits in the place value chart below for $2.1 \\div 100$?\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $0$ | **.** | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186138922 :question/problem "Find the place value chart of $53.2 \\operatorname{div}  10$." "Find the place value chart of $53.2 \\div 10$."] [:db/cas 17592186138961 :question/problem "What is $3 \\operatorname{div}  10$?" "What is $3 \\div 10$?"] [:db/cas 17592186138962 :question/problem "What is $10.8 \\operatorname{div}  10$?" "What is $10.8 \\div 10$?"] [:db/cas 17592186138963 :question/problem "What is $32.28 \\operatorname{div}  10$?" "What is $32.28 \\div 10$?"] [:db/cas 17592186138964 :question/problem "What is $604 \\operatorname{div}  100$?" "What is