"What is $0.7 \\operatorname{div}  10$?" "What is $0.7 \\div 10$?"] [:db/cas 17592186139623 :tutorial/content "We can use place value charts to divide decimals by $10$. For instance, to find the value of\n\n$$\n28 \\operatorname{div}  10\n$$\n\nwe perform the following steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Write down the place value chart for $28$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n**Step 2.** Move all digits by one place to the *right.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n28\\operatorname{div}  10 = 2.8 \\,\n$$" "We can use place value charts to divide decimals by $10$. For instance, to find the value of\n\n$$\n28 \\div 10\n$$\n\nwe perform the following steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Write down the place value chart for $28$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n**Step 2.** Move all digits by one place to the *right.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n28\\div 10 = 2.8 \\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139624 :tutorial/content "When dividing by a power of ten, we shift the digits in the place value chart to the *right* by the number of zeros in the power of ten.\n\nTo illustrate, let's find the value of $47.2 \\operatorname{div}  100$.\n\n**Step 1.** Write down the place value chart for $47.2$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n**Step 2.** Determine the number of places to move. We're dividing by $100$, which has $\\color{blue}2$ zeros, so we move all values to the *right* by $\\color{blue}2$ places:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n**Step 3.** Put zero in all empty cells that are to the right of the decimal point. We can also put a zero in the ones place if it is empty.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n47.2\\operatorname{div}  100 = 0.472 \\,\n$$" "When dividing by a power of ten, we shift the digits in the place value chart to the *right* by the number of zeros in the power of ten.\n\nTo illustrate, let's find the value of $47.2 \\div 100$.\n\n**Step 1.** Write down the place value chart for $47.2$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n**Step 2.** Determine the number of places to move. We're dividing by $100$, which has $\\color{blue}2$ zeros, so we move all values to the *right* by $\\color{blue}2$ places:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n**Step 3.** Put zero in all empty cells that are to the right of the decimal point. We can also put a zero in the ones place if it is empty.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n47.2\\div 100 = 0.472 \\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139625 :tutorial/content "When we divide two whole numbers using long division, we often get a remainder. In this lesson, we will learn how to use long division to express the quotient and remainder as a decimal.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute $4 \\operatorname{div}  5$. We start by writing down $4\\operatorname{div}  5$ using long division notation:\n\n| $\\,\\,0$$5$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{\\color{red}4}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNotice that $\\color{red}4$ is less than the divisor $5$. But we can proceed further by adding a $\\color{blue}0$ after the decimal point in the dividend. We also add a decimal point in the quotient, as shown below:\n\n| $\\,0 {\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}}$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}} {\\color{blue}0}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow we complete the division, as usual, stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0$:\n\n| $\\,0 {\\,.\\,} 8$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\,. \\,} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 0.8\\,\n$$" "When we divide two whole numbers using long division, we often get a remainder. In this lesson, we will learn how to use long division to express the quotient and remainder as a decimal.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute $4 \\div 5$. We start by writing down $4\\div 5$ using long division notation:\n\n| $\\,\\,0$$5$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{\\color{red}4}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNotice that $\\color{red}4$ is less than the divisor $5$. But we can proceed further by adding a $\\color{blue}0$ after the decimal point in the dividend. We also add a decimal point in the quotient, as shown below:\n\n| $\\,0 {\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}}$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}} {\\color{blue}0}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow we complete the division, as usual, stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0$:\n\n| $\\,0 {\\,.\\,} 8$$5$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\,. \\,} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4 \\div 5 = 0.8\\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139628 :tutorial/content "We can use our knowledge of rounding to estimate multi-digit division. To illustrate, let's estimate the value of\n\n$$\n960 \\operatorname{div}  64\n$$\n\nby rounding the divisor to the nearest ten.\n\nThe divisor is $64$, and rounding to the nearest ten we have\n\n$$\n64 \\rightarrow 60 \\,\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of\n\n$$\n960 \\operatorname{div}  60\n$$\n\nwhich we write as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{960}{60}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the fraction, we can divide the numerator and denominator by $10$ to remove the zeros from the end of both numbers:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{960 \\operatorname{div}  10}{60 \\operatorname{div}  10} = \\dfrac{96}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, we just need to compute\n\n$$\n96 \\operatorname{div}  6 \\,\n$$\n\nWe use long division, as follows:\n\n| $\\,1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$3$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $960 \\operatorname{div}  64$ is approximately $16$." "We can use our knowledge of rounding to estimate multi-digit division. To illustrate, let's estimate the value of\n\n$$\n960 \\div 64\n$$\n\nby rounding the divisor to the nearest ten.\n\nThe divisor is $64$, and rounding to the nearest ten we have\n\n$$\n64 \\rightarrow 60 \\,\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of\n\n$$\n960 \\div 60\n$$\n\nwhich we write as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{960}{60}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the fraction, we can divide the numerator and denominator by $10$ to remove the zeros from the end of both numbers:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{960 \\div 10}{60 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{96}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, we just need to compute\n\n$$\n96 \\div 6 \\,\n$$\n\nWe use long division, as follows:\n\n| $\\,1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6$$6$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$3$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $960 \\div 64$ is approximately $16$."] [:db/cas 17592186139629 :tutorial/content "We can use the standard algorithm for division to divide three-digit-numbers by two-digit numbers.\n\nFor example, what is the value of $528 \\operatorname{div}  24$?\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $24$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we would consider the hundreds, but $24$ doesn't go into $\\color{red}5$. So instead, we consider the hundreds and tens.\nNotice that $24$ goes ${\\color{blue}2}$ times into ${\\color{red}52}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}2$$24$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}5} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}2} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\,\\downarrow$$4$$\\color{lightgray}8$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $24 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 48$Subtract: ${\\color{red}52} - 48 = 4$Bring down: $8$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $24$ goes ${\\color{blue}2}$ times into ${\\color{red}48}$. So, we get:\n\n| $2$$\\color{blue}2$$24$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\color{red}4$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\phantom{0}$$0$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $24 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 48$Subtract: ${\\color{red}48} - 48 = 0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n528 \\operatorname{div}  24 = 22 \\,\n$$" "We can use the standard algorithm for division to divide three-digit-numbers by two-digit numbers.\n\nFor example, what is the value of $528 \\div 24$?\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $24$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we would consider the hundreds, but $24$ doesn't go into $\\color{red}5$. So instead, we consider the hundreds and tens.\nNotice that $24$ goes ${\\color{blue}2}$ times into ${\\color{red}52}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}2$$24$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}5} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}2} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\,\\downarrow$$4$$\\color{lightgray}8$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $24 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 48$Subtract: ${\\color{red}52} - 48 = 4$Bring down: $8$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $24$ goes ${\\color{blue}2}$ times into ${\\color{red}48}$. So, we get:\n\n| $2$$\\color{blue}2$$24$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\color{red}4$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\phantom{0}$$0$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $24 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 48$Subtract: ${\\color{red}48} - 48 = 0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n528 \\div 24 = 22 \\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139630 :tutorial/content "We can use the standard algorithm for division to divide four-digit numbers by two-digit numbers.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use the algorithm to find the value of $4,564 \\operatorname{div}  14$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $14$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the thousands and hundreds. Notice that $14$ goes ${\\color{blue}3}$ times into ${\\color{red}45}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}3$$14$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}5} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$3$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}3$Multiply: $14 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 42$Subtract: ${\\color{red}45} - 42 = 3$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, notice that $14$ goes ${\\color{blue}2}$ times into ${\\color{red}36}$. So, we get:\n\n| $3$$\\color{blue}2$$14$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}3$$\\color{red}6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$8$$\\color{lightgray}4$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $14 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 28$Subtract: ${\\color{red}36} - 28 = 8$Bring down: $4$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $14$ goes ${\\color{blue}6}$ times into ${\\color{red}84}$. So, we get:\n\n| $3$$2$$\\color{blue}6$$14$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$2$$3$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$\\color{red}8$$\\color{red}4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$4$$0$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}6$Multiply: $14 \\times {\\color{blue}6} = 84$Subtract: ${\\color{red}84} - 84 = 0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, and the remainder is $0$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4,564 \\operatorname{div}  14 = 326\n$$" "We can use the standard algorithm for division to divide four-digit numbers by two-digit numbers.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use the algorithm to find the value of $4,564 \\div 14$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $14$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the thousands and hundreds. Notice that $14$ goes ${\\color{blue}3}$ times into ${\\color{red}45}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}3$$14$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}5} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$3$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}3$Multiply: $14 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 42$Subtract: ${\\color{red}45} - 42 = 3$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, notice that $14$ goes ${\\color{blue}2}$ times into ${\\color{red}36}$. So, we get:\n\n| $3$$\\color{blue}2$$14$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}3$$\\color{red}6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$8$$\\color{lightgray}4$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $14 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 28$Subtract: ${\\color{red}36} - 28 = 8$Bring down: $4$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $14$ goes ${\\color{blue}6}$ times into ${\\color{red}84}$. So, we get:\n\n| $3$$2$$\\color{blue}6$$14$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$2$$3$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$\\color{red}8$$\\color{red}4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$4$$0$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}6$Multiply: $14 \\times {\\color{blue}6} = 84$Subtract: ${\\color{red}84} - 84 = 0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, and the remainder is $0$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4,564 \\div 14 = 326\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139631 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we need to consider the thousands, hundreds, *and* tens digits in the first step when applying the standard algorithm.\n\nTo illustrate, let's calculate $1,967 \\operatorname{div}  28$. We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $28$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNotice that $28$ does not go into $19$. So, we need to consider the thousands, hundreds, *and* tens.\n\nNow, we see that $28$ goes ${\\color{blue}7}$ times into ${\\color{red}196}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}7$$28$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}1} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}9} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}6} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$9$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}7$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}7$Multiply: $28 \\times {\\color{blue}7} = 196$Subtract: ${\\color{red}196} - 196 = 0$Bring down: $7$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $28$ goes ${\\color{blue}0}$ times into ${\\color{red}7}$. So $\\color{red}7$ is the remainder and we get:\n\n| $7$$\\color{blue}0$$28$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$9$$6$$0$$\\color{red}7$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1,967 \\operatorname{div}  28 = 70\\,\\text{R}\\,7 \\,\n$$" "Sometimes, we need to consider the thousands, hundreds, *and* tens digits in the first step when applying the standard algorithm.\n\nTo illustrate, let's calculate $1,967 \\div 28$. We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $28$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNotice that $28$ does not go into $19$. So, we need to consider the thousands, hundreds, *and* tens.\n\nNow, we see that $28$ goes ${\\color{blue}7}$ times into ${\\color{red}196}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}7$$28$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}1} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}9} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}6} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$9$$6$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}7$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}7$Multiply: $28 \\times {\\color{blue}7} = 196$Subtract: ${\\color{red}196} - 196 = 0$Bring down: $7$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $28$ goes ${\\color{blue}0}$ times into ${\\color{red}7}$. So $\\color{red}7$ is the remainder and we get:\n\n| $7$$\\color{blue}0$$28$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$9$$6$$0$$\\color{red}7$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1,967 \\div 28 = 70\\,\\text{R}\\,7 \\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139632 :tutorial/content "To estimate the result of dividing two decimals, we can round both decimals before we divide.\n\nFor example, let's estimate the value of $60.1 \\operatorname{div}  15.4$ by rounding each decimal to the nearest whole number.\n\nFirst, we round both decimals to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}60.1 & ÷ & 15.4 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ 60 & ÷ & 15\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of $60 \\operatorname{div}  15.$ Computing the quotient, we get:\n\n$$\n60 \\operatorname{div}  15 = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $60.1 \\operatorname{div}  15.4$ is approximately $4.$" "To estimate the result of dividing two decimals, we can round both decimals before we divide.\n\nFor example, let's estimate the value of $60.1 \\div 15.4$ by rounding each decimal to the nearest whole number.\n\nFirst, we round both decimals to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}60.1 & ÷ & 15.4 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ 60 & ÷ & 15\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of $60 \\div 15.$ Computing the quotient, we get:\n\n$$\n60 \\div 15 = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $60.1 \\div 15.4$ is approximately $4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186139633 :tutorial/content "We can use the standard algorithm to divide three-digit decimals by two-digit decimals.\n\nAs an example, let's solve the following division problem:\n\n$$\n92.4 \\operatorname{div}  22\n$$\n\nWe start by writing down our problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $22$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 4}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\: \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$22$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 4}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process, as usual, we get:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\!\\!. 2$$22$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\,. \\,} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$8$$4$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$4$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n92.4 \\operatorname{div}  22 = 4.2\\,\n$$" "We can use the standard algorithm to divide three-digit decimals by two-digit decimals.\n\nAs an example, let's solve the following division problem:\n\n$$\n92.4 \\div 22\n$$\n\nWe start by writing down our problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $22$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 4}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\: \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$22$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 4}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process, as usual, we get:\n\n| $4$$\\!\\!\\!. 2$$22$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\,. \\,} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$8$$4$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$4$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n92.4 \\div 22 = 4.2\\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139634 :tutorial/content "Using the standard algorithm requires the divisor to be a whole number. However, we can often manipulate a division problem using our understanding of fractions to give an equivalent problem where the standard algorithm *can* be applied.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following division problem:\n\n$$\n8.82 \\operatorname{div}  2.5\n$$\n\nWe cannot apply the standard algorithm because the divisor $(2.5)$ is not a whole number.\n\nHowever, if we express this division problem as a fraction, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8.82}{2.5}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to create a whole number in the denominator. To do this, we make an equivalent fraction by multiplying both the numerator and denominator by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8.82 \\times 10}{2.5 \\times 10} = \\dfrac{88.2}{25}\n$$\n\nSo, instead of our original problem, we can now solve the equivalent problem\n\n$$\n88.2 \\operatorname{div}  25\n$$\n\nLet's write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $25$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}8 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\: {\\:\\phantom{\\mid \\mid}} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid \\mid}} {\\phantom{\\mid \\mid}} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$25$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}8 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process, as usual, we get:\n\n| $3$$\\!\\!. 5$$2$$8$$\\!25$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\,. \\,}2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$5$$1$$3$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$5$$7$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$0$$2$$0$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n8.82 \\operatorname{div}  2.5 = 3.528 \\,\n$$" "Using the standard algorithm requires the divisor to be a whole number. However, we can often manipulate a division problem using our understanding of fractions to give an equivalent problem where the standard algorithm *can* be applied.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following division problem:\n\n$$\n8.82 \\div 2.5\n$$\n\nWe cannot apply the standard algorithm because the divisor $(2.5)$ is not a whole number.\n\nHowever, if we express this division problem as a fraction, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8.82}{2.5}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to create a whole number in the denominator. To do this, we make an equivalent fraction by multiplying both the numerator and denominator by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8.82 \\times 10}{2.5 \\times 10} = \\dfrac{88.2}{25}\n$$\n\nSo, instead of our original problem, we can now solve the equivalent problem\n\n$$\n88.2 \\div 25\n$$\n\nLet's write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $25$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}8 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\: {\\:\\phantom{\\mid \\mid}} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid \\mid}} {\\phantom{\\mid \\mid}} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$25$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}8 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process, as usual, we get:\n\n| $3$$\\!\\!. 5$$2$$8$$\\!25$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\,. \\,}2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$5$$1$$3$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$5$$7$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$0$$2$$0$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n8.82 \\div 2.5 = 3.528 \\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139677 :tutorial/content "To convert an improper fraction to an equivalent mixed number, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Divide the numerator by the denominator.\n\n**Step 2**: Express the resulting division problem as a mixed number.\n\nLet's use this method to convert the following improper fraction to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{14}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide $14$ by ${\\color{blue}{3}}$:\n\n$$\n14\\operatorname{div}  3 = 4 \\, \\textrm{R}\\,{\\color{red}2}\n$$\n\nSo, our division gives $4$ wholes, and we're left with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{14}{3} = 4 \\, \\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{14}{3}\n$$\n\nexpressed as a mixed number is\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac23\n$$" "To convert an improper fraction to an equivalent mixed number, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Divide the numerator by the denominator.\n\n**Step 2**: Express the resulting division problem as a mixed number.\n\nLet's use this method to convert the following improper fraction to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{14}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide $14$ by ${\\color{blue}{3}}$:\n\n$$\n14\\div 3 = 4 \\, \\textrm{R}\\,{\\color{red}2}\n$$\n\nSo, our division gives $4$ wholes, and we're left with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{14}{3} = 4 \\, \\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{14}{3}\n$$\n\nexpressed as a mixed number is\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac23\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139678 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, it's necessary to reduce the fraction part to the lowest terms.\n\nFor instance, let's represent the following fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{4}\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide $18$ by ${\\color{blue}{4}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{4} = 4 \\, \\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nSo, our division gives $4$ wholes, and we're left with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nWe can reduce the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nto its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{4} &= \\frac{2 \\operatorname{div}  2}{4 \\operatorname{div}  2} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our mixed number is equivalent to\n\n$$\n4 \\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" "Sometimes, it's necessary to reduce the fraction part to the lowest terms.\n\nFor instance, let's represent the following fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{4}\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide $18$ by ${\\color{blue}{4}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{4} = 4 \\, \\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nSo, our division gives $4$ wholes, and we're left with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nWe can reduce the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nto its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{4} &= \\frac{2 \\div 2}{4 \\div 2} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our mixed number is equivalent to\n\n$$\n4 \\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139690 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, when we convert a decimal into a fraction, the fraction is not in its simplest form.\n\nFor instance, notice that $0.4$ has $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place. So, we can write it as a decimal fraction with $\\color{blue}10$ in the denominator:\n\n$$\n0.4 = \\dfrac{4}{10}\n$$\n\nHowever, this fraction is not in its simplest form. To simplify, we divide the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{10} &= \\frac{4 \\operatorname{div}  2}{10 \\operatorname{div}  2} = \\frac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.4$ as a fraction in its simplest form is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$" "Sometimes, when we convert a decimal into a fraction, the fraction is not in its simplest form.\n\nFor instance, notice that $0.4$ has $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place. So, we can write it as a decimal fraction with $\\color{blue}10$ in the denominator:\n\n$$\n0.4 = \\dfrac{4}{10}\n$$\n\nHowever, this fraction is not in its simplest form. To simplify, we divide the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{10} &= \\frac{4 \\div 2}{10 \\div 2} = \\frac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.4$ as a fraction in its simplest form is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139693 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, when we add two mixed numbers, the sum of the fractional parts is an improper fraction. In those cases, we must convert the improper fraction to a mixed number. Then, we can add the whole numbers and fractions as usual.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute\n\n$$\n1 \\dfrac{2}{3} + 2 \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\n**Step 1**: Adding the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n1+2={\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now, we add the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}+\\dfrac{2}{3}=\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is an improper fraction. We can convert the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nto a mixed number in the usual way:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{div}  3 = 1\\,\\textrm{R}1 = {\\color{blue}{1\\,\\dfrac 1{3}}}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We combine the results to get our final answer:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{3}} + {\\color{blue}{1\\,\\dfrac 1{3}}} = 4\\,\\dfrac 1{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that we needed to add the whole numbers one final time." "Sometimes, when we add two mixed numbers, the sum of the fractional parts is an improper fraction. In those cases, we must convert the improper fraction to a mixed number. Then, we can add the whole numbers and fractions as usual.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute\n\n$$\n1 \\dfrac{2}{3} + 2 \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\n**Step 1**: Adding the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n1+2={\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now, we add the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}+\\dfrac{2}{3}=\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is an improper fraction. We can convert the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nto a mixed number in the usual way:\n\n$$\n4\\div 3 = 1\\,\\textrm{R}1 = {\\color{blue}{1\\,\\dfrac 1{3}}}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We combine the results to get our final answer:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{3}} + {\\color{blue}{1\\,\\dfrac 1{3}}} = 4\\,\\dfrac 1{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that we needed to add the whole numbers one final time."] [:db/cas 17592186139710 :tutorial/content "Remember that to *multiply* a decimal by $10$, we shift the decimal point one space to the *right*.\n\nSo, to *divide* a decimal by $10$, we shift the decimal point one place to the *left*.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's use this method to find the value of\n\n$$\n38.7 \\operatorname{div}  10\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down our number. We can attach as many leading zeros as we want to the whole number part:\n\n![](images/5b/5bac3fcfdb0abdb1550b3802da497b48eadc5389bd381f02a8fbbc4adca73391.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $10$. Since $10$ contains $1$ zero, we move the decimal point $1$ place to the left:\n\n![](images/c9/c9d94def2d8d7d8c52fd05ec0a50fd04cb2f755cc7ef242d727f6c4632444685.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/4f/4f4096b1615e8da780e3b6f7f68d76a0eb62057c4b79e9bbca9439feb3f88435.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n$3.87$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n38.7 \\operatorname{div}  10 = 3.87\n$$" "Remember that to *multiply* a decimal by $10$, we shift the decimal point one space to the *right*.\n\nSo, to *divide* a decimal by $10$, we shift the decimal point one place to the *left*.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's use this method to find the value of\n\n$$\n38.7 \\div 10\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down our number. We can attach as many leading zeros as we want to the whole number part:\n\n![](images/5b/5bac3fcfdb0abdb1550b3802da497b48eadc5389bd381f02a8fbbc4adca73391.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $10$. Since $10$ contains $1$ zero, we move the decimal point $1$ place to the left:\n\n![](images/c9/c9d94def2d8d7d8c52fd05ec0a50fd04cb2f755cc7ef242d727f6c4632444685.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/4f/4f4096b1615e8da780e3b6f7f68d76a0eb62057c4b79e9bbca9439feb3f88435.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n$3.87$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n38.7 \\div 10 = 3.87\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139712 :tutorial/content "To divide a decimal by a larger power of $10$, we shift the decimal point to the left by one place for each zero in the power of $10$.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use this method to find the value of\n\n$$\n9 \\operatorname{div}  100\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down our number. We can attach as many trailing zeros as we want to the whole number part.\n\n![](images/45/459955cbc7618333d1069460b3195918578e2b46b9afbffaddf719de10738aca.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $100$. Since $100$ contains $2$ zeros, we move the decimal point $2$ places to the left:\n\n![](images/69/69d5b5414b7b481d598ddde52a87e256f9e16b0e2ca83dcd62eb67fbcf948362.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/cc/ccef2b026c6f4567dd56ebed3a03fd2da0dcc3680aa4a750d5f56de4511f6dab.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n\n$$\n0.090 = 0.09\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n9 \\operatorname{div}  100 = 0.09\n$$" "To divide a decimal by a larger power of $10$, we shift the decimal point to the left by one place for each zero in the power of $10$.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use this method to find the value of\n\n$$\n9 \\div 100\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down our number. We can attach as many trailing zeros as we want to the whole number part.\n\n![](images/45/459955cbc7618333d1069460b3195918578e2b46b9afbffaddf719de10738aca.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $100$. Since $100$ contains $2$ zeros, we move the decimal point $2$ places to the left:\n\n![](images/69/69d5b5414b7b481d598ddde52a87e256f9e16b0e2ca83dcd62eb67fbcf948362.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/cc/ccef2b026c6f4567dd56ebed3a03fd2da0dcc3680aa4a750d5f56de4511f6dab.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n\n$$\n0.090 = 0.09\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n9 \\div 100 = 0.09\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139740 :tutorial/content "Throughout this lesson, we've practiced multiplying a decimal by a whole number using the following technique:\n\n- First, we multiply the numbers *without* the decimal parts.\n- Then, we count the decimal places in the decimal factor and create a number from our answer with this many decimal places.\n\nTo see why this works, consider the following example:\n\n$$\n6 \\times 3.2\n$$\n\nExpressing $3.2$ as a decimal fraction, we can write this product as follows:\n\n$$\n6 \\times \\dfrac{32}{10}\n$$\n\nSince every fraction is equivalent to a division, we can write this as\n\n$$\n6 \\times32 \\operatorname{div}  10\n$$\n\nLet's add parentheses to make this easier to read:\n\n$$\n\\left(6 \\times32\\right) \\operatorname{div}  10\n$$\n\nTherefore, to calculate $6\\times 3.2$, we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we calculate the value of $6\\times 32$.\n- Then, we divide the final answer by $10$. To do this, we take the answer from the first step and move the decimal point by one space to the left." "Throughout this lesson, we've practiced multiplying a decimal by a whole number using the following technique:\n\n- First, we multiply the numbers *without* the decimal parts.\n- Then, we count the decimal places in the decimal factor and create a number from our answer with this many decimal places.\n\nTo see why this works, consider the following example:\n\n$$\n6 \\times 3.2\n$$\n\nExpressing $3.2$ as a decimal fraction, we can write this product as follows:\n\n$$\n6 \\times \\dfrac{32}{10}\n$$\n\nSince every fraction is equivalent to a division, we can write this as\n\n$$\n6 \\times32 \\div 10\n$$\n\nLet's add parentheses to make this easier to read:\n\n$$\n\\left(6 \\times32\\right) \\div 10\n$$\n\nTherefore, to calculate $6\\times 3.2$, we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we calculate the value of $6\\times 32$.\n- Then, we divide the final answer by $10$. To do this, we take the answer from the first step and move the decimal point by one space to the left."] [:db/cas 17592186141022 :answer/value "6 \\operatorname{div}  4" "6 \\div 4"] [:db/cas 17592186141076 :answer/value "9 + [8 \\operatorname{div}  (1 + 3)]" "9 + [8 \\div (1 + 3)]"] [:db/cas 17592186141077 :answer/value "9 \\operatorname{div}  [8 \\operatorname{div}  (1 + 3)]" "9 \\div [8 \\div (1 + 3)]"] [:db/cas 17592186141078 :answer/value "9 \\cdot [8 \\operatorname{div}  (1 + 3)]" "9 \\cdot [8 \\div (1 + 3)]"] [:db/cas 17592186141079 :answer/value "9 - [8 \\operatorname{div}  (1 + 3)]" "9 - [8 \\div (1 + 3)]"] [:db/cas 17592186141080 :answer/value "[8 \\operatorname{div}  (1 + 3)] - 9" "[8 \\div (1 + 3)] - 9"] [:db/cas 17592186141142 :answer/value "9 - 1 \\operatorname{div}  4" "9 - 1 \\div 4"] [:db/cas 17592186141145 :answer/value "(9 - 1) \\operatorname{div}  4" "(9 - 1) \\div 4"] [:db/cas 17592186141148 :answer/value "(9 - 6) \\operatorname{div}  3" "(9 - 6) \\div 3"] [:db/cas 17592186141149 :answer/value "9 + 6 \\operatorname{div}  3" "9 + 6 \\div 3"] [:db/cas 17592186141150 :answer/value "(9 + 6) \\operatorname{div}  3" "(9 + 6) \\div 3"] [:db/cas 17592186141188 :answer/value "(3 + 5) \\operatorname{div}  2" "(3 + 5) \\div 2"] [:db/cas 17592186141220 :answer/value "5 \\cdot (6 + 2) \\operatorname{div}  4" "5 \\cdot (6 + 2) \\div 4"] [:db/cas 17592186141221 :answer/value "5 \\cdot [(6 + 2) \\operatorname{div}  4]" "5 \\cdot [(6 + 2) \\div 4]"] [:db/cas 17592186141222 :answer/value "5 + [(6 + 2) \\operatorname{div}  4]" "5 + [(6 + 2) \\div 4]"] [:db/cas 17592186141223 :answer/value "5 - [(6 + 2) \\operatorname{div}  4]" "5 - [(6 + 2) \\div 4]"] [:db/cas 17592186141224 :answer/value "5 \\operatorname{div}  [(6 + 2) \\operatorname{div}  4]" "5 \\div [(6 + 2) \\div 4]"] [:db/cas 17592186141226 :answer/value "[9 - (6 \\operator