|\n| $6$ | $\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$ |  |  |\n|  | $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $6$ |  |\n|  |  | $5$ | $2$ |\n|  | $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n|  |  |  | $4$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n112 \\div 6 = 18 \\,\\text{R}\\, {\\color{blue}{4}}\n$$\n\nThis means that Deborah will fill $18$ boxes and have ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ shirts left over.\n\nTo hold the leftover shirts, Deborah will need an additional box. Therefore, she will need\n\n$$\n18+1 =19\n$$\n\nboxes in total."] [:db/cas 17592186142495 :question/worked-solution "To find out the number of fully-filled packs the architect will receive, we need to compute the quotient of $563 \\operatorname{div}  4$.\n\nTo divide $563$ by $4$, we use long division:\n\n| $1$$4$$0$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$1$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$6$$0$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$0$${3}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n563 \\operatorname{div}  4 = {\\color{blue}{140}}\\,\\text{R}\\,3\n$$\n\nSo, the architect will receive ${\\color{blue}{140}}$ fully-filled packs." "To find out the number of fully-filled packs the architect will receive, we need to compute the quotient of $563 \\div 4$.\n\nTo divide $563$ by $4$, we use long division:\n\n| $1$$4$$0$$4$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$1$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$6$$0$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$0$${3}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n563 \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}{140}}\\,\\text{R}\\,3\n$$\n\nSo, the architect will receive ${\\color{blue}{140}}$ fully-filled packs."] [:db/cas 17592186142511 :question/worked-solution "To multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\times 3}{3} = \\dfrac{6}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac 6 3$ as a division:\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{div}  3 =2\n$$" "To multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\times 3}{3} = \\dfrac{6}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac 6 3$ as a division:\n\n$$\n6\\div 3 =2\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142512 :question/worked-solution "To multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1\\times 4}{6} = \\dfrac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nTo simplify this fraction, we can divide the numerator and denominator by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4\\operatorname{div}  2}{6\\operatorname{div}  2} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" "To multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1\\times 4}{6} = \\dfrac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nTo simplify this fraction, we can divide the numerator and denominator by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4\\div 2}{6\\div 2} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142513 :question/worked-solution "To multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n9\\times \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{9\\times 3}{4} = \\dfrac{27}{4}\n$$\n\nWe now convert this to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n27\\operatorname{div}  4 = 6\\,\\textrm{R} 3 = 6\\,\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$" "To multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n9\\times \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{9\\times 3}{4} = \\dfrac{27}{4}\n$$\n\nWe now convert this to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n27\\div 4 = 6\\,\\textrm{R} 3 = 6\\,\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142517 :question/problem "You're given the following multiplication problem:\n$10 \\times 7 = 70$\nUse the equation above and the relationship between multiplication and division to determine which of the following is the solution to $98 \\operatorname{div}  7$.\n\n1. $11$\n2. $12$\n3. $13$\n4. $14$" "You're given the following multiplication problem:\n$10 \\times 7 = 70$\nUse the equation above and the relationship between multiplication and division to determine which of the following is the solution to $98 \\div 7$.\n\n1. $11$\n2. $12$\n3. $13$\n4. $14$"] [:db/cas 17592186142517 :question/worked-solution "If $98 \\operatorname{div}  7 = \\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}},$ then by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n$\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}} \\times 7 = 98.$\n\nWe start with the given multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n10 \\times 7 = {\\color{blue}{70}}\n$$\n\nLet's now try the numbers in the list, *starting with the smallest*. We can use repeated addition to compute the products.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n11 \\cdot 7 &= 70 + 7 = 77\\cdot \\\\\n12 \\cdot 7 &= 77 + 7 = 84\\cdot \\\\\n13 \\cdot 7 &= 84 + 7 = 91\\cdot \\\\\n14 \\cdot 7 &= 91 + 7 = 98✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n98 \\operatorname{div}  7 = 14\n$$" "If $98 \\div 7 = \\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}},$ then by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n$\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}} \\times 7 = 98.$\n\nWe start with the given multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n10 \\times 7 = {\\color{blue}{70}}\n$$\n\nLet's now try the numbers in the list, *starting with the smallest*. We can use repeated addition to compute the products.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n11 \\cdot 7 &= 70 + 7 = 77\\cdot \\\\\n12 \\cdot 7 &= 77 + 7 = 84\\cdot \\\\\n13 \\cdot 7 &= 84 + 7 = 91\\cdot \\\\\n14 \\cdot 7 &= 91 + 7 = 98✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n98 \\div 7 = 14\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142518 :question/problem "You're given the following multiplication problem:\n$120 \\times 4 = 480$\nUse the equation above and the relationship between multiplication and division to find which of the following is the solution to $488 \\operatorname{div}  4$.\n\n1. $121$\n2. $122$\n3. $123$\n4. $124$" "You're given the following multiplication problem:\n$120 \\times 4 = 480$\nUse the equation above and the relationship between multiplication and division to find which of the following is the solution to $488 \\div 4$.\n\n1. $121$\n2. $122$\n3. $123$\n4. $124$"] [:db/cas 17592186142518 :question/worked-solution "If $488 \\operatorname{div}  4 = \\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}},$ then by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n$\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}} \\times 4 = 488.$\n\nWe start with the given multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n120 \\times 4 = {\\color{blue}{480}}\n$$\n\nLet's try the numbers in the list, *starting with the smallest*. We can use repeated addition to compute the products.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n121 \\cdot 4 &= 480 + 4 = 484\\cdot \\\\\n122 \\cdot 4 &= 484 + 4 = 488✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n488 \\operatorname{div}  4 = 122\n$$" "If $488 \\div 4 = \\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}},$ then by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n$\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}} \\times 4 = 488.$\n\nWe start with the given multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n120 \\times 4 = {\\color{blue}{480}}\n$$\n\nLet's try the numbers in the list, *starting with the smallest*. We can use repeated addition to compute the products.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n121 \\cdot 4 &= 480 + 4 = 484\\cdot \\\\\n122 \\cdot 4 &= 484 + 4 = 488✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n488 \\div 4 = 122\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142519 :question/problem "You're given the following multiplication problem:\n$70 \\times 4 = 280$\nUse the equation above and the relationship between multiplication and division to find which of the following is the solution to $268 \\operatorname{div}  4$.\n\n1. $69$\n2. $68$\n3. $67$\n4. $66$" "You're given the following multiplication problem:\n$70 \\times 4 = 280$\nUse the equation above and the relationship between multiplication and division to find which of the following is the solution to $268 \\div 4$.\n\n1. $69$\n2. $68$\n3. $67$\n4. $66$"] [:db/cas 17592186142519 :question/worked-solution "If $268 \\operatorname{div}  4 = \\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}},$ then by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n$\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}} \\times 4 = 268.$\n\nWe start with the given multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n70 \\times 4 = {\\color{blue}{280}}\n$$\n\nLet's now try the numbers in the list, starting with the *largest*. We can use repeated subtraction to compute the products.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n69 \\cdot 4 &= 280 - 4 = 276\\cdot \\\\\n68 \\cdot 4 &= 276 - 4 = 272\\cdot \\\\\n67 \\cdot 4 &= 272 - 4 = 268✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n268 \\operatorname{div}  4 = 67\n$$" "If $268 \\div 4 = \\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}},$ then by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n$\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}} \\times 4 = 268.$\n\nWe start with the given multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n70 \\times 4 = {\\color{blue}{280}}\n$$\n\nLet's now try the numbers in the list, starting with the *largest*. We can use repeated subtraction to compute the products.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n69 \\cdot 4 &= 280 - 4 = 276\\cdot \\\\\n68 \\cdot 4 &= 276 - 4 = 272\\cdot \\\\\n67 \\cdot 4 &= 272 - 4 = 268✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n268 \\div 4 = 67\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142532 :question/worked-solution "The shape to the left of the addition sign shows\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$\n\nand the shape to the right shows\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$\n\nCombined, both shapes give us\n\n$$\n2+4 = \\color{red}6\n$$\n\nshaded parts in total.\n\n![](images/af/afc51311e07819aef6c7a74969b912af496addcf2623da3f9bcce273cf728dd4.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{\\color{blue}8} + \\dfrac{4}{\\color{blue}8} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}6}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{8} =\\dfrac{6\\operatorname{div}  2}{8\\operatorname{div}  2} = \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{8} + \\dfrac{4}{8} = \\dfrac34\n$$" "The shape to the left of the addition sign shows\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$\n\nand the shape to the right shows\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$\n\nCombined, both shapes give us\n\n$$\n2+4 = \\color{red}6\n$$\n\nshaded parts in total.\n\n![](images/af/afc51311e07819aef6c7a74969b912af496addcf2623da3f9bcce273cf728dd4.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{\\color{blue}8} + \\dfrac{4}{\\color{blue}8} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}6}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{8} =\\dfrac{6\\div 2}{8\\div 2} = \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{8} + \\dfrac{4}{8} = \\dfrac34\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142544 :question/worked-solution "We start by finding the greatest common factor of $8$ and $32$:\n\n- Factors of $8:\\quad 1, 2, 4, {\\color{blue}{8}}$\n- Factors of $32:\\quad 1, 2, 4, {\\color{blue}{8}}, 16, 32$\n\nSo, the greatest common factor of $8$ and $32$ is ${\\color{blue}{8}}$.\n\nDividing both the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{8}}$ gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{32} = \\dfrac{8 \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}{8}}}{32 \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}{8}}}=\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{32}\n$$\n\nexpressed in lowest terms is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$" "We start by finding the greatest common factor of $8$ and $32$:\n\n- Factors of $8:\\quad 1, 2, 4, {\\color{blue}{8}}$\n- Factors of $32:\\quad 1, 2, 4, {\\color{blue}{8}}, 16, 32$\n\nSo, the greatest common factor of $8$ and $32$ is ${\\color{blue}{8}}$.\n\nDividing both the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{8}}$ gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{32} = \\dfrac{8 \\div {\\color{blue}{8}}}{32 \\div {\\color{blue}{8}}}=\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{32}\n$$\n\nexpressed in lowest terms is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142545 :question/worked