 be used to represent the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n76 = 19\\times 4\n$$\n\nWriting this as a division problem, we get\n\n$$\n76\\div 4 = 19\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142567 :question/problem "![](images/25/252256ca391d2c709bc9707d5f58eba0271b3d53325799cb5f62183e306c2d1d.png)\n\nThe area model above can be used to represent the following division problem\n\n$\\boxed{ {\\phantom{00}}} \\operatorname{div}  9 = 47.$\n\nWhat is the missing number?" "![](images/25/252256ca391d2c709bc9707d5f58eba0271b3d53325799cb5f62183e306c2d1d.png)\n\nThe area model above can be used to represent the following division problem\n\n$\\boxed{ {\\phantom{00}}} \\div 9 = 47.$\n\nWhat is the missing number?"] [:db/cas 17592186142567 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at the big rectangle:\n\n- its length is $40+7=47$\n- its width is $9$\n- its area is $360 + 63 = 423$\n\nTherefore, this area model can be used to represent the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n423 = 47 \\times 9\n$$\n\nWriting this as a division problem, we get\n\n$$\n423 \\operatorname{div}  9 = 47\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $423$." "Let's look at the big rectangle:\n\n- its length is $40+7=47$\n- its width is $9$\n- its area is $360 + 63 = 423$\n\nTherefore, this area model can be used to represent the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n423 = 47 \\times 9\n$$\n\nWriting this as a division problem, we get\n\n$$\n423 \\div 9 = 47\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $423$."] [:db/cas 17592186142568 :question/worked-solution "To determine the number of bags Leonard packed, we need to calculate $812\\operatorname{div}  4$.\n\n- Let's first look at the blue rectangle (the one on the left-hand side). Its area is $800$, and its width is $4$. Hence, the length of this rectangle must be\n$800 \\operatorname{div}  4$.\nTo carry out this division, we write $800$ as a product with $100$ as one of the factors and then swap the order of multiplication and division:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800÷4 &= 8\\times 100÷4 \\\\ &= 8÷4\\times 100 \\\\ &= ({\\color{black}{8÷4}})\\times 100 \\\\ &= {\\color{black}{2}}\\times 100 \\\\ &= 200\\end{aligned}\n$$\nLet's add this to our model:![](images/a5/a5cd3dd2fa5b35356cd8bb0853569adbb18a91903474cd14014a11bd2d0cbfd5.png)\n- Now, let's look at the pink rectangle (the one on the right-hand side). Its area is $12$, and its width is $4$. Hence, the length of this rectangle must be\n$12 \\operatorname{div}  4 = 3$.\nLet's add this to our model:![](images/f9/f9c0f5b07b1889ad10a560b2658b3491cb0ac399a42f89bccaeac83e86fb2bb6.png)\n\nNow, notice the following regarding the big rectangle:\n\n- its length is $200+3=203$\n- its width is $4$\n- its area is $800+12=812$\n\nTherefore, this area model can be used to represent the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n812 = 203 \\times 4\n$$\n\nWriting this as a division problem, we get\n\n$$\n812 \\operatorname{div}  4 = 203\n$$\n\nTherefore, Leonard packed $203$ bags of apples." "To determine the number of bags Leonard packed, we need to calculate $812\\div 4$.\n\n- Let's first look at the blue rectangle (the one on the left-hand side). Its area is $800$, and its width is $4$. Hence, the length of this rectangle must be\n$800 \\div 4$.\nTo carry out this division, we write $800$ as a product with $100$ as one of the factors and then swap the order of multiplication and division:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800÷4 &= 8\\times 100÷4 \\\\ &= 8÷4\\times 100 \\\\ &= ({\\color{black}{8÷4}})\\times 100 \\\\ &= {\\color{black}{2}}\\times 100 \\\\ &= 200\\end{aligned}\n$$\nLet's add this to our model:![](images/a5/a5cd3dd2fa5b35356cd8bb0853569adbb18a91903474cd14014a11bd2d0cbfd5.png)\n- Now, let's look at the pink rectangle (the one on the right-hand side). Its area is $12$, and its width is $4$. Hence, the length of this rectangle must be\n$12 \\div 4 = 3$.\nLet's add this to our model:![](images/f9/f9c0f5b07b1889ad10a560b2658b3491cb0ac399a42f89bccaeac83e86fb2bb6.png)\n\nNow, notice the following regarding the big rectangle:\n\n- its length is $200+3=203$\n- its width is $4$\n- its area is $800+12=812$\n\nTherefore, this area model can be used to represent the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n812 = 203 \\times 4\n$$\n\nWriting this as a division problem, we get\n\n$$\n812 \\div 4 = 203\n$$\n\nTherefore, Leonard packed $203$ bags of apples."] [:db/cas 17592186142570 :question/worked-solution "When subtracting the fractional parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{6} - \\dfrac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nHowever, we *cannot* perform this subtraction because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nis larger than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{6}\n$$\n\nTo get around this problem, we write each mixed number as an improper fraction.\n\n- Writing $7\\,\\dfrac{2}{6}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{(7\\times 6)+2}{6} = \\dfrac{44}{6}.\n$$\n- Writing $6 \\, \\dfrac{4}{6}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{(6\\times 6) + 4}{6} = \\dfrac{40}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now carry out the subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{44}{6} - \\dfrac {40}{6}= \\dfrac{44-40}{6} = \\dfrac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nby dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6} = \\dfrac{4\\operatorname{div}  2}{6\\operatorname{div}  2} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n7 \\,\\dfrac{2}{6} - 6 \\, \\dfrac{4}{6} = \\dfrac23\n$$" "When subtracting the fractional parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{6} - \\dfrac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nHowever, we *cannot* perform this subtraction because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nis larger than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{6}\n$$\n\nTo get around this problem, we write each mixed number as an improper fraction.\n\n- Writing $7\\,\\dfrac{2}{6}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{(7\\times 6)+2}{6} = \\dfrac{44}{6}.\n$$\n- Writing $6 \\, \\dfrac{4}{6}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{(6\\times 6) + 4}{6} = \\dfrac{40}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now carry out the subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{44}{6} - \\dfrac {40}{6}= \\dfrac{44-40}{6} = \\dfrac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nby dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6} = \\dfrac{4\\div 2}{6\\div 2} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n7 \\,\\dfrac{2}{6} - 6 \\, \\dfrac{4}{6} = \\dfrac23\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142571 :question/worked-solution "When subtracting the fractional parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} - \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nHowever, we *cannot* perform this subtraction because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac34\n$$\n\nis larger than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac14\n$$\n\nTo get around this problem, we write each mixed number as an improper fraction.\n\n- Writing $7\\,\\dfrac 1 4$ as an improper fraction, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{(4\\times 7)+1}{4} = \\dfrac{29}{4}.\n$$\n- Writing $2 \\, \\dfrac{3}{4}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{(2\\times 4) + 3}{4} = \\dfrac {11} 4.\n$$\n\nWe can now carry out the subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{29}{4} - \\dfrac {11} 4 = \\dfrac{29-11}{4} = \\dfrac{18}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{4}\n$$\n\nby dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{4} = \\dfrac{18\\operatorname{div}  2}{4\\operatorname{div}  2} = \\dfrac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n9\\operatorname{div}  2 = 4\\,\\textrm{R}1\n$$\n\nSo, our final answer is\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" "When subtracting the fractional parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} - \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nHowever, we *cannot* perform this subtraction because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac34\n$$\n\nis larger than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac14\n$$\n\nTo get around this problem, we write each mixed number as an improper fraction.\n\n- Writing $7\\,\\dfrac 1 4$ as an improper fraction, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{(4\\times 7)+1}{4} = \\dfrac{29}{4}.\n$$\n- Writing $2 \\, \\dfrac{3}{4}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{(2\\times 4) + 3}{4} = \\dfrac {11} 4.\n$$\n\nWe can now carry out the subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{29}{4} - \\dfrac {11} 4 = \\dfrac{29-11}{4} = \\dfrac{18}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{4}\n$$\n\nby dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{4} = \\dfrac{18\\div 2}{4\\div 2} = \\dfrac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n9\\div 2 = 4\\,\\textrm{R}1\n$$\n\nSo, our final answer is\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142572 :question/problem "Express $59\\operatorname{div}  4$ as a fraction." "Express $59\\div 4$ as a fraction."] [:db/cas 17592186142572 :question/worked-solution "A division problem may be interpreted as a fraction, where the dividend is the numerator, and the divisor is the denominator.\n\nTherefore, we can represent the given division problem as\n\n$$\n59\\operatorname{div}  4=\\dfrac{59}{4}\n$$" "A division problem may be interpreted as a fraction, where the dividend is the numerator, and the divisor is the denominator.\n\nTherefore, we can represent the given division problem as\n\n$$\n59\\div 4=\\dfrac{59}{4}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142573 :question/problem "What is $20\\operatorname{div}  8$ as an improper fraction?" "What is $20\\div 8$ as an improper fraction?"] [:db/cas 17592186142573 :question/worked-solution "First, we can represent the given division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n20\\operatorname{div}  8 = \\dfrac{20}{8}\n$$\n\nThen, we can simplify the fraction by dividing both the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20\\operatorname{div}  4}{8\\operatorname{div}  4} = \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$" "First, we can represent the given division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n20\\div 8 = \\dfrac{20}{8}\n$$\n\nThen, we can simplify the fraction by dividing both the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20\\div 4}{8\\div 4} = \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142574 :question/worked-solution "A fraction may be interpreted as the division of the numerator by the denominator.\n\nTherefore, we can represent the given fraction as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{19}=8\\operatorname{div}  19\n$$" "A fraction may be interpreted as the division of the numerator by the denominator.\n\nTherefore, we can represent the given fraction as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{19}=8\\div 19\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142585 :question/worked-solution "The rule is \"divide by $2$\" and the first missing number comes after $8$. Therefore, the first missing number is\n$8 \\operatorname{div}  2=4$.\n\nThe second missing number comes after $2$. Therefore, the second missing number is\n$2 \\operatorname{div}  2=1$.\n\nSo, we have the following pattern:\n$16,\\qquad 8,\\qquad \\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{4},\\qquad 2,\\qquad \\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{1}$\n\nThe missing numbers are $4$ and $1$." "The rule is \"divide by $2$\" and the first missing number comes after $8$. Therefore, the first missing number is\n$8 \\div 2=4$.\n\nThe second missing number comes after $2$. Therefore, the second missing number is\n$2 \\div 2=1$.\n\nSo, we have the following pattern:\n$16,\\qquad 8,\\qquad \\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{4},\\qquad 2,\\qquad \\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{1}$\n\nThe missing numbers are $4$ and $1$."] [:db/cas 17592186142590 :question/problem "Divide $4 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac 1 {5} .$" "Divide $4 \\div \\dfrac 1 {5} .$"] [:db/cas 17592186142590 :question/worked-solution "Dividing a whole number by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}$ is $\\color{blue}5.$ \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac1{\\color{blue}5} = 4\\times  {\\color{blue}5} =20.\n$$" "Dividing a whole number by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}$ is $\\color{blue}5.$ \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n4\\div \\dfrac1{\\color{blue}5} = 4\\times  {\\color{blue}5} =20.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142591 :question/problem "Calculate the value of $2\\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac67,$ expressing the result as a mixed number." "Calculate the value of $2\\div \\dfrac67,$ expressing the result as a mixed number."] [:db/cas 17592186142591 :question/worked-solution "Dividing a whole number by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nFirst, we write $2$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2=\\dfrac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}6}{\\color{red}7}$ is $\\dfrac{\\color{red}7}{\\color{blue}6}.$ \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac 6 {7} = \n\\dfrac 2 1 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{\\color{blue}6}{\\color{red}7} = \\dfrac 2 1 \\times  \\dfrac{\\color{red}7}{\\color{blue}6}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply our fractions. Note that we can simplify the process by swapping the denominators first.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{1}\\times \\frac{7}{6} & =\\frac{2}{6}\\times \\frac{7}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\times \\frac{7}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 7}{3\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert our result to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac73 = 2\\,\\textrm{R}\\,1 = 2\\,\\dfrac13\n$$" "Dividing a whole number by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nFirst, we write $2$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2=\\dfrac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}6}{\\color{red}7}$ is $\\dfrac{\\color{red}7}{\\color{blue}6}.$ \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n2\\div \\dfrac 6 {7} = \n\\dfrac 2 1 \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}6}{\\color{red}7} = \\dfrac 2 1 \\times  \\dfrac{\\color{red}7}{\\color{blue}6}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply our fractions. Note that we can simplify the process by swapping the denominators first.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{1}\\times \\frac{7}{6} & =\\frac{2}{6}\\times \\frac{7}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\times \\frac{7}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 7}{3\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert our result to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac73 = 2\\,\\textrm{R}\\,1 = 2\\,\\dfrac13\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142592 :question/problem "Divide $8\\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac 2 5.$" "Divide $8\\div \\dfrac 2 5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186142592 :question/worked-solution "Dividing a whole number by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nFirst, we write $8$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n8=\\dfrac{8}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}5}$ is $\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}2}.$ \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac 2 5 = \n\n\\dfrac {8} 1 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}5} = \\dfrac {8} 1 \\times  \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply our fractions. Note that we can simplify the process by swapping the denominators first.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{1}\\times \\frac{5}{2} & =\\frac{8}{2}\\times \\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =4\\times 5 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Dividing a whole number by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nFirst, we write $8$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n8=\\dfrac{8}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}5}$ is $\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}2}.$ \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n8\\div \\dfrac 2 5 = \n\n\\dfrac {8} 1 \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}5} = \\dfrac {8} 1 \\times  \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply our fractions. Note that we can simplify the process by swapping the denominators first.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{1}\\times \\frac{5}{2} & =\\frac{8}{2}\\times \\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =4\\times 5 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142593 :question/problem "Calculate the value of $4\\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac35,$ expressing the result as a mixed number." "Calculate the value of $4\\div \\dfrac35,$ expressing the result as a mixed number."] [:db/cas 17592186142593 :question/worked-solution "Dividing a whole number by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nFirst, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4=\\dfrac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}5}$ is $\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}3}.$ \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac 3 5 = \n\n\\dfrac 4 1 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}5} = \\dfrac 4 1 \\times  \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply our fractions. \n\n$$\n\\dfrac 4 {1} \\times \\dfrac{5}{3} =  \\dfrac {4\\times 5} {1\\times 3} = \\dfrac{20}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{3} =6\\,\\textrm{R} 2 = 6\\,\\dfrac 2 {3}\n$$" "Dividing a whole number by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nFirst, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4=\\dfrac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}5}$ is $\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}3}.$ \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n4\\div \\dfrac 3 5 = \n\n\\dfrac 4 1 \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}5} = \\dfrac 4 1 \\times  \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply our fractions. \n\n$$\n\\dfrac 4 {1} \\times \\dfrac{5}{3} =  \\dfrac {4\\times 5} {1\\times 3} = \\dfrac{20}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{3} =6\\,\\textrm{R} 2 = 6\\,\\dfrac 2 {3}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142594 :question/worked-solution "A fraction is written in lowest terms if the greatest common factor of the numerator and denominator equals $1$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our fractions.\n\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{11}{14}$ is written in lowest terms since the greatest common factor of $11$ and $14$ is $1$.\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{10}{15}$ is **not** written in lowest terms. Notice that the greatest common factor of $10$ and $15$ is $5$. So, we can divide both the numerator and denominator by $5$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{10}{15} = \\dfrac{10\\operatorname{div}  5}{15\\operatorname{div}  5} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{15}{28}$ is written in lowest terms since the greatest common factor of $15$ and $28$ is $1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "A fraction is written in lowest terms if the greatest common factor of the numerator and denominator equals $1$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our fractions.\n\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{11}{14}$ is written in lowest terms since the greatest common factor of $11$ and $14$ is $1$.\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{10}{15}$ is **not** written in lowest terms. Notice that the greatest common factor of $10$ and $15$ is $5$. So, we can divide both the numerator and denominator by $5$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{10}{15} = \\dfrac{10\\div 5}{15\\div 5} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{15}{28}$ is written in lowest terms since the greatest common factor of $15$ and $28$ is $1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186142595 :question/problem "Calculate $12 + 30 \\operatorname{div}  (6 + 9)$." "Calculate $12 + 30 \\div (6 + 9)$."] [:db/cas 17592186142595 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 + 30 \\operatorname{div}  (6 + 9) &= 12 + 30 \\operatorname{div}  15 \\mid\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply and divide, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 + 30 \\operatorname{div}  15 &= 12 + 2 \\mid\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add and subtract, from left to right:\n\n$$\n12 + 2 =14\n$$" "To evaluate the given expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 + 30 \\div (6 + 9) &= 12 + 30 \\div 15 \\mid\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply and divide, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 + 30 \\div 15 &= 12 + 2 \\mid\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add and subtract, from left to right:\n\n$$\n12 + 2 =14\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142596 :question/problem "Evaluate $[10 - (1 + 3)] \\operatorname{div}  2$." "Evaluate $[10 - (1 + 3)] \\div 2$."] [:db/cas 17592186142596 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the inner parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[10 - (1 + 3)] \\operatorname{div}  2 &= [10 - 4] \\operatorname{div}  2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we perform the operation inside the square brackets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[10 - 4] \\operatorname{div}  2 &= 6 \\operatorname{div}  2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 \\operatorname{div}  2 &= 3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate the given expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the inner parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[10 - (1 + 3)] \\div 2 &= [10 - 4] \\div 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we perform the operation inside the square brackets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[10 - 4] \\div 2 &= 6 \\div 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 \\div 2 &= 3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142597 :question/problem "In the expression below, which part of the expression should be calculated first?\n\n$\\{[6 + (3 \\times 4)] \\operatorname{div}  9 \\} \\times 7$" "In the expression below, which part of the expression should be calculated first?\n\n$\\{[6 + (3 \\times 4)] \\div 9 \\} \\times 7$"] [:db/cas 17592186142597 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we must follow the order of operations with parentheses. Therefore, we should start with the operation that's inside the most inner parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\{[6 + ({\\color{blue}{3 \\times 4}})] \\operatorname{div}  9 \\} \\times 7\n$$\n\nSo we should calculate ${\\color{blue}{3 \\times 4}}$ first." "To evaluate the given expression, we must follow the order of operations with parentheses. Therefore, we should start with the operation that's inside the most inner parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\{[6 + ({\\color{blue}{3 \\times 4}})] \\div 9 \\} \\times 7\n$$\n\nSo we should calculate ${\\color{blue}{3 \\times 4}}$ first."] [:db/cas 17592186142598 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\{[(4 \\times 2) - 5] + 3\\} \\operatorname{div}  3$." "Evaluate $\\{[(4 \\times 2) - 5] + 3\\} \\div 3$."] [:db/cas 17592186142598 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the inner parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{[(4 \\cdot 2) - 5] + 3} \\operatorname{div}  3 &= {[(4 \\cdot 2) - 5] + 3} \\operatorname{div}  3 \\\\\n&= {[8 - 5] + 3} \\operatorname{div}  3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we perform the operation inside the square brackets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{[8 - 5] + 3} \\operatorname{div}  3 &= {[8 - 5] + 3} \\operatorname{div}  3 \\\\\n&= {3 + 3} \\operatorname{div}  3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we perform the operation inside the braces:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{3 + 3} \\operatorname{div}  3 &= {3 + 3} \\operatorname{div}  3 \\\\\n&= 6 \\operatorname{div}  3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 \\operatorname{div}  3 &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate the given expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the inner parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{[(4 \\cdot 2) - 5] + 3} \\div 3 &= {[(4 \\cdot 2) - 5] + 3} \\div 3 \\\\\n&= {[8 - 5] + 3} \\div 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we perform the operation inside the square brackets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{[8 - 5] + 3} \\div 3 &= {[8 - 5] + 3} \\div 3 \\\\\n&= {3 + 3} \\div 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we perform the operation inside the braces:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{3 + 3} \\div 3 &= {3 + 3} \\div 3 \\\\\n&= 6 \\div 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 \\div 3 &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142600 :question/worked-solution "To find the reciprocal of a fraction, we switch the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}14} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}14}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nNow, we convert the resulting fraction to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n14\\operatorname{div}  3 = 4\\,\\textrm{R}2=4\\,\\dfrac 2 3\n$$\n\nSo the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{3}{14}$ is $4\\,\\dfrac{2}{3}.$" "To find the reciprocal of a fraction, we switch the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}14} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}14}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nNow, we convert the resulting fraction to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n14\\div 3 = 4\\,\\textrm{R}2=4\\,\\dfrac 2 3\n$$\n\nSo the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{3}{14}$ is $4\\,\\dfrac{2}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186142606 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\dfrac{1}{4} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{8}.$" "Find the value of $\\dfrac{1}{4} \\div \\dfrac{1}{8}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186142606 :question/worked-solution "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}8}$ is $\\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{\\color{blue}1}.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 4 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}8} = \\dfrac 1 4 \\times  \\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {4} \\times \\dfrac{8}{1} =  \\dfrac {1\\times 8} {4\\times 1} = \\dfrac{8}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{div}  4=2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{1}{8} = 2 \\, .\n$$" "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}8}$ is $\\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{\\color{blue}1}.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 4 \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}8} = \\dfrac 1 4 \\times  \\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {4} \\times \\dfrac{8}{1} =  \\dfrac {1\\times 8} {4\\times 1} = \\dfrac{8}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n8\\div 4=2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\div \\dfrac{1}{8} = 2 \\, .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142607 :question/problem "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n$$\n\\dfrac 6 {7}\\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac 4 {3}=\\dfrac{\\,\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}}}{14}\n$$" "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n$$\n\\dfrac 6 {7}\\div \\dfrac 4 {3}=\\dfrac{\\,\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}}}{14}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142607 :question/worked-solution "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}3}$ is $\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4}.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 6 {7} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}3} = \\dfrac 6 {7} \\times  \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 6 {7} \\times \\dfrac{3}{4} =  \\dfrac {6\\times 3} {7\\times 4} = \\dfrac{18}{28}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{28} = \\dfrac{18\\operatorname{div}  2}{28\\operatorname{div}  2} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}9}{14}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 6 {7}\\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac 4 {3} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}9}{14} \\, .\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the missing number is ${\\color{blue}{9}}.$" "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}3}$ is $\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4}.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 6 {7} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}3} = \\dfrac 6 {7} \\times  \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 6 {7} \\times \\dfrac{3}{4} =  \\dfrac {6\\times 3} {7\\times 4} = \\dfrac{18}{28}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{28} = \\dfrac{18\\div 2}{28\\div 2} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}9}{14}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 6 {7}\\div \\dfrac 4 {3} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}9}{14} \\, .\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the missing number is ${\\color{blue}{9}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186142608 :question/problem "Calculate the value of $\\dfrac{1}{12} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{2}{3}.$" "Calculate the value of $\\dfrac{1}{12} \\div \\dfrac{2}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186142608 :question/worked-solution "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}3}$ is $\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}2}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {12} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}3} = \\dfrac 1 {12} \\times  \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {12} \\times \\dfrac{3}{2} =  \\dfrac {1\\times 3} {12\\times 2} = \\dfrac{3}{24}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{24} = \\dfrac{3\\operatorname{div}  3}{24\\operatorname{div}  3} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{12} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{8} \\, .\n$$" "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}3}$ is $\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}2}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {12} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}3} = \\dfrac 1 {12} \\times  \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {12} \\times \\dfrac{3}{2} =  \\dfrac {1\\times 3} {12\\times 2} = \\dfrac{3}{24}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{24} = \\dfrac{3\\div 3}{24\\div 3} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{12} \\div \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{8} \\, .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142609 :question/problem "Calculate the value of $\\dfrac{1}{3} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac 2 {13}.$" "Calculate the value of $\\dfrac{1}{3} \\div \\dfrac 2 {13}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186142609 :question/worked-solution "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}13}$ is $\\dfrac{\\color{red}13}{\\color{blue}2}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {3} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}13} = \\dfrac 1 {3} \\times  \\dfrac{\\color{red}13}{\\color{blue}2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {3} \\times \\dfrac{13}{2} =  \\dfrac {1\\times 13} {3 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{13}{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{13}{6} =2\\,\\textrm{R} 1 = 2\\,\\dfrac 1 {6}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac 2 {13} = 2\\,\\dfrac 1 {6} \\, .\n$$" "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}13}$ is $\\dfrac{\\color{red}13}{\\color{blue}2}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {3} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}13} = \\dfrac 1 {3} \\times  \\dfrac{\\color{red}13}{\\color{blue}2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {3} \\times \\dfrac{13}{2} =  \\dfrac {1\\times 13} {3 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{13}{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{13}{6} =2\\,\\textrm{R} 1 = 2\\,\\dfrac 1 {6}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\div \\dfrac 2 {13} = 2\\,\\dfrac 1 {6} \\, .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142610 :question/problem "![](images/91/916d8a6c0c4ba6c249191c0ee0d85d20f3e14ebfbabbdb0a6a5be583e39be139.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\dfrac{5}{8} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{3}{8}.$" "![](images/91/916d8a6c0c4ba6c249191c0ee0d85d20f3e14ebfbabbdb0a6a5be583e39be139.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\dfrac{5}{8} \\div \\dfrac{3}{8}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186142610 :question/worked-solution "![](images/ea/ea208841c3276de2e8736add1ad5c6f4c343a9ec8d137f74f10e47b9243e87eb.png)\n\nWe can break down our division problem as follows:\n\n- Computing $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{{\\color{red}5}}{8}\\operatorname{div} \\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8}\n$$ means we want to determine how many times $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8}$ fits into $\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{8}.$\n\n- Since both fractions have a *common denominator*, this is the *same* as determining how many times ${\\color{blue}3}$ fits into ${\\color{red}5}.$\n\n- Therefore, instead of $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{{\\color{red}5}}{8}\\operatorname{div} \\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8},\n$$ we can simply compute ${\\color{red}5}\\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}3}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}3} = \\dfrac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{8} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{3}{8} = \\dfrac{5}{3}.\n$$" "![](images/ea/ea208841c3276de2e8736add1ad5c6f4c343a9ec8d137f74f10e47b9243e87eb.png)\n\nWe can break down our division problem as follows:\n\n- Computing $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{{\\color{red}5}}{8}\\div\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8}\n$$ means we want to determine how many times $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8}$ fits into $\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{8}.$\n\n- Since both fractions have a *common denominator*, this is the *same* as determining how many times ${\\color{blue}3}$ fits into ${\\color{red}5}.$\n\n- Therefore, instead of $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{{\\color{red}5}}{8}\\div\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8},\n$$ we can simply compute ${\\color{red}5}\\div {\\color{blue}3}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\div {\\color{blue}3} = \\dfrac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{8} \\div \\dfrac{3}{8} = \\dfrac{5}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142611 :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5e7367d6b3a53b513c2921d91579b1bd6588b850622ecf1c7b370695227a64d.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the equation below.\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{7}{10} = \\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{6} \\hspace{0.167em}}}}{7}\n$$" "![](images/c5/c5e7367d6b3a53b513c2921d91579b1bd6588b850622ecf1c7b370695227a64d.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the equation below.\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5} \\div \\dfrac{7}{10} = \\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{6} \\hspace{0.167em}}}}{7}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142611 :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $5$ and $10.$ We can make a common denominator of $10$ by splitting the shape on the left into $2$ parts vertically:  \n\n![](images/3d/3d4839662bcac31bc197436b533da61e07ecf1ca301b3fa7613d433833e14028.png)\n\n \n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}6}{10} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{\\color{blue}7}{10}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}6} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}7} = \\dfrac{6}{7}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{7}{10}  = \\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} 6 \\hspace{0.167em}}}}{7} \\, .\n$$\n\nHence, the missing number is $6.$" "The denominators are $5$ and $10.$ We can make a common denominator of $10$ by splitting the shape on the left into $2$ parts vertically:  \n\n![](images/3d/3d4839662bcac31bc197436b533da61e07ecf1ca301b3fa7613d433833e14028.png)\n\n \n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}6}{10} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}7}{10}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}6} \\div {\\color{blue}7} = \\dfrac{6}{7}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5} \\div \\dfrac{7}{10}  = \\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} 6 \\hspace{0.167em}}}}{7} \\, .\n$$\n\nHence, the missing number is $6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186142612 :question/problem "![](images/87/87ce51fed33e907d6acb7e213bec596ea97b16e739bb96b5446975dc6a593011.png)\n\nCalculate $\\dfrac{2}{3} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{3}{4}$ using the model above." "![](images/87/87ce51fed33e907d6acb7e213bec596ea97b16e739bb96b5446975dc6a593011.png)\n\nCalculate $\\dfrac{2}{3} \\div \\dfrac{3}{4}$ using the model above."] [:db/cas 17592186142612 :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $4$ and $3.$ We can make a common denominator of $4\\times 3 = 12.$ \n\nWe split the shape on the left of the division sign into $4$ parts vertically, and we split the shape on the right into $3$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/93/931f2220f4f5b4e8d3760bcf283d19ef565d2bd8910db6d95cd44e604782a0f0.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{12} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{\\color{blue}9}{12}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}8} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}9} = \\dfrac{8}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{8}{9} \\, .\n$$" "The denominators are $4$ and $3.$ We can make a common denominator of $4\\times 3 = 12.$ \n\nWe split the shape on the left of the division sign into $4$ parts vertically, and we split the shape on the right into $3$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/93/931f2220f4f5b4e8d3760bcf283d19ef565d2bd8910db6d95cd44e604782a0f0.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{12} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}9}{12}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}8} \\div {\\color{blue}9} = \\dfrac{8}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3} \\div \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{8}{9} \\, .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142613 :question/problem "![](images/93/93a73e4b7458251fdb96891e2b00af4fd5ecf6a442aed9fb7c289ca25ae0e389.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\dfrac{11}{14} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{3}{7}.$" "![](images/93/93a73e4b7458251fdb96891e2b00af4fd5ecf6a442aed9fb7c289ca25ae0e389.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\dfrac{11}{14} \\div \\dfrac{3}{7}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186142613 :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $7$ and $14.$ We can make a common denominator of $14.$\n\nWe split the shape on the right of the division sign into $2$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/a9/a9867f3e62605ccbaad6fdc6a1925173a460f287e36b8cc563ab62bb3074a13c.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}11}{14} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{\\color{blue}6}{14}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}11} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}6} = 1\\,\\dfrac56.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{14} \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{3}{7} = 1\\,\\dfrac56 \\, .\n$$" "The denominators are $7$ and $14.$ We can make a common denominator of $14.$\n\nWe split the shape on the right of the division sign into $2$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/a9/a9867f3e62605ccbaad6fdc6a1925173a460f287e36b8cc563ab62bb3074a13c.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}11}{14} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}6}{14}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}11} \\div {\\color{blue}6} = 1\\,\\dfrac56.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{14} \\div \\dfrac{3}{7} = 1\\,\\dfrac56 \\, .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142629 :question/problem "Calculate $12 \\operatorname{div}  6 \\times 4$." "Calculate $12 \\div 6 \\times 4$."] [:db/cas 17592186142629 :question/worked-solution "To calculate the given expression, we follow the order of operations.\n\nIn this case, we multiply and divide from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 \\operatorname{div}  6 \\cdot 4 &= 12 \\operatorname{div}  6 \\cdot 4 \\\\ &= 2 \\cdot 4 \\\\ &= 8\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To calculate the given expression, we follow the order of operations.\n\nIn this case, we multiply and divide from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 \\div 6 \\cdot 4 &= 12 \\div 6 \\cdot 4 \\\\ &= 2 \\cdot 4 \\\\ &= 8\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142631 :question/problem "What is the dividend in the division problem below?\n$20 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 4$" "What is the dividend in the division problem below?\n$20 \\div 5 = 4$"] [:db/cas 17592186142632 :question/problem "Calculate $4 \\times 5 + 6 \\operatorname{div}  3$." "Calculate $4 \\times 5 + 6 \\div 3$."] [:db/cas 17592186142632 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we follow the order of operations.\n\nFirst, we multiply and divide, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 \\cdot 5 + 6 \\operatorname{div}  3 &= 4 \\cdot 5 + 6 \\operatorname{div}  3 \\\\ &= 20 + 6 \\operatorname{div}  3 \\\\ &= 20 + 6 \\operatorname{div}  3 \\\\ &= 20 + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20 + 2 &= 22\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate the given expression, we follow the order of operations.\n\nFirst, we multiply and divide, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 \\cdot 5 + 6 \\div 3 &= 4 \\cdot 5 + 6 \\div 3 \\\\ &= 20 + 6 \\div 3 \\\\ &= 20 + 6 \\div 3 \\\\ &= 20 + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20 + 2 &= 22\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142633 :question/problem "What is the divisor in the division problem below?\n$12 \\operatorname{div}  4 = 3$" "What is the divisor in the division problem below?\n$12 \\div 4 = 3$"] [:db/cas 17592186142634 :question/problem "What is the quotient in the division problem below?\n$18 \\operatorname{div}  2 = 9$" "What is the quotient in the division problem below?\n$18 \\div 2 = 9$"] [:db/cas 17592186142635 :question/problem "What is the remainder in the division problem below?\n$32 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 6 \\: \\textrm{R} \\: 2$" "What is the remainder in the division problem below?\n$32 \\div 5 = 6 \\: \\textrm{R} \\: 2$"] [:db/cas 17592186142636 :question/problem "![](images/45/45d4e4ca4f1cdfddcb624dccf32cb053818a2fd70cbb7b5a02d1ff30f9784e2b.png)\n\n \n\nThe model above represents $3$ wholes. Use the model to determine the missing number in the following division problem. \n$$\n3 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{3}{5} = \\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}\n$$" "![](images/45/45d4e4ca4f1cdfddcb624dccf32cb053818a2fd70cbb7b5a02d1ff30f9784e2b.png)\n\n \n\nThe model above represents $3$ wholes. Use the model to determine the missing number in the following division problem. \n$$\n3 \\div \\dfrac{3}{5} = \\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142636 :question/worked-solution "We proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we split each whole into fifths.\n\n![](images/9c/9c10c283125b23a553855e247149fba126c1977bf62a487b09121eb539dabfbf.png)\n\n \n\n- Next, we create groups of ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac3{5}}}.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8ee616adc072552e64182bba65bd74754b4a16857bfc7fe7e411ab9784bd331.png)\n\n \n\n- Then, we count the groups.\n\n![](images/ad/ad9c3bcb60e6277ae536b00489b3f4267a31fb9f2d0a7af368ee0cc43215287d.png)\n\n \n\nSo, if we divide $\\color{blue}3$ wholes into groups of ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac3{5}}},$ we get ${\\color{purple}{5}}$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3 \\operatorname{div}  \\dfrac{3}{{5}} = \\fbox{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the missing number is $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{purple}5}.$" "We proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we split each whole into fifths.\n\n![](images/9c/9c10c283125b23a553855e247149fba126c1977bf62a487b09121eb539dabfbf.png)\n\n \n\n- Next, we create groups of ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac3{5}}}.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8ee616adc072552e64182bba65bd74754b4a16857bfc7fe7e411ab9784bd331.png)\n\n \n\n- Then, we count the groups.\n\n![](images/ad/ad9c3bcb60e6277ae536b00489b3f4267a31fb9f2d0a7af368ee0cc43215287d.png)\n\n \n\nSo, if we divide $\\color{blue}3$ wholes into groups of ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac3{5}}},$ we get ${\\color{purple}{5}}$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3 \\div \\dfrac{3}{{5}} = \\fbox{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the missing number is $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{purple}5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186142637 :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c3f53204a0271ea9816eaddbdbeeb84648638918531ace2f06251af765fe05f.png)\n\n \n\nUse the model above to determine the missing numbers in the following division problem.\n$$\n\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{ {0}^{2}}}}\\:  \\operatorname{div}  \\:\\! \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\,\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{ {0}^{2}} \\hspace{0.167em}}}\n$$" "![](images/0c/0c3f53204a0271ea9816eaddbdbeeb84648638918531ace2f06251af765fe05f.png)\n\n \n\nUse the model above to determine the missing numbers in the following division problem.\n$$\n\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{ {0}^{2}}}}\\:  \\div \\:\\! \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\,\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{ {0}^{2}} \\hspace{0.167em}}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142637 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $\\color{blue}2$ wholes. Each whole is split into *thirds*.\n\n![](images/d9/d9c692ed44d98c47b475dc18c2d28a01d0ab1f2f231ed24a9604f14000f599b1.png)\n\n \n\n- The thirds are grouped, and each group contains ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac23}}.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c9fd6222f68deffa02ce69a2f31dca220ce2285f71dbd8255d364126ea0d8c8.png)\n\n \n\n- There are $\\color{purple} 3$ groups in total.\n\n![](images/22/228c9e5eea2b5696d64d4423ecd927e12a11e6672d614cc30a947549943be0c4.png)\n\n \n\nSo, if we divide $\\color{blue}2$ wholes into groups of ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac23}},$ we get ${\\color{purple}{3}}$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents the following division problem:\n$$\n\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{blue}2} \\hspace{0.167em}}} \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{red}{\\dfrac23}} = \\boxed{ { {\\color{purple}\\hspace{0.167em} 3 \\hspace{0.167em}}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing numbers are $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$ and $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{purple}3}.$" "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $\\color{blue}2$ wholes. Each whole is split into *thirds*.\n\n![](images/d9/d9c692ed44d98c47b475dc18c2d28a01d0ab1f2f231ed24a9604f14000f599b1.png)\n\n \n\n- The thirds are grouped, and each group contains ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac23}}.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c9fd6222f68deffa02ce69a2f31dca220ce2285f71dbd8255d364126ea0d8c8.png)\n\n \n\n- There are $\\color{purple} 3$ groups in total.\n\n![](images/22/228c9e5eea2b5696d64d4423ecd927e12a11e6672d614cc30a947549943be0c4.png)\n\n \n\nSo, if we divide $\\color{blue}2$ wholes into groups of ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac23}},$ we get ${\\color{purple}{3}}$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents the following division problem:\n$$\n\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{blue}2} \\hspace{0.167em}}} \\div {\\color{red}{\\dfrac23}} = \\boxed{ { {\\color{purple}\\hspace{0.167em} 3 \\hspace{0.167em}}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing numbers are $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$ and $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{purple}3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186142640 :question/worked-solution "To multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 5 {6} \\times \\dfrac 3 {4} =  \\dfrac {5\\times 3} {6\\times 4} = \\dfrac{15}{24}\n$$\n\nWe reduce this fraction to its simplest form by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {15} {24} =  \\dfrac {15\\operatorname{div}  3} {24\\operatorname{div}  3} = \\dfrac{5}{8}\n$$" "To multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 5 {6} \\times \\dfrac 3 {4} =  \\dfrac {5\\times 3} {6\\times 4} = \\dfrac{15}{24}\n$$\n\nWe reduce this fraction to its simplest form by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {15} {24} =  \\dfrac {15\\div 3} {24\\div 3} = \\dfrac{5}{8}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142644 :question/problem "What is the value of $\\dfrac 1 6 \\operatorname{div}  5 ?$" "What is the value of $\\dfrac 1 6 \\div 5 ?$"] [:db/cas 17592186142644 :question/worked-solution "Dividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\color{blue}5$ is $\\dfrac{1}{{\\color{blue}{5}}}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 6\\, {\\color{red}\\operatorname{div} } \\, {\\color{blue}5} = \\dfrac 1 6\\, {\\color{red}\\times} \\,\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 6 \\times \\dfrac{1}{5} = \\dfrac {1\\times 1} {6\\times 5} = \\dfrac{1}{30}\n$$" "Dividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\color{blue}5$ is $\\dfrac{1}{{\\color{blue}{5}}}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 6\\, {\\color{red}\\div} \\, {\\color{blue}5} = \\dfrac 1 6\\, {\\color{red}\\times} \\,\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 6 \\times \\dfrac{1}{5} = \\dfrac {1\\times 1} {6\\times 5} = \\dfrac{1}{30}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142645 :question/problem "What is the values of $\\dfrac 2 3 \\operatorname{div}  7 ?$" "What is the values of $\\dfrac 2 3 \\div 7 ?$"] [:db/cas 17592186142645 :question/worked-solution "Dividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\color{blue}7$ is $\\dfrac{1}{{\\color{blue}{7}}}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 3\\, {\\color{red}\\operatorname{div} } \\, {\\color{blue}7} = \\dfrac 2 3\\, {\\color{red}\\times} \\,\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}7} .\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 3 \\times \\dfrac{1}{7} = \\dfrac {2\\times 1} {3\\times 7} = \\dfrac{2}{21}\n$$" "Dividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\color{blue}7$ is $\\dfrac{1}{{\\color{blue}{7}}}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 3\\, {\\color{red}\\div} \\, {\\color{blue}7} = \\dfrac 2 3\\, {\\color{red}\\times} \\,\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}7} .\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 3 \\times \\dfrac{1}{7} = \\dfrac {2\\times 1} {3\\times 7} = \\dfrac{2}{21}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142646 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\dfrac{6}{11} \\operatorname{div}  3 .$" "Find the value of $\\dfrac{6}{11} \\div 3 .$"] [:db/cas 17592186142646 :question/worked-solution "Dividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\color{blue}3$ is $\\dfrac{1}{{\\color{blue}{3}}}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {6}{11} \\, {\\color{red}\\operatorname{div} } \\, {\\color{blue}3} =  \\dfrac {6}{11}\\, {\\color{red}\\times} \\,\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {6}{11} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac {6\\times 1} {11\\times 3} = \\dfrac{6}{33}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify our result by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{33} = \\dfrac{6\\operatorname{div}  3}{33\\operatorname{div}  3} = \\dfrac{2}{11}\n$$" "Dividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\color{blue}3$ is $\\dfrac{1}{{\\color{blue}{3}}}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {6}{11} \\, {\\color{red}\\div} \\, {\\color{blue}3} =  \\dfrac {6}{11}\\, {\\color{red}\\times} \\,\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {6}{11} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac {6\\times 1} {11\\times 3} = \\dfrac{6}{33}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify our result by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{33} = \\dfrac{6\\div 3}{33\\div 3} = \\dfrac{2}{11}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142654 :question/worked-solution "First, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4 = \\dfrac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{(2 \\times 4) + 1}{4} = \\dfrac{9}{4}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{1} \\times \\dfrac{9}{4} = \\dfrac{4 \\times 9}{1 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{36}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{36}{4} = \\dfrac{36 \\operatorname{div}  4}{4 \\operatorname{div}  4} = \\dfrac{9}{1} = 9\n$$" "First, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4 = \\dfrac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{(2 \\times 4) + 1}{4} = \\dfrac{9}{4}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{1} \\times \\dfrac{9}{4} = \\dfrac{4 \\times 9}{1 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{36}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{36}{4} = \\dfrac{36 \\div 4}{4 \\div 4} = \\dfrac{9}{1} = 9\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142655 :question/problem "![](images/7f/7fbc79b1d259f645a8296be12c2748e5abb05d02217f3628e24ef20781f06c71.png)\n\n \n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the following division problem. \n$$\n4 \\operatorname{div}  \\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{ {\\displaystyle \\frac{0}{0}}} \\hspace{0.167em}}} = 10\n$$" "![](images/7f/7fbc79b1d259f645a8296be12c2748e5abb05d02217f3628e24ef20781f06c71.png)\n\n \n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the following division problem. \n$$\n4 \\div \\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{ {\\displaystyle \\frac{0}{0}}} \\hspace{0.167em}}} = 10\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142655 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $\\color{blue}4$ wholes. Each whole is split into *fifths*.\n\n![](images/20/2096f3451d19fc44bccca9ebe3033e9af29878aa928ad05b102e391be3f76105.png)\n\n \n\n- The fifths are grouped, and each group contains ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac{2}{5}}}.$\n\n![](images/96/96bc693ffe7fa65c90758e0d86a7176b19f7a279cfff12607926e0350448fe42.png)\n\n \n\n- There are $\\color{purple} 10$ groups in total.\n\n![](images/1e/1ebdc6bce6be33bbdd5fd7ff79a562c22e68103cebc8f0ded5ddac6833633989.png)\n\n \n\nSo, if we divide $\\color{blue}4$ wholes into groups of ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac{2}{5}}},$ we get ${\\color{purple}{10}}$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents the following division problem:\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{4}} \\operatorname{div}  \\boxed{ { {\\color{red}{\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}}}}} = {\\color{purple}{10}}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{red}\\dfrac{2}{5}}.$" "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $\\color{blue}4$ wholes. Each whole is split into *fifths*.\n\n![](images/20/2096f3451d19fc44bccca9ebe3033e9af29878aa928ad05b102e391be3f76105.png)\n\n \n\n- The fifths are grouped, and each group contains ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac{2}{5}}}.$\n\n![](images/96/96bc693ffe7fa65c90758e0d86a7176b19f7a279cfff12607926e0350448fe42.png)\n\n \n\n- There are $\\color{purple} 10$ groups in total.\n\n![](images/1e/1ebdc6bce6be33bbdd5fd7ff79a562c22e68103cebc8f0ded5ddac6833633989.png)\n\n \n\nSo, if we divide $\\color{blue}4$ wholes into groups of ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac{2}{5}}},$ we get ${\\color{purple}{10}}$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents the following division problem:\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{4}} \\div \\boxed{ { {\\color{red}{\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}}}}} = {\\color{purple}{10}}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{red}\\dfrac{2}{5}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186142659 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \"${\\color{blue}8}$ *times smaller* than the expression\" means that we should *divide* the given expression by $8$.\n\nFirst, we add some brackets:\n\n$$\n\\left[5 + (2\\times 4)\\right]\n$$\n\nNext, we divide the expression by $\\color{blue}8$:\n\n$$\n\\left[5 + (2\\times 4)\\right]\\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}8}\n$$" "First, we note that \"${\\color{blue}8}$ *times smaller* than the expression\" means that we should *divide* the given expression by $8$.\n\nFirst, we add some brackets:\n\n$$\n\\left[5 + (2\\times 4)\\right]\n$$\n\nNext, we divide the expression by $\\color{blue}8$:\n\n$$\n\\left[5 + (2\\times 4)\\right]\\div {\\color{blue}8}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142664 :question/problem "What is $62 \\operatorname{div}  9$?" "What is $62 \\div 9$?"] [:db/cas 17592186142664 :question/worked-solution "The whole number part of $62\\operatorname{div}  9$ must be ${\\color{blue}6}$, because\n\n- $9$ can go into $62$ a total of ${\\color{blue}6}$ times ($9 \\times {\\color{blue}6} = 54$), but\n- $9$ cannot go into $62$ a total of $7$ times ($9 \\times 7 = 63$).\n\nThe remainder is\n\n$$\n62 - 54 = {\\color{red}8}\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n62\\operatorname{div}  9 = {\\color{blue}{6}} \\,\\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{8}}\n$$\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we divide it by the original divisor ($9$). Therefore,\n\n$$\n62\\operatorname{div}  9 = {\\color{blue}6}\\,\\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{9}\\,\n$$" "The whole number part of $62\\div 9$ must be ${\\color{blue}6}$, because\n\n- $9$ can go into $62$ a total of ${\\color{blue}6}$ times ($9 \\times {\\color{blue}6} = 54$), but\n- $9$ cannot go into $62$ a total of $7$ times ($9 \\times 7 = 63$).\n\nThe remainder is\n\n$$\n62 - 54 = {\\color{red}8}\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n62\\div 9 = {\\color{blue}{6}} \\,\\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{8}}\n$$\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we divide it by the original divisor ($9$). Therefore,\n\n$$\n62\\div 9 = {\\color{blue}6}\\,\\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{9}\\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142665 :question/worked-solution "We need to work out $61\\operatorname{div}  4$. We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$\\color{blue}5$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$2$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$\\color{red}1$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we conclude that\n\n$$\n61\\operatorname{div}  4 = {\\color{blue}{15}} \\,\\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we divide it by the original divisor ($4$). So, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n61 \\operatorname{div}  4 = {\\color{blue}15} \\, \\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fisherman put\n\n$$\n15 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\npounds of fish in each box." "We need to work out $61\\div 4$. We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$\\color{blue}5$$4$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$2$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$\\color{red}1$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we conclude that\n\n$$\n61\\div 4 = {\\color{blue}{15}} \\,\\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we divide it by the original divisor ($4$). So, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n61 \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}15} \\, \\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fisherman put\n\n$$\n15 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\npounds of fish in each box."] [:db/cas 17592186142666 :question/problem "What is $74 \\operatorname{div}  6$?" "What is $74 \\div 6$?"] [:db/cas 17592186142666 :question/worked-solution "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$\\color{blue}2$$6$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\color{red}2$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we conclude that\n\n$$\n74\\operatorname{div}  6 = {\\color{blue}{12}} \\,\\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we divide it by the original divisor ($6$). So, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n74\\operatorname{div}  6 = {\\color{blue}{12}} \\, \\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fractional part by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{6} = \\dfrac{2 \\operatorname{div}  2}{6 \\operatorname{div}  2} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the simplified mixed number is\n\n$$\n74\\operatorname{div}  6 = 12 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\n$$" "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$\\color{blue}2$$6$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\color{red}2$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we conclude that\n\n$$\n74\\div 6 = {\\color{blue}{12}} \\,\\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we divide it by the original divisor ($6$). So, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n74\\div 6 = {\\color{blue}{12}} \\, \\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fractional part by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{6} = \\dfrac{2 \\div 2}{6 \\div 2} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the simplified mixed number is\n\n$$\n74\\div 6 = 12 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142667 :question/problem "What is $942 \\operatorname{div}  9\\,$?" "What is $942 \\div 9\\,$?"] [:db/cas 17592186142667 :question/worked-solution "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$\\color{blue}0$$\\color{blue}4$$9$$\\!\\!\\phantom{\\mid}\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$0$$4$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$\\color{red}6$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we conclude that\n\n$$\n942 \\operatorname{div}  9 = {\\color{blue}{104}} \\,\\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{6}}\n$$\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we divide it by the original divisor ($9$). So, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n942 \\operatorname{div}  9 = {\\color{blue}{104}} \\, \\dfrac{\\color{red}6}{9}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fractional part by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{9} = \\dfrac{6 \\operatorname{div}  3}{9 \\operatorname{div}  3} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the simplified mixed number is\n\n$$\n942 \\operatorname{div}  9 = 104 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\n$$" "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$\\color{blue}0$$\\color{blue}4$$9$$\\!\\!\\phantom{\\mid}\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$0$$4$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$\\color{red}6$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we conclude that\n\n$$\n942 \\div 9 = {\\color{blue}{104}} \\,\\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{6}}\n$$\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we divide it by the original divisor ($9$). So, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n942 \\div 9 = {\\color{blue}{104}} \\, \\dfrac{\\color{red}6}{9}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fractional part by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{9} = \\dfrac{6 \\div 3}{9 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the simplified mixed number is\n\n$$\n942 \\div 9 = 104 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142668 :question/worked-solution "To find out how many trees are in each row, we divide the number of trees ($280$) by the number of rows ($14$). So, we need to work out $280 \\operatorname{div}  14$.\n\nIf\n\n$$\n280 \\operatorname{div}  14 = \\square\n$$\n\nthen the corresponding multiplication equation is:\n\n$$\n\\square\\times 14 = 280\n$$\n\nWe can represent this multiplication problem using an area model:\n\n![](images/3d/3df101307774a2b4d4d87597a43a4f6e9c1ba00ca532b588a48e4afba4931cd3.png)\n\nFirst, let's list the possible answers, from smallest to largest:\n\n$$\n18,\\quad 19,\\quad 20\n$$\n\nWe'll try each of the given numbers until we find one that satisfies the multiplication equation.\n\n- $18 \\times 14 = 252 \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- $19 \\times 14 = 252 + 14 = 266 \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- $20 \\times 14 = 266 + 14 = 280 \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSo we have:\n\n![](images/f1/f1b9dcbab59bec3483df421220e919a92af2135f50a69c29ebfe25360aedb2d6.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n280 \\operatorname{div}  14 = 20\n$$\n\nSo there are $20$ trees in each row." "To find out how many trees are in each row, we divide the number of trees ($280$) by the number of rows ($14$). So, we need to work out $280 \\div 14$.\n\nIf\n\n$$\n280 \\div 14 = \\square\n$$\n\nthen the corresponding multiplication equation is:\n\n$$\n\\square\\times 14 = 280\n$$\n\nWe can represent this multiplication problem using an area model:\n\n![](images/3d/3df101307774a2b4d4d87597a43a4f6e9c1ba00ca532b588a48e4afba4931cd3.png)\n\nFirst, let's list the possible answers, from smallest to largest:\n\n$$\n18,\\quad 19,\\quad 20\n$$\n\nWe'll try each of the given numbers until we find one that satisfies the multiplication equation.\n\n- $18 \\times 14 = 252 \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- $19 \\times 14 = 252 + 14 = 266 \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- $20 \\times 14 = 266 + 14 = 280 \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSo we have:\n\n![](images/f1/f1b9dcbab59bec3483df421220e919a92af2135f50a69c29ebfe25360aedb2d6.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n280 \\div 14 = 20\n$$\n\nSo there are $20$ trees in each row."] [:db/cas 17592186142669 :question/problem "Given the following options, what is the result of calculating $1,335 \\operatorname{div}  15$ using the relationship between multiplication and division?\n\n1. $89$\n2. $88$\n3. $90$" "Given the following options, what is the result of calculating $1,335 \\div 15$ using the relationship between multiplication and division?\n\n1. $89$\n2. $88$\n3. $90$"] [:db/cas 17592186142669 :question/worked-solution "If\n\n$$\n1,335 \\operatorname{div}  15 = \\square\n$$\n\nthen the corresponding multiplication equation is:\n\n$$\n\\square \\times 15 = 1,335\n$$\n\nWe can represent this multiplication problem using an area model:\n\n![](images/23/23472b4b3b9e08379b782e32561a46b46132aa4ee3f7c13df5711170d04c714a.png)\n\nFirst, let's list the possible answers, from smallest to largest:\n\n$$\n88,\\quad 89,\\quad 90\n$$\n\nWe'll try each of the given numbers until we find one that satisfies the multiplication equation.\n\n- $88 \\times 15 = 1,320 \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- $89 \\times 15 = 1,320 + 15 = 1,335 \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSo we have:\n\n![](images/71/712ae12dcfb728e8a88dc3b4912bda3f01998feb44814d0d9c5e45934f686af1.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1,335 \\operatorname{div}  15 = 89\n$$" "If\n\n$$\n1,335 \\div 15 = \\square\n$$\n\nthen the corresponding multiplication equation is:\n\n$$\n\\square \\times 15 = 1,335\n$$\n\nWe can represent this multiplication problem using an area model:\n\n![](images/23/23472b4b3b9e08379b782e32561a46b46132aa4ee3f7c13df5711170d04c714a.png)\n\nFirst, let's list the possible answers, from smallest to largest:\n\n$$\n88,\\quad 89,\\quad 90\n$$\n\nWe'll try each of the given numbers until we find one that satisfies the multiplication equation.\n\n- $88 \\times 15 = 1,320 \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- $89 \\times 15 = 1,320 + 15 = 1,335 \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSo we have:\n\n![](images/71/712ae12dcfb728e8a88dc3b4912bda3f01998feb44814d0d9c5e45934f686af1.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1,335 \\div 15 = 89\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142670 :question/problem "Given the following options, what is the result of calculating $96 \\operatorname{div}  16$ using the relationship between multiplication and division?\n\n1. $5$\n2. $6$\n3. $7$" "Given the following options, what is the result of calculating $96 \\div 16$ using the relationship between multiplication and division?\n\n1. $5$\n2. $6$\n3. $7$"] [:db/cas 17592186142670 :question/worked-solution "To find which choice is correct, we start by writing the corresponding multiplication equation. If\n\n$$\n96 \\operatorname{div}  16 = \\square\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\square \\times 16 = 96\n$$\n\nWe can represent this multiplication problem using an area model:\n\n![](images/ef/ef4c4260eb1f3cf09c6594ddadf15d6badb9956b82486128e5f602e26028aa7b.png)\n\nWe'll try each of the given numbers ($5,6,7$) until we find one that satisfies the multiplication equation.\n\n- $5 \\times 16 = 80 \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- $6 \\times 16 = 80 + 16 = 96 \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSo we have:\n\n![](images/83/830c87378171135fb003d6359178d33b8229600c3caba0bc29f1732a745b7075.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n96 \\operatorname{div}  16 = 6\n$$" "To find which choice is correct, we start by writing the corresponding multiplication equation. If\n\n$$\n96 \\div 16 = \\square\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\square \\times 16 = 96\n$$\n\nWe can represent this multiplication problem using an area model:\n\n![](images/ef/ef4c4260eb1f3cf09c6594ddadf15d6badb9956b82486128e5f602e26028aa7b.png)\n\nWe'll try each of the given numbers ($5,6,7$) until we find one that satisfies the multiplication equation.\n\n- $5 \\times 16 = 80 \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- $6 \\times 16 = 80 + 16 = 96 \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSo we have:\n\n![](images/83/830c87378171135fb003d6359178d33b8229600c3caba0bc29f1732a745b7075.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n96 \\div 16 = 6\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142675 :question/worked-solution "To find the length of wood she gave to Louis and Diana, we need to calculate the sum\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{3}{4} + 2\\,\\dfrac{7}{10}\n$$\n\nTo add two mixed numbers, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions. Adding the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n1+2 = {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, we add the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} + \\dfrac{7}{10}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator. In this question, we can make a common denominator of $20$.\n\n- To put $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ over a denominator of $20$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $5$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{3 \\times 5}{4 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{15}{20}\n$$\n- To put $\\dfrac{7}{10}$ over a denominator of $20$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{7}{10} = \\dfrac{7 \\times 2}{10 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{14}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same and we add the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{20} + \\frac{14}{20} = \\frac{29}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can convert the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{29}{20}\n$$\n\nto a mixed number:\n\n$$\n29 \\operatorname{div}  20 = 1\\,\\textrm{R}\\,9 = {\\color{blue}{1\\,\\dfrac 9{20}}}\n$$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{3}{4} + 2\\,\\dfrac{7}{10} = {\\color{red}{3}} + {\\color{blue}{1\\,\\dfrac 9{20}}} = 4\\,\\dfrac{9}{20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in total, Nadia cut\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac{9}{20}\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$\n\nof wood." "To find the length of wood she gave to Louis and Diana, we need to calculate the sum\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{3}{4} + 2\\,\\dfrac{7}{10}\n$$\n\nTo add two mixed numbers, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions. Adding the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n1+2 = {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, we add the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} + \\dfrac{7}{10}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator. In this question, we can make a common denominator of $20$.\n\n- To put $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ over a denominator of $20$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $5$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{3 \\times 5}{4 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{15}{20}\n$$\n- To put $\\dfrac{7}{10}$ over a denominator of $20$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{7}{10} = \\dfrac{7 \\times 2}{10 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{14}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same and we add the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{20} + \\frac{14}{20} = \\frac{29}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can convert the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{29}{20}\n$$\n\nto a mixed number:\n\n$$\n29 \\div 20 = 1\\,\\textrm{R}\\,9 = {\\color{blue}{1\\,\\dfrac 9{20}}}\n$$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{3}{4} + 2\\,\\dfrac{7}{10} = {\\color{red}{3}} + {\\color{blue}{1\\,\\dfrac 9{20}}} = 4\\,\\dfrac{9}{20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in total, Nadia cut\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac{9}{20}\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$\n\nof wood."] [:db/cas 17592186142682 :question/worked-solution "The total weight of the bags (in ounces) is\n\n$$\n2 + 2 + 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} + 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} + 2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{4} \\,\n$$\n\nTo add mixed numbers, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions.\n\n- Adding the whole numbers, we get\n$2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 10$.\n- Adding the fractions, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{4} +\\dfrac{1}{4} + \\dfrac{2}{4} = \\dfrac{4}{4} \\,\n$$.\nSimplifying this fraction, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{4}{4} = 4 \\operatorname{div}  4 = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total weight of the bags (in ounces) is\n\n$$\n2 + 2 + 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} + 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} + 2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{4} = 10 + 1 = 11 \\,\n$$\n\nTo redistribute this total weight equally among each bag of fries, we divide the total weight by the number of bags:\n\n$$\n11 \\operatorname{div}  5 = 2 \\,\\textrm{R} \\, 1 = 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, each bag would weigh\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nounces." "The total weight of the bags (in ounces) is\n\n$$\n2 + 2 + 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} + 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} + 2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{4} \\,\n$$\n\nTo add mixed numbers, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions.\n\n- Adding the whole numbers, we get\n$2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 10$.\n- Adding the fractions, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{4} +\\dfrac{1}{4} + \\dfrac{2}{4} = \\dfrac{4}{4} \\,\n$$.\nSimplifying this fraction, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{4}{4} = 4 \\div 4 = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total weight of the bags (in ounces) is\n\n$$\n2 + 2 + 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} + 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} + 2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{4} = 10 + 1 = 11 \\,\n$$\n\nTo redistribute this total weight equally among each bag of fries, we divide the total weight by the number of bags:\n\n$$\n11 \\div 5 = 2 \\,\\textrm{R} \\, 1 = 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, each bag would weigh\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nounces."] [:db/cas 17592186142683 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we note the following:\n\n- There were $2$ days on which $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ centimeters of rain fell.\n- There were $2$ days on which $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ centimeters of rain fell.\n\nSo the total amount of rain that fell is given by\n\n$$\n2 \\times \\dfrac{2}{3} + 2 \\times \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{4}{3} + \\dfrac{6}{4} \\,\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{4}\n$$\n\nby dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6 \\operatorname{div}  2}{4 \\operatorname{div}  2} = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to add the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3} + \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nHere, we can make a common denominator of $6$.\n\n- To put $\\dfrac{4}{3}$ over a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{4}{3} = \\dfrac{4 \\times 2}{3 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{8}{6}\n$$\n- To put $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ over a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{3}{2} = \\dfrac{3 \\times 3}{2 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{9}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we add the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3} + \\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{8}{6} + \\frac{9}{6} = \\frac{17}{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{17}{6}\n$$\n\nto a mixed number.\n\n$$\n17 \\operatorname{div}  6 = 2 \\,\\textrm{R} \\,5 = 2 \\, \\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\ncentimeters of rain fell during this period." "From the graph, we note the following:\n\n- There were $2$ days on which $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ centimeters of rain fell.\n- There were $2$ days on which $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ centimeters of rain fell.\n\nSo the total amount of rain that fell is given by\n\n$$\n2 \\times \\dfrac{2}{3} + 2 \\times \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{4}{3} + \\dfrac{6}{4} \\,\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{4}\n$$\n\nby dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6 \\div 2}{4 \\div 2} = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to add the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3} + \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nHere, we can make a common denominator of $6$.\n\n- To put $\\dfrac{4}{3}$ over a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{4}{3} = \\dfrac{4 \\times 2}{3 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{8}{6}\n$$\n- To put $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ over a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{3}{2} = \\dfrac{3 \\times 3}{2 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{9}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we add the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3} + \\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{8}{6} + \\frac{9}{6} = \\frac{17}{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{17}{6}\n$$\n\nto a mixed number.\n\n$$\n17 \\div 6 = 2 \\,\\textrm{R} \\,5 = 2 \\, \\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\ncentimeters of rain fell during this period."] [:db/cas 17592186142696 :question/worked-solution "Note that we can divide each term in pattern B by $3$ to get the corresponding term in pattern A:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n36 \\operatorname{div}  3 &= 12 \\\\\n30 \\operatorname{div}  3 &= 10 \\\\\n24 \\operatorname{div}  3 &= 8 \\\\\n18 \\operatorname{div}  3 &= 6 \\\\\n12 \\operatorname{div}  3 &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is:\n\n- Each term in pattern A is **$3$ times smaller** than the corresponding term in pattern B." "Note that we can divide each term in pattern B by $3$ to get the corresponding term in pattern A:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n36 \\div 3 &= 12 \\\\\n30 \\div 3 &= 10 \\\\\n24 \\div 3 &= 8 \\\\\n18 \\div 3 &= 6 \\\\\n12 \\div 3 &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is:\n\n- Each term in pattern A is **$3$ times smaller** than the corresponding term in pattern B."] [:db/cas 17592186142698 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the missing numbers in the two patterns.\n\n- The rule of pattern A is \"add $3$\", and the first number is $0$. So, we have the following pattern:![](images/fb/fbaa7ceb815c926a39dadbded56310fa4dae533391b621826082b838055d513d.png)\n- The rule of pattern B is \"add $18$\", and the first number is $0$. So, we have the following pattern:![](images/2b/2b41bc6d8aae1a4