{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}\\times 6 \\times 2.5$\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n6 \\times 2.5 = 15\n$$\n\n, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n75 &= \\,\\phantom{0}\\, \\cdot \\underbrace{6 \\cdot 2.5}_{15} \\\\\n75 &= \\,\\phantom{0}\\, \\cdot 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the relationship between multiplication and division, we obtain that the length of the package must be\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}} = 75 \\operatorname{div}  15 = 5 \\, \\text{in}.\n$$" "First, recall that the volume of a rectangular package can be calculated as\n\n$$\nV = \\text{length} \\times \\text{width} \\times \\text{height}\n$$\n\nand we are told that this volume is equal to $75 \\, \\text{in}^3$.\n\n![](images/32/32798500f85c9bc6dfc97b588d787ec9befd8a4f5b52aa0bc482588f71ca989f.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n$75 = \\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}\\times 6 \\times 2.5$\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n6 \\times 2.5 = 15\n$$\n\n, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n75 &= \\,\\phantom{0}\\, \\cdot \\underbrace{6 \\cdot 2.5}_{15} \\\\\n75 &= \\,\\phantom{0}\\, \\cdot 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the relationship between multiplication and division, we obtain that the length of the package must be\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}} = 75 \\div 15 = 5 \\, \\text{in}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142717 :question/worked-solution "We have $\\dfrac{5}{7}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{5}{21}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/84/84cba43457adc42afc433238410fefc9fe98b042d326c10ea3fce6221e7e620c.png)\n\nTherefore, the division problem shown is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{7} \\operatorname{div}  3 = \\dfrac{5}{21}.\n$$" "We have $\\dfrac{5}{7}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{5}{21}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/84/84cba43457adc42afc433238410fefc9fe98b042d326c10ea3fce6221e7e620c.png)\n\nTherefore, the division problem shown is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{7} \\div 3 = \\dfrac{5}{21}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142719 :question/worked-solution "We have $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{3}{20}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/eb/eb114471d69ebd728a176b521ab410ab2b16d2bccc039e003faacd9c5d445e96.png)\n\nThe division operation splits each vertical line into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts. So the shaded parts on the left are $\\color{blue}5$ times larger than the shaded parts on the right. \n\nTherefore, the division problem shown is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 4 \\operatorname{div}  {\\color{blue}{5}} = \\dfrac{3}{20}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{blue}5.$" "We have $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{3}{20}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/eb/eb114471d69ebd728a176b521ab410ab2b16d2bccc039e003faacd9c5d445e96.png)\n\nThe division operation splits each vertical line into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts. So the shaded parts on the left are $\\color{blue}5$ times larger than the shaded parts on the right. \n\nTherefore, the division problem shown is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 4 \\div {\\color{blue}{5}} = \\dfrac{3}{20}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{blue}5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186142720 :question/problem "Use the model below to calculate $\\dfrac{5}{6} \\operatorname{div}  4.$\n\n![](images/d1/d1c1c3842b04174137bfe8a938c70493493b2c9a0e74bee619d965446c9326a6.png)" "Use the model below to calculate $\\dfrac{5}{6} \\div 4.$\n\n![](images/d1/d1c1c3842b04174137bfe8a938c70493493b2c9a0e74bee619d965446c9326a6.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186142720 :question/worked-solution "Since we need to divide by $\\color{blue}4$, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}4$ equal parts horizontally.\n\n![](images/24/24bffed596b77ba53d57e04ebb6baede437d40f825c03a794ae7689f11c3c250.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac{5}{24}$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6} \\operatorname{div}  4 = \\dfrac{5}{24} \\, .\n$$" "Since we need to divide by $\\color{blue}4$, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}4$ equal parts horizontally.\n\n![](images/24/24bffed596b77ba53d57e04ebb6baede437d40f825c03a794ae7689f11c3c250.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac{5}{24}$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6} \\div 4 = \\dfrac{5}{24} \\, .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142721 :question/problem "What is $9.888 \\operatorname{div}  24$?" "What is $9.888 \\div 24$?"] [:db/cas 17592186142721 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the problem using long division notation. We keep the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $24$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the dot in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\: \\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$24$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $0$$\\!\\!. 4$$1$$2$$24$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\,. \\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$6$$2$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$4$$4$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n9.888 \\operatorname{div}  24 = 0.412\\,\n$$" "First, we write down the problem using long division notation. We keep the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $24$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the dot in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\: \\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$24$$\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $0$$\\!\\!. 4$$1$$2$$24$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\,. \\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$6$$2$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$4$$4$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n9.888 \\div 24 = 0.412\\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186142722 :question/worked-solution "To determine the number of liters of water the pump would deliver in $1$ hour, we need to calculate $367.5\\operatorname{div}  3.5$.\n\nWe can write this division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{367.5}{3.5}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{367.5 \\times 10}{3.5 \\times 10} = \\dfrac{3,675}{35}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $3,675 \\operatorname{div}  35$.\n\nNext, we proceed to use long division:\n\n| $1$$0$$5$$35\\,$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$5$$1$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$0$$1$$7$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$7$$5$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n367.5\\operatorname{div}  3.5 = 105\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the pump would deliver $105$ liters of water every hour." "To determine the number of liters of water the pump would deliver in $1$ hour, we need to calculate $367.5\\div 3.5$.\n\nWe can write this division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{367.5}{3.5}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{367.5 \\times 10}{3.5 \\times 10} = \\dfrac{3,675}{35}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $3,675 \\div 35$.\n\nNext, we proceed to use long division:\n\n| $1$$0$$5$$35\\,$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$5$$1$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$0$$1$$7$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$7$$5$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n367.5\\div 3.5 = 105\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the pump would deliver $105$ liters of water every hour."] [:db/cas 17592186142723 :question/problem "What is the value of $73.71 \\operator